**What changed:** Inside Angular core’s HMR implementation (`packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts`), the function `clearRendererCache` previously attempted to clear a component’s cached renderer from an internal map named `rendererByCompId` by invoking `.remove(def.id)` on it after casting the `RendererFactory` to `any`. This failed because `rendererByCompId` is a `Map`, which exposes `.delete` rather than `.remove`, so the entry was never removed and template HMR did not correctly update the component. The fix replaces the cast with `(factory as {rendererByCompId?: Map<string, unknown>})` and calls `.delete(def.id)` instead. The PR/commit text explicitly notes: “Component template HMR is currently considered experimental.” (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/502ee0e7221a9e7cfa0fa3bd92298d3b650a7713, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58724)

**Affects:** Applications that use experimental component template HMR (template changes are hot-swapped via the built-in HMR support) and that depend on the renderer factory internal cache being cleared so a component can update.

**Action:** none (this is a bug fix in Angular core; no application code, configuration, or dependency change is required. If applications rely on experimental template HMR, re-verify that component templates correctly hot-update after upgrade)

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
// Cast to `any` to read a private field.
// NOTE: This must be kept synchronized with the renderer factory implementation in platform-browser.
(factory as any).rendererByCompId?.remove(def.id);

// After
// Cast to read a private field.
// NOTE: This must be kept synchronized with the renderer factory implementation in platform-browser.
(factory as {rendererByCompId?: Map<string, unknown>}).rendererByCompId?.delete(def.id);
```

**What changed:** The `route-lazy-loading` schematic (`@angular/core:route-lazy-loading`) used by `ng generate @angular/core:route-lazy-loading` previously located a component's metadata by inspecting only the first decorator modifier (`node.modifiers?.find((m) => m.kind === ts.SyntaxKind.Decorator)`) inside `isStandaloneComponent`, so a component class decorated with an extra decorator ahead of `@Component` (for example `@OtherDecorator()` placed before `@Component({standalone: true})`) caused a crash (`Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'kind')`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/99715104a1a787c3899dfbfac6b44f28c7d24356, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58793). The fixed implementation obtains decorators via `ReflectionHost.getDecoratorsOfDeclaration(node)` (using `TypeScriptReflectionHost`) and selects the Angular metadata with `findAngularDecorator(decorators, 'Component', false)` before reading `standalone` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/route-lazy-loading/util.ts).

**Affects:** Applications that run `ng generate @angular/core:route-lazy-loading` and contain component classes referenced in route definitions that carry an additional decorator before the `@Component` decorator; such components could previously abort the migration instead of being converted.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// util.ts - 19.0.0
export function isStandaloneComponent(node: ts.ClassDeclaration): boolean {
  const decorator = node.modifiers?.find((m) => m.kind === ts.SyntaxKind.Decorator);
  if (!decorator) {
    return false;
  }
  if (ts.isCallExpression(decorator.expression)) {
    const arg = decorator.expression.arguments[0];
    if (ts.isObjectLiteralExpression(arg)) {
      const property = findLiteralProperty(arg, 'standalone') as ts.PropertyAssignment;
      if (property) {
        return property.initializer.getText() === 'true';
      } else {
        return true; // standalone is true by default in v19
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}

// util.ts - 20.0.0
export function isStandaloneComponent(
  node: ts.ClassDeclaration,
  reflector: ReflectionHost,
): boolean {
  const decorators = reflector.getDecoratorsOfDeclaration(node);
  if (decorators === null) {
    return false;
  }
  const decorator = findAngularDecorator(decorators, 'Component', false);
  if (decorator === undefined || decorator.args === null || decorator.args.length !== 1) {
    return false;
  }
  const arg = decorator.args[0];
  if (ts.isObjectLiteralExpression(arg)) {
    const property = findLiteralProperty(arg, 'standalone') as ts.PropertyAssignment;
    if (property) {
      return property.initializer.getText() === 'true';
    } else {
      return true; // standalone is true by default in v19
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

**What changed:** This is an internal bug fix in `ApplicationRef` where `_tick` is now always executed within a `TracingSnapshot`. Previously, `_tick` was called directly (e.g., from `zoneless_scheduling_impl.ts`) in some cases, bypassing the snapshot, which meant snapshots requested but not resumed were leaked. Now, `_tick` checks if `this.tracingSnapshot` is not null and runs itself within that snapshot using `snapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick)` if it exists. The direct call `this.tracingSnapshot?.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick) ?? this._tick();` in the `tick()` method was replaced by a direct call `this._tick();` because `_tick` itself handles the snapshot execution. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58881, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/118803035f366acdffc577ec857b888f764bb338)

**Affects:** All applications upgrading to Angular 20, specifically regarding internal tracing snapshot execution.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// application_ref.ts (Before)
    // Run `_tick()` in the context of the most recent snapshot, if one exists.
    this.tracingSnapshot?.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick) ?? this._tick();
  }

  /** @internal */
  _tick = (): void => {
    this.tracingSnapshot = null;

// application_ref.ts (After)
    this._tick();
  }

  /** @internal */
  _tick = (): void => {
    if (this.tracingSnapshot !== null) {
      const snapshot = this.tracingSnapshot;
      this.tracingSnapshot = null;

      // Ensure we always run `_tick()` in the context of the most recent snapshot,
      // if one exists. Snapshots may be reference counted by the implementation so
      // we want to ensure that if we request a snapshot that we use it.
      snapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick);
      return;
    }
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core`'s `resource()` API, the internal `setErrorState` in `BaseWritableResource` (packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts) previously did `this.status.set(ResourceStatus.Error)` **before** `this.value.set(undefined)`. Because overriding `value.set` routes through `BaseWritableResource.set()`, which sets `this.status.set(ResourceStatus.Local)` and clears `error`, a resource that already held a valid value never ended up in the error state — `status()` stayed `Local`/stale and `error()` was cleared. The fix reorders the calls, setting the value first and then `this.status.set(ResourceStatus.Error)` so the status wins (comment: "The previous line will set the status to `Local`, so we need to update it."). Now after a failed reload: `status()` is `ResourceStatus.Error`, `hasValue()` is `false`, `value()` is `undefined`, and `error()` holds the thrown error (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/08b9452f012b2ef660f767c2f0a4bf86bb15bb61.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58855).

**Affects:** Applications using `resource()` (or `rxResource`/`httpResource` built on it) whose loader throws/rejects *after* a previous successful load (reload, request-signal change, or `reload()`), and code/tests that branch on `status()`, `isLoading()`, `hasValue()`, `value()` or `error()` in that case.

**Action:** none — behavior fix; re-verify UI and tests that handled loader errors after a prior success (they may have relied on the stale non-error status) and update expectations to `ResourceStatus.Error` / `hasValue() === false`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts)
protected setErrorState(err: unknown): void {
  this.status.set(ResourceStatus.Error);
  this.value.set(undefined);
  this.error.set(err);
}

// after
protected setErrorState(err: unknown): void {
  this.value.set(undefined);
  // The previous line will set the status to `Local`, so we need to update it.
  this.status.set(ResourceStatus.Error);
  this.error.set(err);
}
```

**What changed:** Angular's internal `getComponentId` (in `packages/core/src/render3/definition.ts`) hashes component-def fields (`selectors`, `ngContentSelectors`, `hostVars`, `hostAttrs`, `consts`, `vars`, `decls`, `encapsulation`, prototype own property names, `!!contentQueries`, `!!viewQuery`) to produce the id used by hydration to match server- and client-rendered components (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/84f45ea3ffe02003350c6c19fdafdc6f4d521ccb). For templates containing `i18n`, the compiler emits `consts` as a **function**, so its serialized body differed between client and server bundles when minification/mangling differed — Angular CLI used `mangling: true` for the browser build but `mangling: false` for the server build under `optimization = true` — producing mismatched ids and hydration being skipped (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58713). Now the code computes `const componentDefConsts = typeof componentDef.consts === 'function' ? '' : componentDef.consts;` and hashes that instead, and in `ngDevMode` an `assertNotEqual(typeof item, 'function', 'Internal error: attempting to use a function in component id computation logic.')` guard runs over every hashed item (patch file of the same commit). The PR is labeled `action: merge, area: i18n, area: core, core: hydration, target: patch` and is not a breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58813); it is not listed in the v20 Breaking Changes section (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** SSR apps with hydration plus i18n in component templates (`provideClientHydration(withI18nSupport())`), where the server and browser builds are produced with different minification/mangling configuration.

**Action:** none — no code, config or dependency change. Required: re-verify hydration of i18n components rendered in production builds (e.g. inside `router-outlet`); ids are now computed without `consts`, so components previously excluded due to mismatched ids may now hydrate (or mismatch differently). Angular still skips hydration for i18n components by default unless `withI18nSupport` is added (https://angular.dev/guide/hydration).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
componentDef.consts,
...
].join('|');
for (const char of hashSelectors) {

// after
const componentDefConsts = typeof componentDef.consts === 'function' ? '' : componentDef.consts;
...
componentDefConsts,
...
];
for (const char of hashSelectors.join('|')) {
```

**What changed:** An internal bug fix in Angular core's incremental hydration mechanism. Previously, when hydrating a tree of `@defer` blocks, the cleanup function could fire more than once per tree, or fire before hydration was fully complete. This caused a race condition where interactive elements within control flow blocks inside deferred blocks would fail on the first click (the first click would trigger hydration and remove the `ngb` attribute, while the navigation would not proceed until the second click). The fix prevents cleanup from firing more than once per tree and ensures it happens after hydration has finished (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58722, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58690).

**Affects:** Applications using incremental hydration where defer blocks contain nested blocks and are combined with control flow (`@if`, `@for`, etc.), specifically when they contain event listeners that depend on the `hydrate on interaction` or similar triggers.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- This structure could previously fail on first click for navigation if nested in @if -->
@defer(on interaction; hydrate on interaction) {
  @if (isMenuOpen) {
    <a href="/about">About Us</a>
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/forms`, the return type of `FormBuilder.group` was changed from the inline mapped type `FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<T[K], null>}>` to a new exported type alias `ɵNullableFormGroup<T>`, and `NonNullableFormBuilder.group` from `FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<T[K], never>}>` to `ɵNonNullableFormGroup<T>` (aliases defined in `packages/forms/src/form_builder.ts`, exported for the workaround to work). This works around a TypeScript 5.6→5.7 regression in `.d.ts` generation (microsoft/TypeScript#60506), where declaration emit for `FormBuilder.group` produced wrong types such as `FormGroup<{ data: string[] | null }>` instead of `FormGroup<{ data: FormControl<string[] | null> }>` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4dfe5b6cefd7901a466b37b660f8b3a051a06cb3, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58731, https://github.com/Microsoft/TypeScript/issues/60506). Runtime behaviour is unchanged. Note Angular v20 drops support for TypeScript < 5.8 (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications using `FormBuilder.group` / `NonNullableFormBuilder.group` where code explicitly annotates variables or members with the concrete `FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<...>}>` shape, or that otherwise consumed the emitted declaration types; type-only change, fixing previously incorrect inferred types under TS 5.7+.

**Action:** none — the change is type-level and source-compatible (both aliases resolve to the same `FormGroup` type). Applications should re-verify that form-group types inferred from `.group()` calls still compile after the upgrade (which also requires TypeScript >= 5.8 < 5.9).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// old (v19)
group<T extends {}>(controls: T, options?: AbstractControlOptions | null):
    FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<T[K], null>}>;
// new (v20)
export type ɵNullableFormGroup<T> = FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<T[K], null>}>;
export type ɵNonNullableFormGroup<T> = FormGroup<{[K in keyof T]: ɵElement<T[K], never>}>;
group<T extends {}>(controls: T, options?: AbstractControlOptions | null): ɵNullableFormGroup<T>;
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20 the `@angular/language-service` code fixes now work in more cases (PR #58719). Previously `getCodeActions` in `fixUnusedStandaloneImportsMeta` (file `packages/language-service/src/codefixes/fix_unused_standalone_imports.ts`) was a stub (`getCodeActions: () => []`), so the "Remove all unused imports" / "Remove unused import X" quick fix for the `UNUSED_STANDALONE_IMPORTS` diagnostic never appeared for individual diagnostics — only the fix-all version worked (commit a983865bff). Additionally, `CodeFixes.getCodeFixesAtPosition` and `CodeActionContext.templateInfo` in `packages/language-service/src/codefixes/` were changed from `TemplateInfo` to `TemplateInfo | null`, and `LanguageService.getCodeFixesAtPosition` no longer returns `[]` early when `getTemplateInfoAtPosition` returns `undefined`; each fixer (`fix_missing_import.ts`, `fix_missing_member.ts`, `fix_invalid_banana_in_box.ts`) now handles the null case itself, so fixes also run for diagnostics reported in `.ts` files (commit e6e7a4e22b).
**Affects:** Developers using the Angular Language Service in an editor (VS Code, WebStorm, etc.) — an editor/IDE behavior improvement; no application code, build, or configuration is affected. Also affects third-party code built against the internal `CodeActionContext`/`CodeFixes` APIs of `@angular/language-service` (the `templateInfo` field is now nullable).
**Action:** none — upgrading applications and editors need no changes; the unused-import quick fixes simply start appearing in the IDE. Editor users of custom language-service integrations should re-verify code-fix behavior against the nullable `templateInfo`.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/language-service/src/codefixes/fix_unused_standalone_imports.ts
// Before (v19):
export const fixUnusedStandaloneImportsMeta: CodeActionMeta = {
  errorCodes: [ngErrorCode(ErrorCode.UNUSED_STANDALONE_IMPORTS)],
  getCodeActions: () => [],
// After (v20):
  getCodeActions: ({start, fileName, compiler}) => {
    // ...returns "Remove unused import <name>" or "Remove all unused imports"
// packages/language-service/src/codefixes/utils.ts
-  templateInfo: TemplateInfo;
+  templateInfo: TemplateInfo | null;
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a983865bffa828a982ef7e56204924d9c2989ead, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e6e7a4e22b0a654808e5eb88a30cd6effa383332, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58719)

**What changed:** In `@angular/language-service`, code fixes previously bailed out entirely when `getTemplateInfoAtPosition` returned no `TemplateInfo` for the diagnostic position, so fixes never ran for diagnostics reported in `.ts` files. The `templateInfo` parameter of `CodeFixes.getCodeFixesAtPosition` and the `CodeActionContext.templateInfo` field were changed from `TemplateInfo` to `TemplateInfo | null`, and `LanguageService` now passes `getTemplateInfoAtPosition(fileName, start, compiler) ?? null`, leaving the early exit to each fix (`fix_invalid_banana_in_box.ts`, `fix_missing_import.ts`, `fix_missing_member.ts` now check `templateInfo === null`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e6e7a4e22b0a654808e5eb88a30cd6effa383332). Related commit in the same PR (#58719): `fixUnusedStandaloneImportsMeta.getCodeActions` in `fix_unused_standalone_imports.ts` was a no-op (`getCodeActions: () => []`); it now returns per-diagnostic code actions such as "Remove unused import X" and "Remove all unused imports" for the `UNUSED_STANDALONE_IMPORTS` error ().

**Affects:** Internal to the language service (VS Code / editor integrations consuming `@angular/language-service`); applications using the Angular editor code fixes for diagnostics in `.ts` files, e.g. unused standalone `imports`. No application code, templates, or build files are affected.

**Action:** none — this is an editor-behavior fix; upgrading applications need no changes. Optionally re-verify that code fixes (e.g. removing unused standalone imports) now appear in the editor.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (fix_unused_standalone_imports.ts)
export const fixUnusedStandaloneImportsMeta: CodeActionMeta = {
  errorCodes: [ngErrorCode(ErrorCode.UNUSED_STANDALONE_IMPORTS)],
  getCodeActions: () => [],

// after
export const fixUnusedStandaloneImportsMeta: CodeActionMeta = {
  errorCodes: [ngErrorCode(ErrorCode.UNUSED_STANDALONE_IMPORTS)],
  getCodeActions: ({start, fileName, compiler}) => { /* ... */ },

// CodeActionContext: templateInfo: TemplateInfo  ->  templateInfo: TemplateInfo | null
```

**What changed:** A bug in the `provide-initializer` migration schematic caused it to generate incorrect code when migrating `APP_INITIALIZER` providers using `useFactory` with dependencies. Previously, it produced code that incorrectly executed the initializer factory at migration time rather than runtime, or resulted in invalid TypeScript syntax (e.g., returning a function instead of a Promise/Observable/void). The fix ensures the migrated code correctly wraps the initializer logic in an arrow function that executes in an injection context, allowing proper usage of `inject()` to resolve dependencies. Specifically, it changes the output from potentially invalid immediate execution to `provideAppInitializer(() => { const initializerFn = ((service: Service) => { return () => service.init(); })(inject(Service)); return initializerFn(); });` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58518, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5ce10264a434ffc8e31cdc68208d2c3c7f8378ed).

**Affects:** Applications that ran the automatic `provide-initializer` migration (e.g., via `ng update`) on code containing `APP_INITIALIZER` providers with `useFactory` and `deps` properties, resulting in broken builds or runtime errors.

**Action:** required: Re-run the `ng update` migration for `@angular/core` to apply the fix, or manually correct any `provideAppInitializer` calls generated by previous migration runs to ensure the initializer function is wrapped correctly to execute at runtime using `inject`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Previously generated (broken):
provideAppInitializer(() => {
  return ((initService: InitService) => {
    return () => initService.init()
  })(inject(InitService));
})

// Now generated (correct):
provideAppInitializer(() => {
  const initializerFn = ((service: Service) => {
    return () => service.init();
  })(inject(Service));
  return initializerFn();
});
```

**What changed:** The `inject()` migration schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`, implemented in `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/analysis.ts` and `migration.ts`) now handles constructor parameters that have TypeScript default initializers, e.g. `constructor(@Optional() private foo: Foo = createFoo()) {}`. Previously this case was "omitted initially, because we don't officially support such cases, but it came up internally" (commit d4f5c85f60, PR #58769). The fix (a) adds the initializer as a nullish fallback in the generated call via `createInjectReplacementCall`: `inject(Foo, { optional: true }) ?? createFoo()`; (b) adds a new `parameterReferencesOtherParameters()` helper so that when an initializer references *other constructor parameters* (e.g. `private foo: Foo = createFoo(bar)`), the property is declared without initializer (`private foo: Foo;`) and assigned inside the constructor (`this.foo = inject(Foo, { optional: true }) ?? createFoo(bar);`), including after a `super()` call when the param is used in `super(...)`; (c) treats parameters used only in initializers as accessed (new `declaration.parameters.forEach` walk in `getConstructorUnusedParameters`); and (d) prevents removing the constructor when statements must be prepended to it (`canRemoveConstructor` now checks `prependToConstructor.length > 0`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d4f5c85f60133550303d59b3f9e3e34f14ca63ce, diff at .../d4f5c85f60133550303d59b3f9e3e34f14ca63ce.diff).

**Affects:** Applications running the `inject()` migration schematic where constructor-injected parameters declare default initializers (`constructor(private foo: Foo = createFoo())`); no effect if the schematic is not run or no such parameters exist.

**Action:** none — this is an internal correctness fix to the migration tooling; no application code, config, or dependency changes are required. After running the schematic, re-verify that constructor parameters with default values were converted to `inject(...) ?? <initializer>` form and compile cleanly.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before migration
class MyDir {
  constructor(@Optional() private foo: Foo = createFoo()) {}
}
// After migration (with the fix)
class MyDir {
  private foo = inject(Foo, { optional: true }) ?? createFoo();
}
```

**What changed:** This is a bug fix inside Angular's own `inject()` migration schematic (`packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/migration.ts`, PR #58804), not an application API change. When the inject migration runs in "internal" mode it hoists constructor-based injections and initializer assignments into class field declarations. Previously the schematic did not mark those hoisted property declarations as removed (`removedMembers.add(decl)` was missing after `tracker.removeNode(decl, true)` in `applyInternalOnlyChanges`), so new initializer lines were inserted at a property that was about to be deleted and were silently dropped, producing an empty class body such as:

```ts
import { Injectable, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router';
@Injectable()
export class MyClass {}
```

After the fix the hoisted output is preserved, e.g. `private readonly route = inject(ActivatedRoute);`, `readonly a = this.route.get();`, `readonly b = this.a.get();` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58804, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a6d2d2dc104608f14c3850b21bc23ba75ca04e4d.patch).

**Affects:** Only projects that run the `ng generate @angular/core:inject` schematic () on code where constructor-assigned fields are hoisted; the fix was flagged "Does not introduce a breaking change" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58804).

**Action:** none — no application code, config, or dependency changes are required for upgrading to 20.0.0. If you run the inject migration, re-verify its output diffs compile, as with any schematic run.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// After (correct hoisted output produced by the fixed migration):
export class MyClass {
  uninitialized!: string;

  private readonly route = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  readonly a = this.route.get();
  readonly b = this.a.get();
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler-cli previously assumed only one namespace import (`@angular/core`) existed in generated HMR code. For cases where multiple namespace imports are required (e.g., importing directives from local modules alongside `@angular/core`), this caused runtime errors like `ReferenceError: i1 is not defined` when Hot Module Replacement (HMR) triggered metadata replacement ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58924), https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9f99196d239479bcba0b42a18a5155ed5a1764ff)). The update changes the internal `R3HmrMetadata` interface to use `namespaceDependencies: R3HmrNamespaceDependency[]` and `localDependencies: string[]` instead of `coreName: string` and `locals: string[]` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9f99196d239479bcba0b42a18a5155ed5a1764ff.patch)). The `ɵɵreplaceMetadata` function in `@angular/core` now accepts an array of namespace objects (`namespaces: unknown[]`) as its third argument instead of a single `environment` object ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9f99196d239479bcba0b42a18a5155ed5a1764ff.patch)). Generated HMR initializer code now passes an array of namespaces (e.g., `[i0, i1]`) to `ɵɵreplaceMetadata`, and the update callback expects this array (parameter named `ɵɵnamespaces`) instead of the `__ngCore__` object, extracting individual namespaces as `ɵhmr0`, `ɵhmr1`, etc. ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9f99196d239479bcba0b42a18a5155ed5a1764ff.patch)).

**Affects:** Applications using Hot Module Replacement (HMR) where components depend on multiple namespace imports, such as standalone components importing directives through separate modules or local files alongside `@angular/core` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58924)).

**Action:** none: The compiler automatically generates the corrected code with the new namespace array format, so no application code or configuration changes are required.
**Before/after:**
```javascript
// Old generated initializer call:
i0.ɵɵreplaceMetadata(Cmp, m.default, i0, [Dep, ...]);

// New generated initializer call:
i0.ɵɵreplaceMetadata(Cmp, m.default, [i0, i1], [Dep, ...]);

// Old generated update callback signature:
function Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, __ngCore__, Dep) { ... }

// New generated update callback signature:
function Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, ɵɵnamespaces, Dep) {
  const ɵhmr0 = ɵɵnamespaces[0];
  const ɵhmr1 = ɵɵnamespaces[1];
  ...
}
```

**What changed:** The `inject()` migration (`ng generate @angular/core:inject-migration`, `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/migration.ts`) previously deleted a class's remaining content when every member of the class was rewritten, because it inserted the new `inject` fields after `constructor.getEnd() + 1`; it now inserts after the end of the last original member (or after `node.getEnd() - 1` when there are no members), so fields declared after the constructor are preserved instead of dropped (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7b5bacc2285803e6ac9691c2dae2361ddca9da9a, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959).
**Affects:** applications that run the `@angular/core:inject-migration` schematic on classes whose constructor parameters are migrated and that also declare members after that constructor (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959).
**Action:** required: re-verify the files touched by `ng generate @angular/core:inject-migration` for lost class members/fields when the migration runs on Angular 19.x tooling before the fix is present.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// input
@Directive()
class MyDir {
  private beforeConstructor: number;

  constructor(private foo: Foo) {
    this.beforeConstructor = this.foo.getValue();
    this.afterConstructor = this.beforeConstructor + 1;
  }

  private afterConstructor: number;
}

// expected output
@Directive()
class MyDir {
  private foo = inject(Foo);
  private beforeConstructor: number = this.foo.getValue();
  private afterConstructor: number = this.beforeConstructor + 1;
}
```

**What changed:** The `inject` dependency-injection migration schematic (the optional `migrate-di-to-inject` migration in Angular 20) previously produced invalid code when a constructor parameter was surrounded by comments, because it did not correctly strip away those parameters. The fix updates the migration to remove comments that sit around the parameters being rewritten (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d1cbdd6acb228773e0fb33958978a14e12be178f, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959). The `inject` migration converts constructor-based injection into field initialization via `inject()`; it is an optional schematic run via `ng generate @angular/core:migrate-di-to-inject` (https://angular.dev/reference/migrations/inject-function).

**Affects:** Applications that run the optional `migrate-di-to-inject` / `inject()` migration on classes whose constructors contain parameters annotated or separated by comments. Not affected if the migration is skipped.

**Action:** none — this is a bug fix inside the migration tool itself; after upgrading to 20.0.0, re-verify the output of the `migrate-di-to-inject` schematic on constructors with parameter comments, as the previous buggy output is no longer generated.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// constructor injection form the schematic operates on
@Component()
export class MyComp {
  constructor(
    private service: MyService,
    @Inject(DI_TOKEN) @Optional() readonly token: string,
  ) {}
}
```

**What changed:** This is a behavior fix inside the `inject` migration schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`), not a runtime breaking change. Previously the schematic rewrote constructor-based DI to `inject()` calls for any class supporting DI; now `analyzeFile` in `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/analysis.ts` adds a check (`allParamsInjectable`) that skips a class entirely when any constructor parameter is typed with an uninjectable primitive keyword — tracked via a new `UNINJECTABLE_TYPE_KINDS` set of `ts.SyntaxKind.TrueKeyword`, `FalseKeyword`, `NumberKeyword`, `StringKeyword`, `NullKeyword`, `VoidKeyword` — unless that parameter carries an `@Inject` or `@Attribute` decorator (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e17ff71c318a1b32d5207b7516856f330f2bcf5a, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959). The check is "not exhaustive" because the migration has no full type checker (same commit). It shipped in v20 as part of PR #58959 ("Fixes for edge cases in inject migration", area: migrations).

**Affects:** Only projects that run the optional `ng generate @angular/core:inject` schematic and contain decorated classes whose constructor parameters have primitive/literal keyword types (e.g. `string`, `number`) without `@Inject`/`@Attribute`. No effect from `ng update` itself.

**Action:** none — no code, config, or dependency changes are required. If you run the inject migration, just re-verify that classes with non-injectable constructor parameters are now left untouched (left as constructor injection) rather than converted, and migrate those manually if desired (schematic options documented at https://angular.dev/reference/migrations/inject-function).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Left unchanged by the v20 inject migration (test case from the commit):
import { Directive, Inject } from '@angular/core';
import { FOO_TOKEN, Foo } from 'foo';

@Directive()
class MyDir {
  constructor(readonly injectable: Foo, private notInjectable: string) {}
}
```

**What changed:** This is a fix to the internal logic of the optional `inject` migration schematic (`@angular/core` `inject-migration`, invoked via `ng generate @angular/core:inject`). Previously the migration's TypeScript reference-counting walker treated constructor parameters of classes it was migrating as "removed," so imports referenced only by those parameter modifiers/decorators were stripped from the file, even when the same imports were still used by classes that were *not* migrated (for example abstract classes, or classes containing a parameter that can't be injected) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7c5f990001c4aac9f48c5461421579c398295356). After the fix, when a constructor parameter is not owned by a class being migrated, its `modifiers` (the decorator usages such as `@Optional()`, `@Inject()`) are still walked and counted, so those imports are preserved (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959).

**Affects:** Applications that run the `inject` migration on files containing more than one class where at least one class is left unmigrated (e.g. an abstract class, or a class with an uninjectable constructor parameter) and the unmigrated class still references an imported decorator or token in its constructor. Not applicable to applications that never run `ng generate @angular/core:inject`.

**Action:** required: re-verify the output of the `inject` migration (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`); ensure that imports still used by unmigrated classes (e.g. `@Optional`, `@Inject` from `@angular/core`, or imported tokens) are not removed, and review the generated diff / run tests before committing.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// dir.ts before migration (two classes; the abstract class is NOT migrated)
import { Directive, Optional } from '@angular/core';
import { Foo } from 'foo';
import { Bar } from 'bar';

@Directive()
class WillMigrate {
  constructor(@Optional() private foo: Foo) {}
}

@Directive()
abstract class WillNotMigrate {
  constructor(@Optional() private bar: Bar) {}
}

// after migration (correct behaviour: Bar import kept because WillNotMigrate still uses it)
import { Directive, Optional, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { Foo } from 'foo';
import { Bar } from 'bar';

@Directive()
class WillMigrate {
  private foo = inject(Foo, { optional: true });
}

@Directive()
abstract class WillNotMigrate {
  constructor(@Optional() private bar: Bar) {}
}
```

**What changed:** The `@angular/core:inject` schematic (which rewrites constructor-based DI to the `inject` function) now preserves TypeScript type literal and tuple type parameters on constructor parameters marked with `@Inject`. Previously, the migration only passed the type as an `inject<T>(...)` type argument for `ts.isTypeReferenceNode` or union types containing type references, silently dropping type literals and tuples. The fix adds `ts.isTypeLiteralNode(type)` and `ts.isTupleTypeNode(type)` to the condition that generates the type argument in `migrateInjectDecorator` (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/58959.diff). A related change in the same PR adds an `UNINJECTABLE_TYPE_KINDS` check so classes whose parameters include uninjectable types (e.g. `string`, `number`, `void`, literal keywords) without an `@Inject`/`@Attribute` decorator are now **skipped** entirely by the migration (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/58959.diff, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959).

**Affects:** Classes using the `@angular/core:inject` schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`) that have `@Inject(TOKEN)` parameters with object literal types (e.g. `{id: number}`) or tuple types (e.g. `[a: number, b: number]`) (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/58959.diff). Also affects classes with parameters whose type is a primitive keyword (e.g. `private notInjectable: string`) with no `@Inject`/`@Attribute` decorator, which are now left unmigrated instead of being incorrectly converted (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/58959.diff).

**Action:** none: this is a fix inside the `@angular/core:inject` schematic itself (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9cbebc6dda89d2fdfc52799aef1ea895dcac2d00, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58959). Applications that were previously migrated with the buggy schematic and ended up with lost type literals or uninjectable classes wrongly converted should **required: re-verify** the generated output by re-running `ng generate @angular/core:inject` after upgrading (https://angular.dev/reference/migrations/inject-function).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: type literal and tuple types were dropped from inject<T>
// After (preserved):
import { Directive, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { FOO_TOKEN } from 'foo';

@Directive()
class MyDir {
  private foo = inject<{
    id: number;
  }>(FOO_TOKEN);

  private bar = inject<[
    a: number,
    b: number
  ]>(FOO_TOKEN);
}
```
```typescript
// Before: classes with uninjectable parameters were (incorrectly) migrated.
// After: such classes are now skipped entirely.
import { Directive } from '@angular/core';
import { Foo } from 'foo';

@Directive()
class MyDir {
  // unchanged - string type is uninjectable and lacks @Inject
  constructor(readonly injectable: Foo, private notInjectable: string) {}
}
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` feature-detection helper `coreVersionSupportsFeature` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/src/feature_detection.ts` now calls `semver.satisfies(coreVersion, minVersion, {includePrerelease: true})` instead of `semver.satisfies(coreVersion, minVersion)`, and the version ranges it is called with in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/src/compiler.ts` were simplified from `'>= 19.0.0-0'` to `'>= 19.0.0'` (implicit standalone default) and from `'>= 17.2.0-0'` to `'>= 17.2.0'` (signals in two-way bindings, `allowSignalsInTwoWayBindings`) (, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59061). Previously, Angular pre-release versions such as `19.1.0-next.0` failed these checks, so the language service treated components as `standalone: false` and mis-flagged declarations ("Extension thinks components are default standalone false with 19.1.0-next.0") (https://github.com/angular/vscode-ng-language-service/issues/2123). After the fix, pre-release `@angular/core` versions correctly get the implicit-standalone default and other feature gating, e.g. diagnostics like "Component TestComp is standalone, and cannot be declared in an NgModule." ()

**Affects:** Projects (mostly editor/language-service and `ngtsc` consumers) using pre-release `@angular/core` versions (e.g. `19.x.y-next.z`); no impact on stable releases.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// feature_detection.ts
- return semver.satisfies(coreVersion, minVersion);
+ return semver.satisfies(coreVersion, minVersion, {includePrerelease: true});

// compiler.ts
- coreVersionSupportsFeature(this.angularCoreVersion, '>= 19.0.0-0');
+ coreVersionSupportsFeature(this.angularCoreVersion, '>= 19.0.0');
```

**What changed:** A crash (`Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'kind')`) in the `UnusedStandaloneImportsRule` extended diagnostic of `@angular/compiler-cli` has been fixed by handling `undefined` values for `Node.parent` that TypeScript's built-in types do not fully reflect (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cd764a31152004d37aa621efc4990c090d86f1e0). This error occurred during `ng build` when the `unusedStandaloneImports` check (NG8113) was enabled (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58872).
**Affects:** Applications that experience build errors due to the `unusedStandaloneImports` diagnostic during the `ng build` process in Angular 19 (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58872).
**Action:** none: The upgrade to Angular 20.0.0 automatically resolves this specific crash. If the diagnostic is not needed, it can be disabled in `tsconfig.json` under `angularCompilerOptions.extendedDiagnostics.checks.unusedStandaloneImports` (https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics/NG8113).
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (in UnusedStandaloneImportsRule)
current = current.parent;

// After (in UnusedStandaloneImportsRule)
// `Node.parent` can be undefined, but the TS types don't reflect it.
// Coerce to null so the value is consistent with the type.
current = current.parent ?? null;
```

**What changed:** In `AfterRenderImpl.maybeTrace` (`packages/core/src/render3/after_render/manager.ts`), the call to `snapshot.run(TracingAction.AFTER_NEXT_RENDER, fn)` was previously combined with the nullish coalescing operator (`?? fn()`). Because `TracingSnapshot.run` may itself return a nullish/falsey value, the `??` operator incorrectly evaluated to true and caused the fallback `fn()` to be executed a second time. The fix replaces the `??` operator with a ternary expression `snapshot ? snapshot.run(...) : fn()` to ensure the function runs exactly once when a snapshot is present (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59073.diff).

**Affects:** Internal framework behavior; no public API changes. Only affects `@angular/core` applications utilizing tracing snapshots during `afterNextRender` phases where the traced callback returns `null`, `undefined`, or `false`.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (packages/core/src/render3/after_render/manager.ts)
protected maybeTrace<T>(fn: () => T, snapshot: TracingSnapshot | null): T {
  return snapshot?.run(TracingAction.AFTER_NEXT_RENDER, fn) ?? fn();
}

// After
protected maybeTrace<T>(fn: () => T, snapshot: TracingSnapshot | null): T {
  return snapshot ? snapshot.run(TracingAction.AFTER_NEXT_RENDER, fn) : fn();
}
```

**What changed:** `SharedStylesHost` in `@angular/platform-browser` (`packages/platform-browser/src/dom/shared_styles_host.ts`) previously only collected SSR-generated inline `<style>` elements tagged with the `ng-app-id` attribute for reuse during hydration; external component styles emitted by dev-mode SSR as `<link rel="stylesheet">` elements (from `styleUrls`, via `ɵɵExternalStylesFeature`) were not collected, so a second `<link>` was added on the client — two copies of the stylesheet in the DOM. The internal `addServerStyles(doc, appId, inline, external)` function now queries `style[ng-app-id="..."],link[ng-app-id="..."]` in `doc.head` and registers matching `HTMLLinkElement`s in the `external` usage map (keyed by the filename portion of `href`, since the build generates a unique href value), letting `addStyles(styles, urls)` reuse the server element (marked with `ng-style-reused` in dev mode) instead of appending a duplicate (commit ae0802d, PR #59031; https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ae0802d63c50307791e8a5d765573836dfe89075, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59031). The duplicate links broke Vite-based CSS HMR in the Angular CLI dev server, which only updates the first matching stylesheet, leaving styles inconsistent (PR #59031).

**Affects:** Angular SSR applications running in development mode (`ng serve` with SSR) whose components use `styleUrls`; the duplicate `<link>` elements and the HMR issue only occur in dev mode. Production builds are unaffected. This is an internal runtime fix with no public API change.

**Action:** none — no application code, configuration, or dependency changes are required; after upgrading, apps should simply show a single `<link>` element (tagged `ng-style-reused` in dev) per SSR-rendered external component stylesheet, and CSS hot module replacement via the Angular CLI dev server should be re-verified as working.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19: only inline <style> elements from SSR were collected
addServerStyles(doc, appId, this.inline);

// v20: <link> elements from SSR are collected too
addServerStyles(doc, appId, this.inline, this.external);

// from shared_styles_host_spec.ts (v20): reused SSR link instead of a duplicate
expect(doc.head.innerHTML).toContain(
  '<link rel="stylesheet" href="component-1.css" ng-style-reused="">',
);
```

**What changed:** Before v20, during server-side rendering Angular called `scheduleDelayedTrigger` (in `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts`) for `@defer (on idle)` and `@defer (on timer(...))` blocks, which invoked `scheduleFn` (`onIdle`/`onTimer`) and created `setTimeout` calls. Those pending timeouts kept the app unstable, so the SSR serialization step waited for them to clear before rendering the response. Commit 3793218e77 adds an early exit — `if (!shouldTriggerDeferBlock(TriggerType.Regular, lView)) return;` right after `renderPlaceholder(lView, tNode)` — so on the server in regular SSR mode (incremental hydration **not** enabled) the triggers never fire and no `setTimeout` is scheduled. The placeholder is still rendered; deferred blocks still trigger on the client after hydration (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3793218e77d699ddfae95a53ad048d4bfb9f042c, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59177). When incremental hydration is enabled, `shouldTriggerDeferBlock` returns true and behaviour is unchanged.

**Affects:** Applications using `@defer` blocks with `on idle` or `on timer(...)` triggers that render on the server via `@angular/platform-server` in regular SSR/SSG mode without incremental hydration; previously SSR responses were delayed by those timeouts. The PR is marked `action: merge, target: patch, Bugfix`, "breaking change: No", and it is not listed in the v20.0.0 release notes breaking changes.

**Action:** none (behavioral fix; re-verify SSR render timing and that deferred blocks still load/hydrate in the browser).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts — scheduleDelayedTrigger
   renderPlaceholder(lView, tNode);

+  // Exit early to avoid invoking `scheduleFn`, which would
+  // add `setTimeout` call and potentially delay serialization
+  // on the server unnecessarily.
+  if (!shouldTriggerDeferBlock(TriggerType.Regular, lView)) return;
+
   const injector = lView[INJECTOR];
   const lDetails = getLDeferBlockDetails(lView, tNode);

   const cleanupFn = scheduleFn(
     () => triggerDeferBlock(TriggerType.Regular, lView, tNode),
     injector,
   );
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 fixed a bug in incremental hydration where nested `@defer` blocks with `hydrate on timer(<delay>)` failed to serialize the delay value during hydration annotation processing on the server, due to type mismatches and unintended `any` types (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cfc96ed82cbe958ea7548718f76a2e7a3d6826a9, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59173). Internally, `getHydrateTriggers` changed its return type from `Map<DeferBlockTrigger, number | null>` to `Map<DeferBlockTrigger, HydrateTriggerDetails | null>`, and `ɵɵdeferHydrateOnTimer(delay: number)` now stores `{delay}` in the hydrate triggers map instead of the raw `delay` number (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cfc96ed82cbe958ea7548718f76a2e7a3d6826a9.patch). The serialization in `serializeHydrateTriggers` now pushes `{trigger, delay: details.delay}` rather than `{trigger, details}` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cfc96ed82cbe958ea7548718f76a2e7a3d6826a9.patch). The PR notes it is a bugfix and introduces no breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59173).

**Affects:** applications that use incremental hydration (`withIncrementalHydration()`) with SSR/hydration and have `@defer` blocks using `hydrate on timer(...)` inside another `@defer` block; the fix is internal to `@angular/core` with no public API change.

**Action:** required: re-verify that nested `@defer` blocks with `hydrate on timer(...)` correctly hydrate after the specified delay after the SSR-to-client transition.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// internal @angular/core change (not public API), from commit cfc96ed82c
- hydrateTriggers.set(DeferBlockTrigger.Timer, delay);
+ hydrateTriggers.set(DeferBlockTrigger.Timer, {delay});
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/platform-server`, the transfer-state serialization hook (`serializeTransferStateFactory`, registered via `BEFORE_APP_SERIALIZED`) now runs `warnIfStateTransferHappened()` in dev mode (`ngDevMode`). A new platform-provided injection token `TRANSFER_STATE_SERIALIZED_FOR_APPID` (a `Set<string>` of `APP_ID`s) records whether server state was already serialized; on a second serialization for the same app id, Angular logs a `console.warn` starting with "Angular detected an incompatible configuration, which causes duplicate serialization of the server-side application state" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9085a8fbd8cb61e3ce45adfa9ca2e96ba0be6f62, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58935). This happens when SSR server providers are registered more than once, e.g. combining `imports: [ServerModule]` (or `importProvidersFrom(ServerModule)`) with `providers: [provideServerRendering()]`; the symptom is two `<script id="ng-state" type="application/json">` blocks in the rendered HTML (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58531). The test added in the commit notes the duplicate serialization is "a warning in v19 / and might become an error in v20" (commit patch).

**Affects:** SSR applications using `@angular/platform-server` where server providers are registered through more than one mechanism — e.g. `ServerModule` imported alongside `provideServerRendering()` — resulting in duplicate `ng-state` scripts and the new dev-mode warning.

**Action:** required: re-verify SSR output for a single `<script id="ng-state">` element and no "Angular detected an incompatible configuration" warning; if duplicated, keep `provideServerRendering()` as the only server-provider registration and remove the other registration(s) (e.g. `ServerModule` / `importProvidersFrom(ServerModule)`), as the warning text instructs.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Bad: server providers registered twice -> duplicate ng-state script + dev-mode warning
@NgModule({
  declarations: [MyTransferStateApp],
  imports: [BrowserModule, ServerModule],
  providers: [provideServerRendering()],
  bootstrap: [MyTransferStateApp],
})
class DoubleTransferStateModule {}
```

