**Affects:** Applications that have HMR/Live Reload enabled, use `@defer` blocks, and have class metadata generation disabled (e.g., when `supportTestBed` is set to false).
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```json
// angular.json
"projects": {
  "my-project": {
    "architect": {
      "serve": {
        "options": {
          "hmr": false
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 contains the bug fix `fix(core): Ensure that a destroyed effect never run. (#59415)` (commit 07afce81b8), fixing #59410 where "`EffectRef.destroy` has no impact". Before: "a scheduled root effect, even if destroyed instantly, would still run at least once" — i.e. calling `effectRef.destroy()` on an `effect()` whose run was already scheduled (e.g. a root effect created via `TestBed.runInInjectionContext(() => effect(..., {injector: TestBed.inject(EnvironmentInjector)}))`, or one scheduled through microtasks/`TestBed.flushEffects()`) did not cancel the pending run, and the body still executed (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59415, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59410, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/07afce81b8ce28d1b308ff25017a4d4993881f36). The fix adds `abstract remove(e: SchedulableEffect): void` to the internal `EffectScheduler` in `packages/core/src/render3/reactivity/root_effect_scheduler.ts` (implemented by `ZoneAwareEffectScheduler`, which deletes the handle from `this.queues` and decrements `this.queuedEffectCount`) and calls `this.scheduler.remove(this)` from the root effect node's destroy logic in `packages/core/src/render3/reactivity/effect.ts` (diff of the commit). After: an effect destroyed before its scheduled run never executes, and neither do subsequent runs after later signal writes.

**Affects:** Applications or unit tests that call `EffectRef.destroy()` (or destroy an injector/view) while a run of the `effect()` is already scheduled and relied on that queued body still executing at least once; also third-party implementations of the internal `EffectScheduler` token.

**Action:** none — no code, configuration or dependency change is required; however, re-verify any test that asserted an effect body runs after `destroy()` was called, since such assertions now fail (upstream test expectations were changed to `expect(effectCounter).toBe(0)` after `effectRef.destroy()` + `TestBed.flushEffects()`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular 19 behaviour (issue #59410): body still ran after destroy
effectRef.destroy();
TestBed.flushEffects();
expect(effectCounter).toBe(1); // destroyed effect still executed

// Angular 20 behaviour (new test in reactivity_spec.ts, #59415):
effectRef.destroy();
TestBed.flushEffects();
expect(effectCounter).toBe(0);

counter.set(2);
TestBed.flushEffects();
expect(effectCounter).toBe(0);
```

**What changed:** Angular fixed a bug where component styles were not replaced during Hot Module Replacement (HMR) when an animations renderer was in use (PR #59393, commit 6d00efd). Previously, `clearRendererCache` in `packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts` reached into a private field of the renderer factory — `(factory as {rendererByCompId?: Map<string, unknown>}).rendererByCompId?.delete(def.id)` — but with animations enabled, the `AnimationRendererFactory` from `@angular/platform-browser/animations` wraps the base `DomRendererFactory2`, so the cache lookup on the wrapper failed and stale renderer data (including styles) persisted (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/6d00efde952971573359b32cab06d0a513600fe0). The fix introduces a `componentReplaced(componentId: string)` method: `DomRendererFactory2` implements it to call `this.rendererByCompId.delete(componentId)`, `AnimationRendererFactory` implements it to forward the call to its `delegate`, and `clearRendererCache` now calls `(factory as {componentReplaced?: (id: string) => void}).componentReplaced?.(def.id)` (diff of the same commit). This is an internal, non-public API rework; no migration schematic exists for it in Angular 20.0.0 (`packages/core/schematics/migrations.json` at the 20.0.0 tag only lists `inject-flags`, `test-bed-get`, `control-flow-migration`, `document-core`; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/schematics/migrations.json).

**Affects:** Applications using HMR (`ng serve --hmr` / dev-server HMR) together with animations (`provideAnimations` / `@angular/platform-browser/animations`), where editing a component's `styles`/`styleUrl` previously left stale styles on screen until a full reload. Only dev-time behavior; no production code paths change. Custom `RendererFactory2` implementations that subclassed `DomRendererFactory2` or mimicked the `rendererByCompId` cache-clearing hack in the NOTE comment would need re-verification.

**Action:** none — the fix is internal to `@angular/core` HMR and `@angular/platform-browser`/animations; application code, templates and configuration need no changes. Optionally re-verify that editing a component's styles while running with `--hmr` and animations applied updates styles live.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// core/src/render3/hmr.ts (clearRendererCache) — before
(factory as {rendererByCompId?: Map<string, unknown>}).rendererByCompId?.delete(def.id);

// after
(factory as {componentReplaced?: (id: string) => void}).componentReplaced?.(def.id);
```
```ts
// platform-browser/animations AnimationRendererFactory — added
protected componentReplaced(componentId: string) {
  (this.delegate as {componentReplaced?: (id: string) => void}).componentReplaced?.(componentId);
}
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` HMR dependency extraction logic (`PotentialTopLevelReadsVisitor` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/hmr/src/extract_dependencies.ts`) was extended to recognize more TypeScript AST node types when deciding whether an identifier used in a component definition counts as a top-level dependency captured for hot module replacement. Previously, identifiers appearing as variable-declaration initializers/names in factories, arrow function bodies, parameter/binding-element/property-declaration/enum-member initializers, `for`/`for-in`/`for-of` statement parts, and expression statements could be missed; the visitor now handles `ts.isVariableDeclaration` (name or initializer), `ts.isArrowFunction` (body), `ts.isParameter`, `ts.isBindingElement`, `ts.isPropertyDeclaration`, `ts.isEnumMember`, `ts.isForStatement`, `ts.isForInStatement`, `ts.isForOfStatement` and `ts.isExpressionStatement` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2b4b7c3ebfb2d4f4fd96fd2f1890b67c832505fd.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59445). Result: such values (e.g. a `const value` read inside a `useFactory` arrow function) are now passed into the generated `ɵɵreplaceMetadata(Cmp, m.default, [i0], [token, value, Component])` call and the `Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, ɵɵnamespaces, token, value, Component)` HMR update function, so HMR updates no longer lose these dependencies.

**Affects:** Applications that enable HMR (`ng serve --hmr`) and have components whose metadata (e.g. `providers` with `useFactory`) references top-level identifiers via variable initializers, arrow function bodies, or the newly-handled node types.

**Action:** none — this is an internal compiler-cli bug fix; no application code, configuration, or dependency changes are required. Applications relying on HMR with factory-referenced constants should re-verify that hot updates apply correctly.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New handling in PotentialTopLevelReadsVisitor (extract_dependencies.ts):
if (ts.isPropertyAssignment(parent) || ts.isParameter(parent) ||
    ts.isBindingElement(parent) || ts.isPropertyDeclaration(parent) ||
    ts.isEnumMember(parent)) {
  return parent.initializer === node;
}
if (ts.isVariableDeclaration(parent)) {
  return parent.name === node || parent.initializer === node;
}
if (ts.isArrowFunction(parent)) {
  return parent.body === node;
}
```

**What changed:** In the HMR metadata-replacement path (`packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts`, `ɵɵreplaceMetadata` → `recreateLView` → `clearRendererCache`), the old component renderer was only removed from the factory cache, never destroyed, so its `<style>` tags stayed in the DOM and old styles leaked back into the component alongside the new ones. The fix adds `this.rendererByCompId.get(componentId)?.destroy();` before `this.rendererByCompId.delete(componentId);` in `DomRendererFactory2.componentReplaced` in `packages/platform-browser/src/dom/dom_renderer.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f893d0723262d699979d55e43e4ddbcf64a3fc13.patch). The same commit also reworked internals: `recreateLView`/`recreateMatchingLViews` now take both `newDef` and `oldDef`, and `ngDevMode && assertNotEqual(newDef, oldDef, 'Expected different component definition')` was added (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59514). By Angular 20.0.x the HMR code additionally destroys the old `LView` (`destroyLView`) and attaches the new renderer via `rendererFactory.createRenderer(host, newDef)` only afterwards (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using dev-time HMR of components (`ɵɵreplaceMetadata` / `ɵɵgetReplaceMetadataURL`, i.e. `ng serve` with component hot reload) whose component styles change between reloads; custom `RendererFactory2` implementations that mirror `DomRendererFactory2.componentReplaced`. Production behaviour is unchanged.

**Action:** none — the fix is internal to `@angular/core` and `@angular/platform-browser`. Only re-verify that after an HMR style change the previously rendered component styles are actually removed from the DOM.

**Before/after:**
```diff
