if (
  this.config.bestEffortMode &&
  (info.fieldReason === null ||
    !nonIgnorableFieldIncompatibilities.includes(info.fieldReason))
) {
  continue;
}

incompatibleQueries++;
```

I must correct the row's framing first: I verified that this change is a *notable new capability* that is **already available in Angular 19.1.x** (the `componentInstance` getter and `exportAs="ngComponentOutlet"` were introduced in the 19.1 minor and are present in the version-neutral sources), not something introduced at the 19.0.0 → 20.0.0 hop. The 20.x source confirms the same `componentInstance` getter is present. So for a 19.0 → 20.0 migration, no new action is required beyond what a 19.1→20.0 app already has.

**What changed:** The `NgComponentOutlet` directive exposes the currently rendered component via a new public getter `componentInstance(): T | null`, returning the instance from the underlying `ComponentRef.instance` (or `null` if none has been rendered) (#58698). The directive is also exported via `exportAs: 'ngComponentOutlet'`, so a template can capture it with a template variable and read `componentInstance` (; ; https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e4c50b3bea22ca2afba74465893c36730952f4b9). This capability already shipped in Angular 19.1, so it is present in both the source and target versions of this hop.

**Affects:** Applications using `NgComponentOutlet` (from `@angular/common`) that previously had to obtain the dynamically created component instance another way (e.g. via `@ViewChild` or `ViewContainerRef`); no behavior previously relied upon changes.

**Action:** none (the feature is additive and already available as of Angular 19.1).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- ngComponentOutlet directive source (20.x) -->
@Directive({
  selector: '[ngComponentOutlet]',
  exportAs: 'ngComponentOutlet',
})
export class NgComponentOutlet<T = any> implements OnChanges, DoCheck, OnDestroy {
  get componentInstance(): T | null {
    return this._componentRef?.instance ?? null;
  }
}

<!-- consuming it via exportAs in a template -->
<ng-container [ngComponentOutlet]="ct.type"
              [ngComponentOutletInputs]="inputs()"
              [ngComponentOutletInjector]="ct.injector"
              #instance="ngComponentOutlet" />
@let componentInstance = instance?.componentInstance;
```

**What changed:** Two-way bindings (`[(foo)]`) previously generated a type-check-block (TCB) statement of the form `someOutput.subscribe($event => expr);`, which effectively did no type checking on the event side (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59002). Angular 20 re-adds output-side checking by emitting instead:

```
someOutput.subscribe($event => { var _t1 = unwrapSignalValue(this.someField); _t1 = $event; });
```

so the value emitted by the output event is now assigned to (and therefore type-checked against) the bound target expression (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c5c20e9d86d72b33840dcf0adea02876437a589f). Before v19 the parser turned two-way bindings into `foo = $event` at the parser level; #54065 changed that to preserve the actual expression for signal support, which is what caused the lost checking. The regression only produced missed errors "for the case where the input side of the two-way binding has a wider type than the output side" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59002). The change is in `@angular/compiler-cli` and ships in 20.0.0 (listed under `compiler-cli` in https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0); it is *not* listed in the v20 "Breaking Changes" section of those release notes.

**Affects:** Templates with two-way bindings `[(input)]=(output)` — e.g. `[(ngModel)]`, `[(value)]`, `[(disabled)]`, custom `@Input`/`@Output` pairs — where the output event payload type is not assignable to the bound target (target is narrower than the emitted value, including targets that are `WritableSignal`s).

**Action:** required: re-verify that `ng build` / `ng test` template type checking passes after upgrading; fix any newly reported assignment errors on the event side of `[(...)]` bindings by narrowing the `@Output`/`output()`/`emit()` payload type, widening the bound target, or replacing the two-way binding with separate `[input]` and `(output)` bindings. No dependency, config, or build change is described by the sources.

**Before/after:**
```
// old TCB (no type checking of the event value)
someOutput.subscribe($event => expr);

// new TCB (event value is assigned to the bound expression)
someOutput.subscribe($event => {
  var _t1 = unwrapSignalValue(this.someField);
  _t1 = $event;
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 added an optional 4th `options` parameter to `Renderer2.listen`, and a new exported interface `ListenerOptions { capture?: boolean; once?: boolean; passive?: boolean; }` from `@angular/core` (`packages/core/src/render.ts` now exports `ListenerOptions` alongside `Renderer2`, `RendererFactory2`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d010e11b735562ded439989ddb84cc83c6c00e81). Adjacent APIs gained the same optional `options?: ListenerOptions` parameter: `EventManager.addEventListener`, abstract `EventManagerPlugin.addEventListener`, internal `ɵRenderer.listen`, `DomAdapter.onAndCancel(el, evt, listener, options?)`, `DomEventsPlugin.addEventListener`/`removeEventListener`, `KeyEventsPlugin.addEventListener`, `ServerEventManagerPlugin.addEventListener`, `BaseAnimationRenderer.listen`/`AnimationRenderer.listen` in `@angular/animations/browser`, `DynamicDelegationRenderer.listen` in `@angular/platform-browser/animations/async`, and `DefaultDomRenderer2.listen`. `DomEventsPlugin` now passes `options` to `addEventListener`/`removeEventListener` instead of hard-coding `false`. PR was labeled `action: merge, area: core, target: minor, detected: feature` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59092).

**Affects:** Applications that implement or extend `Renderer2` / custom renderers (including animation renderer wrappers) or subclass `EventManagerPlugin` and override `addEventListener`; the new parameter is optional, so existing 3-argument implementations still satisfy the widened signatures.

**Action:** none — no code change is required; the parameter is additive. Only verify that custom `Renderer2`/`EventManagerPlugin` implementations that delegate to `super`/a delegate renderer still compile and forward `options` if they need `capture`/`once`/`passive` behaviour.

