// after (fix in #59256): lazy resolution
private scheduler: ChangeDetectionScheduler | null = null;
private readonly injector = inject(Injector);
// ... in the async renderer load callback:
this.scheduler ??= this.injector.get(ChangeDetectionScheduler, null, {optional: true});
this.scheduler?.notify(NotificationSource.AsyncAnimationsLoaded);
```

**What changed:** The `Router.dispose()` method now manually completes/unsubscribes the `events` subject (via `this._events.unsubscribe()` internally, though the changelog describes it as "complete") to release all active observers and enable granular garbage collection. This is because users often forget to unsubscribe manually when subscribing to `router.events` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59327), (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/52a6710f54bcec81f4cde23a78b9f78d038156c5)). The commit note states: "We call `unsubscribe()` to release observers, as users may forget to unsubscribe manually when subscribing to `router.events`. We do not call `complete()` because it is unsafe; if someone subscribes using the `first` operator and the observable completes before emitting a value, RxJS will throw an error." ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/52a6710f54bcec81f4cde23a78b9f78d038156c5)).

**Affects:** Applications that subscribe to `router.events` and rely on the observable remaining active or incomplete after `Router.dispose()` is called, or those using operators like `first` on `router.events` without proper error handling in the case of completion.

**Action:** required: Re-verify subscriptions to `router.events`, especially those using `first` operator, to ensure they handle the completion/unsubscription correctly. Ensure proper cleanup strategies are in place for router event subscriptions.

