}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's HMR (Hot Module Replacement) initializer no longer requests component metadata from the dev-server at the absolute URL `/@ng/component`, but at the relative URL `./@ng/component`. In `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_hmr_compiler.ts`, `compileHmrInitializer` changed `const urlPartial = \`/@ng/component?c=${id}&t=\`;` to `const urlPartial = \`./@ng/component?c=${id}&t=\`;`, so the generated code becomes `new URL("./@ng/component?c=test.ts%40Cmp&t=" + encodeURIComponent(t), import.meta.url).href` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8dcd88998700a94115a542462e6ae6beedbfbd9d.patch). This makes HMR work correctly with `base href`, proxies and other advanced development setups, and resolves angular-cli issue #29248 (HMR/CSS requests from a remote dev server pointing at the wrong host when `ng serve` runs on localhost). It works in conjunction with angular-cli PR #29248 and PR #29386 ("fix(@angular/build): handle relative `@ng/components`"), which taught the dev-server to handle the relative form (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59620, https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/pull/29386, https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/pull/29248).

**Affects:** Applications using HMR with the `application` builder / `ng serve` (Vite dev server), especially under a non-root `<base href>`, a reverse proxy, or a remote dev-server setup. Only the generated dev-mode HMR URL changes; production output is unaffected.

**Action:** none — no source, configuration or dependency changes are required; optionally re-verify that HMR component/style updates still work when serving through a proxy or custom `base href`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_hmr_compiler.ts (compileHmrInitializer)
- const urlPartial = `/@ng/component?c=${id}&t=`;
+ const urlPartial = `./@ng/component?c=${id}&t=`;

// generated HMR loader
- import(/* @vite-ignore */ new URL("/@ng/component?c=test.ts%40Cmp&t=" + encodeURIComponent(t), import.meta.url).href)
+ import(/* @vite-ignore */ new URL("./@ng/component?c=test.ts%40Cmp&t=" + encodeURIComponent(t), import.meta.url).href)
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` now disables tree shaking for standalone imports/pipes during Hot Module Replacement (HMR) generation. Previously, unused standalone imports were shaken out and omitted from the generated HMR metadata function (e.g., `ɵɵreplaceMetadata` arguments), causing mismatched dependency sets if the template changed. Now, all declared dependencies are preserved in the static part of the HMR code to ensure stability (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59595).

**Affects:** Angular applications with HMR enabled (`ng serve` with `hmr`) that use standalone components with `imports` containing directives or pipes that are not used in the current template state.

**Action:** required: verify that removing component selectors from templates during HMR updates renders correctly without causing missing component dependencies or inconsistent behavior (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59595).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (HMR code omitted unused 'Dep' if not in template)
export default function Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, ɵɵnamespaces, Component) { ... }

// After (HMR code preserves 'Dep' in metadata function)
export default function Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, ɵɵnamespaces, Dep) { ... }
```

**What changed:** The generic `T` in `Resource<T>` previously represented the resolved type of the resource, and `undefined` was implicitly added to `.value` signal types. In Angular 20, `resource()` and `rxResource()` now return `ResourceRef<T | undefined>` explicitly. The union with `undefined` was removed from the base types (`Resource<T>.value` is now `Signal<T>`, `WritableResource<T>.value` is now `WritableSignal<T>`). `hasValue()` now narrows the resource type to `Exclude<T, undefined>`. Previously, `hasValue()` returned a type guard that only ensured the `value` property wasn't optional, but didn't strip `undefined` if it was part of the original `T`. (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59024.diff)

**Affects:** Applications using `resource()`, `rxResource()`, or manually typing `Resource<T>` / `WritableResource<T>` where `T` already includes `undefined`, or where code relies on the previous signature of `hasValue()` narrowing `Resource<T>` without excluding `undefined`.

**Action:** required: Update explicit `Resource<T>` type annotations if `T` previously included `undefined` and the new implicit `| undefined` in `ResourceRef` is undesirable. Re-verify code using `hasValue()` to ensure it correctly narrows the type to `Exclude<T, undefined>` as needed for non-null assertions. If `hasValue()` usage was intended to guarantee non-null access without `undefined` exclusion, adjust logic or types accordingly.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// BEFORE
export function resource<T, R>(options: ResourceOptions<T, R>): ResourceRef<T>;
export interface Resource<T> {
    hasValue(): this is Resource<T> & { value: Signal<T> };
    readonly value: Signal<T | undefined>;
}

// AFTER
export function resource<T, R>(options: ResourceOptions<T, R>): ResourceRef<T | undefined>;
export interface Resource<T> {
    hasValue(): this is Resource<Exclude<T, undefined>>;
    readonly value: Signal<T>;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular's HMR machinery (`ɵɵreplaceMetadata` / `recreateLView` in `packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts`) previously threw an error when re-creating a live component instance that uses `ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom`, because the browser only allows one shadow root per node and Angular tried to attach a second one. The fix checks `oldDef.encapsulation === ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom` and, before recreating the `LView`, performs `host.cloneNode(false)`, calls `host.replaceWith(newHost)` and uses the clone as the new host for future updates (the shadow root cannot be detached). Fixes issue #59588 ("HMR Ionic Angular live reload not working (ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom)"), where live reload failed or blanked the page during `ionic serve` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/266a8f2f2ebf9f5e310ba5de695be5072790e1e5, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59597).

**Affects:** Applications using dev-time HMR / live reload together with components declared with `encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom`. Production behaviour is unchanged; only host node identity during an HMR swap changes (the old element is replaced by a shallow clone, so external references to the old host element and attributes like `_nghost`/`_ngcontent` on it are replaced by the new node's).

**Action:** none — internal framework fix; re-verify that live reload works for `ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom` components during HMR. Note that any app code caching a reference to the host element of such a component should re-verify it after an HMR update, since the node is replaced.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/render3/hmr.ts (after fix)
if (oldDef.encapsulation === ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom) {
  const newHost = host.cloneNode(false) as HTMLElement;
  host.replaceWith(newHost);
  host = newHost;
}
```

**What changed:** The `computed()` implementation in `@angular/core/primitives/signals` was updated to treat exceptions thrown by the `equal` function as part of the computation phase. Previously, signal reads and exceptions inside `equal` could leak to the consumer. Now, `setActiveConsumer(null)` is called before evaluating `node.equal(oldValue, newValue)`, ensuring Angular does **not** track reactive reads within `equal` and that any exceptions thrown by `equal` are caught and cached as an error state for the computed signal.
**Affects:** Applications providing a custom `equal` function in the `computed()` options where that function reads other signals or may throw exceptions.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: equal was evaluated in the consumer's reactive context
// node.equal(oldValue, newValue) could leak reads/exceptions

// After: equal is evaluated with no active consumer (untracked)
// https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d12a186d531b41e6a16f84446a1d54eaed010fc4
const c = computed(
  () => {
    return source();
  },
  {
    equal: (a, b) => {
      // Reads in equal are no longer tracked by dependents
      // Exceptions in equal are now caught and cached as ERRORED state
      return a === b;
    },
  },
);
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's shadow-CSS shim (`packages/compiler/src/shadow_css.ts`) previously handled `:host-context` with a regex that terminated each pseudo-selector at the first `,` or `{`, without awareness of nested selectors. Because `:host-context(.foo)` is replaced by *two* scoped selectors, a comma inside a nested pseudo-class broke scoping: `:host-context(.foo) a:not(.a, .b) {}` incorrectly compiled to `.foo[a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .foo [a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .b) {}` (malformed CSS) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59276). In v20, `_convertColonHostContext` splits selector text on top-level commas (tracking parenthesis depth), processes `:host-context` per selector part via the new `_convertColonHostContextInSelectorPart`, and stitches results back together; the `:host-context` pattern is now terminated only by `{` (`_hostContextPattern`), unlike `:host` which still terminates on `{` or `,` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ecfb74d287bec7bec37d0b476b321b047bef2c43).

**Affects:** Components whose component CSS uses `:host-context(...)` combined with a comma-separated child selector or commas inside nested pseudo-classes (e.g. `a:not(.a, .b)`); such styles previously compiled to broken CSS in v19.

**Action:** none — no application code or configuration changes; upgrading components using `:host-context` with comma-separated/nested-comma selectors simply get correctly scoped CSS. Re-verify rendered styles of such components (previously broken output may have been worked around with extra escaping/specificity hacks that can now be removed).

**Before/after:**
```css
/* Input selector (unchanged in both versions): */
:host-context(.foo) a:not(.a, .b) {}

/* v19 (broken) */
.foo[a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .foo [a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .b) {}

/* v20 (correct) */
.foo[a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .b), .foo [a-host] a[contenta]:not(.a, .b) {}
```

**What changed:** The `fix(compiler-cli): extract parenthesized dependencies during HMR (#59644)` change modifies the HMR dependency extraction logic to correctly handle parenthesized identifiers. Previously, `extractHmrDependencies` failed to identify top-level reads wrapped in parentheses (e.g., `(foo)` or `((bar))`) within component definitions, leading to missing dependencies in the generated HMR metadata (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/53160e504df44b05f59cacd9afeb40a0e627b744). The fix adds logic in `isTopLevelIdentifierReference` to unwrap these `ParenthesizedExpression` nodes (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/53160e504df44b05f59cacd9afeb40a0e627b744/packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/hmr/src/extract_dependencies.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using Hot Module Replacement (HMR) where components have dependencies on variables or imports enclosed in parentheses within their metadata (e.g., providers, imports, or animations) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59644).

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
