**What changed:** Angular 20 modifies `@angular/platform-browser`'s `DomRendererFactory2` renderers (`NoneEncapsulationDomRenderer` and `ShadowDomRenderer`) so that in dev mode (`ngDevMode`) component CSS containing an external `/*# sourceMappingURL=... */` comment is rewritten by the new internal function `addBaseHrefToCssSourceMap(baseHref, styles)` before being injected, using the document's `<base href>` value obtained via `getDOM().getBaseHref(doc)` (packages/platform-browser/src/dom/dom_renderer.ts) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1828a8406201827e52549c8afa487bf6364a70c3, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59730). Previously, relative sourcemap URLs in inline component styles resolved against the current page URL, so visiting a multi-segment URL like `` caused the browser/dev server to request `` instead of `` (fixes issue #59729, where SSR dev requests for `.component.css.map` hit the API server and 404'd). Now relative map URLs are resolved against the absolute baseHref (e.g. baseHref `/base/` yields `/*# sourceMappingURL=/base/cmp-none.css.map */`). Inline `data:` maps, URLs starting with `/`, and `http(s)://` URLs are left unchanged. The rewrite only happens in development; production builds are unaffected.
**Affects:** Applications (typically SSR or apps served under sub-paths) whose components include an external CSS sourcemap comment (`sourceMappingURL=<file>.css.map`) in `styles`, viewed in dev mode with a `<base href>` set; dev-server/network requests for `.css.map` files.
**Action:** none — this is an automatic bug fix; no code, configuration, or dependency changes are required. Re-verify that component CSS sourcemaps load correctly in dev under multi-segment routes if you previously worked around the 404 issue.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Test from the commit: with <base href="/base/" />
// Before: relative map URL resolved against page URL, e.g. /foo/bar/cmp-none.css.map
// After: rewritten to be relative to baseHref:
expect(document.head.querySelector('style')?.textContent).toContain(
  '/*# sourceMappingURL=/base/cmp-none.css.map */',
);

// addBaseHrefToCssSourceMap('/base/', ['/*# sourceMappingURL=style.css */'])
// => ['/*# sourceMappingURL=/base/style.css */']
// data:, absolute-path, and http(s) URLs are left unchanged
```

**What changed:** In Angular v20 the pseudo-mouse events produced by `createMouseSpecialEvent` (in `packages/core/primitives/event-dispatch/src/event.ts`, part of `@angular/core/primitives/event-dispatch`, with the change noted under the `platform-browser` commit scope) are now built as a plain object `copy` whose type is `{-readonly [P in keyof Event]?: Event[P]} & {'_originalEvent'?: Event}` — i.e. the previously-declared optional `_originalEvent?: Event` property on the copied event is actually populated (assigned) rather than left undefined. The copy loop iterates `for (const property in e)` and skips `srcElement` and `target` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/12256574626f04f5fe2b41e805f7bdc93d62df0a.patch). PR #59690 is described by the author as "This fixes an internal bug" with `action: merge, area: core, target: patch`, and explicitly "Does this PR introduce a breaking change? … No" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59690). Only the type declaration and formatting appear in the diff of the source file; the rest of the commit is `package.json`/`yarn.lock` build tooling noise (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1c84cbca30e6606e6df3f40346989d9434d89bc6.patch).

**Affects:** No application-facing condition identified; this is an internal event-dispatch bug fix that the PR author marks as non-breaking. Only code that inspected `_originalEvent` on generated mouseenter/mouseleave pseudo events (advanced/manual `MouseEventsPlugin` internals) could observe a difference — such an object now carries the originating `Event` instead of `undefined`.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// createMouseSpecialEvent — packages/core/primitives/event-dispatch/src/event.ts
-  const copy: {-readonly[P in keyof Event]?: Event[P]}&
-      {'_originalEvent'?: Event} = {};
+  const copy: {-readonly [P in keyof Event]?: Event[P]} & {'_originalEvent'?: Event} = {};
   for (const property in e) {
     if (property === 'srcElement' || property === 'target') {
       continue;
```

**What changed:** The internal `createMouseSpecialEvent` function (in `packages/core/primitives/event-dispatch/src/event.ts`) synthesizes pseudo `mouseenter`/`mouseleave` events from native `mouseover`/`mouseout`. Previously the pseudo-event copy did not carry the native source event; the fix adds the `_originalEvent` property to the synthesized event, setting `copy['_originalEvent'] = e` so consumers can recover the underlying native event via `event._originalEvent` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3f4d5f636aac90cabe32ff6c4d75180ced99eb97.patch)). The PR is labeled a "Bugfix" and explicitly "Does not introduce a breaking change" ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59690)).

**Affects:** Applications that rely on `mouseenter`/`mouseleave` event objects exposing an `_originalEvent` property (e.g. event-dispatch / zone.js consumers reading `getNativeEvent`-style accessors); the property was previously absent on these pseudo-events and is now populated. No public API signature changes.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/primitives/event-dispatch/src/event.ts
// Before:
const copy: {-readonly [P in keyof Event]?: Event[P]} = {};
// ...
copy['bubbles'] = false;
return copy as Event;

// After:
const copy: {-readonly[P in keyof Event]?: Event[P]} & {'_originalEvent'?: Event} = {};
// ...
copy['bubbles'] = false;
copy['_originalEvent'] = e;
return copy as Event;
```

The retrieved changelog and release notes do not mention this commit. The PR and commit describe an internal fix inside `NavigationTransitions` (`packages/router/src/navigation_transition.ts`). It does not change public API, and there is no user-facing migration step. The behavioral effect is that an `unhandledrejection` / `NG0205: Injector has already been destroyed` no longer occurs when a router guard observable completes before emitting and the application is destroyed.

**What changed:** In the router, the internal `NavigationTransitions` class now tracks destruction via `DestroyRef.onDestroy` and a `destroyed` flag; when an observable (e.g., a `canActivate` guard) completes without emitting and the application is already destroyed, the `catchError` block calls `overallTransitionState.resolve(false)` and returns `EMPTY` instead of running error handling that would invoke `runInInjectionContext` on a destroyed injector. Previously this threw `NG0205: Injector has already been destroyed` and produced an unhandled promise rejection (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e3da35ec749395239731158f89e29d47e7a9ef36, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59457)

**Affects:** Applications using router guards that return an observable/`Subject` which can complete without emitting a value (for example an in-flight HTTP request that completes after the application is destroyed) and that destroy the application while a navigation is in progress.

**Action:** none — the fix is internal to the router; no application code, configuration, or dependency must change. Re-verify that destroying the application mid-navigation no longer logs `NG0205: Injector has already been destroyed` or triggers an `unhandledrejection` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e3da35ec749395239731158f89e29d47e7a9ef36).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
catchError((e) => {
  // If the application is already destroyed, the catch block should not
  // execute anything in practice because other resources have already
  // been released and destroyed.
  if (this.destroyed) {
    overallTransitionState.resolve(false);
    return EMPTY;
  }

  errored = true;
  /* This error type is issued during Redirect, and is handled as a
   * cancellation rather than an error. */
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, the `@angular/service-worker` package's `peerDependencies` were modified: `@angular/common` was removed and `rxjs` was added as an explicit peer dependency with the range `"^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0"` (PR title/commit message: "build: remove `@angular/common` from Service Worker dependencies" and "fix(service-worker): add missing `rxjs` peer dependency") (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59747, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/522acbf3d7ed502e7802117776acda3529a9a2b4). The 19.0.0 `package.json` listed peerDependencies `@angular/core` and `@angular/common`; the 20.0.0 `package.json` lists `@angular/core` and `rxjs` (`"rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0"`), with only `tslib` remaining under `dependencies` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/service-worker/package.json, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/service-worker/package.json, https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59747.diff). Internally the Bazel `ng_module` deps for service-worker also dropped `//packages/common` (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59747.diff).

**Affects:** Applications that use `@angular/service-worker`; every Angular application normally already has `rxjs` as a direct dependency, so no resolution errors are expected in practice.

**Action:** none — no application-side change is required; `npm install` peer resolution behavior can be re-verified (any Angular app providing `rxjs` `^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0` satisfies the new peer dependency).

**Before/after:**
```json
// 19.0.0 @angular/service-worker package.json (peerDependencies)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/core": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "@angular/common": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER"
}

// 20.0.0
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/core": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0"
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler-cli HMR logic (`@angular/compiler-cli`, `extract_dependencies.ts`, `metadata.ts`, `handler.ts`) previously passed local dependencies to HMR replacement functions by reference. This caused runtime `ReferenceError` crashes when a `const enum` value was referenced in component metadata (e.g., `providers`), because `const enum`s are inlined and have no runtime representation. The compiler now evaluates `const enum` values using `PartialEvaluator` and passes them as inline object literals instead. If a runtime representation cannot be produced, the dev server falls back to a full page refresh instead of failing. Additionally, initializer-less enum members are now correctly evaluated based on their index rather than `undefined` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/53a4668b58b645e41baddc5b67d52ede21c8e945, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59800)
**Affects:** Applications using `const enum`s within Angular component/directive metadata (such as `@Component({ providers: [...] })`) while running the dev server with Hot Module Replacement (HMR) enabled (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59800)
**Action:** required: re-verify the HMR behavior in the dev server for components using `const enum` values in metadata, ensuring HMR updates apply correctly without crashing or unnecessary full-page refreshes.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Generated HMR replacement function data representation

// Before (broken - const enum has no runtime reference, passed by name)
ɵɵreplaceMetadata(Cmp, m.default, [i0], [token, Foo, Component]);

// After (fixed - const enum value inlined as an object literal)
ɵɵreplaceMetadata(Cmp, m.default, [i0], [token, { one: 0, two: "2", three: 3 }, Component]);
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` HMR update generator now handles `const enum` values that have no runtime representation. Previously, the generator passed used locals by reference, causing a `ReferenceError` (e.g., `Foo is not defined`) when a `const enum` was used in decorator metadata like `@Component providers`. The fix changes the generation strategy to pass values as an object literal instead of by reference. Additionally, the partial evaluator was fixed to interpret initializer-less enum members by their index instead of `undefined`. If a runtime representation still cannot be produced, the dev server now gracefully falls back to a full page refresh instead of crashing (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59815, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/53a4668b58b645e41baddc5b67d52ede21c8e945, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d7b5c597ffcb6469ae3f08a97e7790599d569cc4, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/976125e0b4cf4e7fb4621a7203e3f43b009885f0).
**Affects:** Applications using Hot Module Replacement (HMR) that reference `const enum` values inside component or directive decorators (such as the `providers` array) during development.
**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular < 20.0.0): HMR update function referenced const enums by name,
// causing a ReferenceError because they don't exist at runtime.

// After (Angular >= 20.0.0): HMR replacement function passes enum values as literals.
export function Component_Hmr_Update() {
  // Updated metadata is passed as a literal object instead of referencing
  // a const enum variable that is undefined at runtime.
  ɵɵreplaceMetadata(/* ... */);
}
```

**What changed:** In `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/partial_evaluator/src/interpreter.ts`, `StaticInterpreter.visitEnumDeclaration` now resolves an enum member that has no initializer to its positional `index` instead of leaving it `undefined` (`const resolved = member.initializer ? this.visit(member.initializer, context) : index;`), matching TypeScript semantics so `Foo.B` evaluates to `1` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/976125e0b4cf4e7fb4621a7203e3f43b009885f0.patch). The same PR #59815 changed HMR codegen: `extractHmrDependencies`/`getRuntimeRepresentation` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/hmr/src/extract_dependencies.ts` now return `o.Expression | null` / `null`, and `extractHmrMetatadata` in `hmr/src/metadata.ts` returns `null` when a `const enum` (no runtime representation) used in decorator metadata cannot be passed through; values are instead inlined as an object literal, and the dev server falls back to a full page refresh (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59815, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d7b5c597ffcb6469ae3f08a97e7790599d569cc4.patch). Fixes #59800 ("HMR breaks when using a const enum value inside decorator metadata", e.g. a `const enum` value in a `@Component` `providers` array crashed `ng serve` on HMR apply).

**Affects:** Applications that use `const enum`s / initializer-less enum members inside decorator metadata (e.g. `providers`) — mostly under `ng serve` with HMR (`--hmr`); build output is not otherwise changed by user code.

**Action:** none — this is an internal compiler-cli fix; no application code, config or dependency changes are required. Re-verify that `ng serve --hmr` no longer crashes when a `const enum` value appears in decorator metadata, and that metadata using initializer-less enum members now evaluates to the index value.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before: partial_evaluator/src/interpreter.ts
node.members.forEach((member) => {
  const name = this.stringNameFromPropertyName(member.name, context);
  if (name !== undefined) {
    const resolved = member.initializer && this.visit(member.initializer, context);
    map.set(name, new EnumValue(enumRef, name, resolved));
  }
});

// after:
node.members.forEach((member, index) => {
  const name = this.stringNameFromPropertyName(member.name, context);
  if (name !== undefined) {
    const resolved = member.initializer ? this.visit(member.initializer, context) : index;
    map.set(name, new EnumValue(enumRef, name, resolved));
  }
});
```

**What changed:** The `@for` block compiler now emits ops needed to evaluate the `track` expression inside the generated tracking function. Previously the expression was evaluated in the context of the creation block, so ops emitted for nullish coalescing (`??`) or safe (`?.`) reads generated temporary variables that were never defined in the tracking scope. At runtime this raised an error similar to `tmp_7_0 is not defined`, with generated code of the form `var _forTrack0 = ($index, $item) => (tmp_7_0 = $item.field1) !== null && tmp_7_0 !== void 0 ? tmp_7_0 : $item.field2;` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58520, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/56256).

**Affects:** Applications whose `@for` `track` expression uses the nullish coalescing operator (`??`), safe property access (`?.`), or any expression requiring a compiler-generated temporary variable.

**Action:** none. This is a compiler bug fix, not an API change; applications that previously crashed at runtime now render the `@for` block correctly.

**Before/after:**
```html
@for (item of myList; track item.field1 ?? item.field2) {
  {{ $index }}: {{ item.field1 }}, {{ item.field2 }}
}
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` `ComponentDecoratorHandler.resolveAllDeferredDependencies` logic (in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/component/src/handler.ts`) now de-duplicates the per-component set of deferred-block dependencies via a `seenDeps` `Set<ClassDeclaration>`. Previously, when a component had multiple `@defer` blocks referencing the same imported symbol, the compiler emitted `setClassMetadataAsync` / `ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadataAsync` calls whose `resolveMetadata` callback had multiple parameters with the same name, producing invalid code. This broke both full and partial (linker) compilation, but was most visible in partial compilation of libraries, where Babel fails with e.g. `Duplicate argument name "FooComponent"` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/dcfb9f1959164baf45f5f954b4bf681d650d8a2d, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59926, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59922).

**Affects:** Applications and especially publishable libraries (`ng-packagr`/partial compilation) whose component templates contain two or more `@defer` blocks importing the same component/directive (issue filed against Angular 19.1.5; a regression, so builds that previously worked in earlier versions are fixed, not broken, by 20.0.0) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59922).

**Action:** none — this is a compiler bug fix; no application code, config or dependency changes are required. Applications that previously worked around the build failure (e.g. by splitting shared deferred components across components) may re-verify those workarounds can be removed.

**Before/after:**
```js
// Generated output after the fix (de-duplicated callback params), from the
// compliance test golden file deferred_with_duplicate_external_dep_template.js
$r3$.ɵsetClassMetadataAsync(MyApp, () => [
  import("./deferred_with_duplicate_external_dep_lazy").then(m => m.DuplicateLazyDep),
  import("./deferred_with_duplicate_external_dep_other").then(m => m.OtherLazyDep)
], (DuplicateLazyDep, OtherLazyDep) => { /* ... */ });

// Before (Angular 19): DuplicateLazyDep appeared twice in the callback ->
// Babel: Duplicate argument name "FooComponent" (library build failure)
```

**What changed:** A bug fix in the control flow migration (`ng generate @angular/core:control-flow`) ensures correct usage detection for `CommonModule`. Previously, the migration removed `CommonModule` from component `imports` if usage was found only within `@let` declarations or pre-existing control flow blocks (e.g., `@if`) when migrating mixed templates. The fix adds `visitLetDeclaration` to the `CommonCollector` to account for `@let` declarations when determining used symbols. It also ensures dependencies within existing control flow nodes are counted. This affects the migration tool's behavior, not the runtime API, but fixes incorrect code generation that would cause runtime errors for directives/pipes like `async`, `date`, and `ngTemplateOutlet` (#59861, #710759ddcc0ecbad68deb20821b535fd5deb69c6, #46f36a58bf3a7b9131b6330e84d4adb3e73f3601).

**Affects:** Applications using the `ng generate @angular/core:control-flow` schematic on templates containing `@let` declarations, `@if`/`@for`/`@switch` blocks alongside structural directives (`*ngIf`, etc.), or mixed new/old control flow syntax.

**Action:** required: Re-verify that `CommonModule` and other `@angular/common` imports are correctly retained in `imports: []` arrays after running the migration, especially if they are used inside `@let` or existing new control flow blocks. If a prior migration incorrectly removed `CommonModule`, re-run the migration or manually restore the imports.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (incorrectly removes CommonModule if only used in @let or existing @if)
// imports: [] (CommonModule removed), causing runtime errors

// After (CommonModule retained if symbols are used)
import {CommonModule} from '@angular/common';
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  imports: [CommonModule],
  template: `@let foo = 123 | date; @if (foo) {<span>{{foo}}</span>}`,
})
class Comp {}
```

**What changed:** The `ng generate @angular/core:control-flow` migration schematic now traverses pre-existing new-style control flow blocks and `@let` declarations to count dependencies before deciding whether to strip `CommonModule` from a component's `imports: []`. Previously, the schematic did not check the content of these nodes for pipes/directives (e.g., `async`, `date`, `ngTemplateOutlet`) and incorrectly removed `CommonModule`, causing runtime errors for symbols still in use. The fix adds logic to `visitBlock` and `visitLetDeclaration` inside `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/control-flow-migration/types.ts`. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59861, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59846, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59861.diff)

**Affects:** Applications that previously ran the control flow migration and where the tool incorrectly removed `CommonModule` because dependencies were used inside existing `@if`/`@for`/`@switch` blocks or `@let` declarations.

**Action:** required: Re-verify applications previously migrated. If `CommonModule` was incorrectly removed in an earlier version, add it back to the `imports: []` array. After upgrading to 20.0.0, running the migration again (`ng generate @angular/core:control-flow`) should correctly retain `CommonModule` if dependencies like pipes are detected inside these new-style blocks.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {CommonModule} from '@angular/common';
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  imports: [CommonModule],
  template: `@if (toggle) {<div>{{ d | date }}</div>} <span *ngIf="toggle">hi</span>`
})
class Comp {
  toggle = false;
}
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/common/upgrade` (`packages/common/upgrade/src/location_shim.ts`), the `urlChanges` `ReplaySubject` is now completed when the AngularJS root scope is destroyed via `$rootScope.$on('$destroy', ...)`, which releases all active observers and fixes the leak that occurred when the internal `urlChanges.subscribe` callback captured `this` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e9f10eb4c950692992098619b9628ecefd1b36ce.patch). In Angular 20.0.x the `$destroy` handler also calls `this.removeOnUrlChangeFn()`, whereas in Angular 19.0.x the constructor calls `this.location.onUrlChange(...)` without saving/calling the returned deregister function (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/common/upgrade/src/location_shim.ts vs https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/packages/common/upgrade/src/location_shim.ts).
**Affects:** Applications that bootstrap `UpgradeModule` and use the `$location` shim (`$locationShim`) via `LocationUpgradeModule` / AngularJS hybrid routing, especially those that create and destroy the root scope or rely on location-change propagation after root-scope teardown.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// old
this.location.onUrlChange((newUrl, newState) => {
  this.urlChanges.next({newUrl, newState});
});

// new
$rootScope.$on('$destroy', () => {
  this.removeOnUrlChangeFn();
  this.urlChanges.complete();
});
```

**What changed:** The typing of `ɵRawValue` for a `FormRecord` was corrected to no longer wrap values in `Partial`, and the `setValue` method parameter was updated from `[key: string]: ɵValue<TControl>` to `[key: string]: ɵRawValue<TControl>` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ec1e4c3d9430f5ea4380252098d2b4b71d8a950f, https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/59993.diff). Previously, TypeScript inferred partial values incorrectly for `FormRecord` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59985).
**Affects:** Applications using the `FormRecord` class from `@angular/forms` with typed forms, specifically when calling `setValue` or accessing `ɵRawValue` types, which now strictly require all keys and values to be present.
**Action:** required: update calls to `setValue` on `FormRecord` instances to ensure they include all nested control values, as optional/partial value assignments are no longer permitted by the type system. Fix any TypeScript compilation errors related to `ɵRawValue` inference.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19)
setValue(
  value: {[key: string]: ɵValue<TControl>},
  options?: {
    onlySelf?: boolean;
    emitEvent?: boolean;
  }
): void;

// After (Angular 20)
setValue(
  value: {[key: string]: ɵRawValue<TControl>},
  options?: {
    onlySelf?: boolean;
    emitEvent?: boolean;
  }
