**What changed:** In Angular 19, `resource()`'s internal state (managed around `linkedSignal`) reported the wrong value for `previous.status` passed to the loader: on a resource's very first run, even when it transitioned immediately to `Loading`, `previous.status` was `Loading` (`ResourceStatus.Loading`) instead of `Idle`. The 20.0.0 fix (PR #59708) captures both fields in `loadEffect()` — `const {status: currentStatus, previousStatus} = untracked(this.state);` — so `previous.status` is `ResourceStatus.Idle` on first load and only `Loading`/`Reloading` on subsequent loads (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/127fc0dc847a4e8b62be36cdd980a067c4da974f, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59708). Note the surrounding API also changed in v20: `ResourceStatus` became the string union `'idle' | 'error' | 'loading' | 'reloading' | 'resolved' | 'local'`, and the loader receives `previous: {status}` via `ResourceLoaderParams` alongside `params` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/src/resource/api.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using the experimental `resource()` (from `@angular/core`) whose `loader`/`stream` function reads the `previous.status` parameter and branches on it (e.g. distinguishing a first load from a reload/error recovery).

**Action:** required: re-verify any loader logic that inspects `previous.status`. Code written against the 19 bug that treated `previous.status === 'loading'` as "this is the first load" must be re-checked; the correct first-load test on 20.0.0 is `previous.status === 'idle'`. Comparisons must also switch from the old enum members (`ResourceStatus.Loading`, `ResourceStatus.Idle`) to the v20 string statuses (`'loading'`, `'idle'`) shown in the v20 `ResourceStatus` type.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Angular 20.0.0 behavior (from resource_spec.ts added by the fix):
// the loader now sees previous.status === 'idle' on the first run
resource({
  request: ...,
  loader: async ({previous}) => {
    prevStatus = previous.status;   // 'idle' on first load (was 'loading' in 19.x)
    return true;
  },
});
expect(prevStatus).toBe(ResourceStatus.Idle);
```

**What changed:** In v19, `resource()` was refactored onto `linkedSignal()`, which introduced a race where a request change during the loading phase could be dropped and the resource would show stale data or get stuck: while loading, the request changes; before the internal `effect()` runs, the previous load's `Promise` resolves and moves the resource out of the loading state, so the effect's check bails out (issue #59842). PR #59851 (commit b592b1b051, Angular 20.0.0) fixed this by tracking the request itself inside the resource state: the state signal (`ResourceState`, now including `extRequest: WrappedRequest` with `{request, reload}`) is a `linkedSignal` whose computation always enters `Loading` (or `Idle`) on request change, and `loadEffect()` now ignores resolved values or streams for stale requests (`if (abortSignal.aborted || untracked(this.extRequest) !== extRequest) { return; }`), replacing the old, too-strict status check in v19's `loadEffect` (`currentStatus !== ResourceStatus.Loading && currentStatus !== ResourceStatus.Reloading`). The public API of `resource()` is unchanged (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59851, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59842, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b592b1b0516786c52c7d0638c4e7545b0de8a545).

**Affects:** Applications using the experimental `resource()` (or `rxResource`/`httpResource`) API from `@angular/core`, particularly where the `request` can change while the resource is still in a loading state, and especially chained resources (one resource depending on another's output) in tests, where the race is easiest to trigger.

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix with no API or configuration change; applications using `resource()` should simply re-verify that resources now update correctly when the request changes mid-load (stale results from the previous request are now discarded rather than applied).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v20.0.0 (after fix) — loadEffect ignores resolutions of stale requests:
// https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b592b1b0516786c52c7d0638c4e7545b0de8a545
const stream = await untracked(() => {
  return this.loaderFn({request: extRequest.request as Exclude<R, undefined>, abortSignal, previous: {status: previousStatus}});
});

// If this request has been aborted, or the current request no longer
// matches this load, then we should ignore this resolution.
if (abortSignal.aborted || untracked(this.extRequest) !== extRequest) {
  return;
}
```
(v19 only checked `if (abortSignal.aborted) { return; }` after the loader, and used a strict status check that could drop the new request.)

**What changed:** In `@angular/core`, `ApplicationRef._tick` was restructured so the change-detection tracing snapshot is no longer cleared at the *start* of change detection but only after it finishes. Previously the snapshot was taken from `this.tracingSnapshot`, set to `null` immediately, then executed via `snapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick)` followed by `snapshot.dispose()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a299e02e9141cdc4d74185deb58308fa010bb36e). Now `_tick()` runs `this.tracingSnapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this.tickImpl)` (or `this.tickImpl()` when no snapshot exists), and the new private `tickImpl` clears it in its `finally` block: `this.tracingSnapshot?.dispose(); this.tracingSnapshot = null;` — alongside `setActiveConsumer(prevConsumer)` and `this.afterTick.next()`. Per the PR, "This change waits until the end of `tick` to clear the tracing snapshot. This ensures that if we notify the scheduler during `tick` the correct tracing snapshot is used" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59796). Involved internal symbols: `TracingService`, `TracingSnapshot`, `TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION` from `application/tracing`, `EffectScheduler`, `profiler`/`ProfilerEvent` (all in `packages/core/src/application/application_ref.ts`).

**Affects:** Only applications using Angular's public tracing API (`TracingService` / `provideExperimentALCheckNoChanges...`-style experimental tracing hooks, i.e. custom `TracingSnapshot` implementations) — during change detection, `snapshot.dispose()` is now deferred until the tick completes, so a snapshot may still be the active `ApplicationRef.tracingSnapshot` when the effect `EffectScheduler` is notified. The change touched only internal files (12 insertions, 11 deletions in `application_ref.ts`) and is not listed in the v20 release notes breaking changes (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Action:** none — no code, configuration, or dependency change is required. Applications implementing a custom `TracingService`/`TracingSnapshot` should merely re-verify that reference counting / `dispose()` behaviour still works, since `dispose()` is now called by Angular after the tick rather than before it.

**Before/after:**
```diff
-  _tick = (): void => {
+  _tick(): void {
     profiler(ProfilerEvent.ChangeDetectionStart);

     if (this.tracingSnapshot !== null) {
-      const snapshot = this.tracingSnapshot;
-      this.tracingSnapshot = null;
-
-      snapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this._tick);
-      snapshot.dispose();
-      return;
+      this.tracingSnapshot.run(TracingAction.CHANGE_DETECTION, this.tickImpl);
+    } else {
+      this.tickImpl();
     }
+  }

+  private tickImpl = (): void => {
     ...
     } finally {
       this._runningTick = false;
+      this.tracingSnapshot?.dispose();
+      this.tracingSnapshot = null;
       setActiveConsumer(prevConsumer);
       this.afterTick.next();
```

**What changed:** `Validators.required`, `Validators.minLength`, and `Validators.maxLength` now operate correctly when the bound control value is a `Set` (a type exposing `size` instead of `length`). Previously these validators did not work with `Set`s because they relied on `length` (commit fa0c3e3210, PR #45793) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fa0c3e3210885a36e5c9e9eb76e821032f5cd215, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/45793). The implementation introduces a `lengthOrSize` helper that returns `value.length` for arrays and strings, `value.size` for `value instanceof Set`, and `null` otherwise; `isEmptyInputValue`, `minLengthValidator`, and `maxLengthValidator` read it via `control.value?.length ?? lengthOrSize(control.value)` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/fa0c3e3210885a36e5c9e9eb76e821032f5cd215/packages/forms/src/validators.ts). The API docs now state `minLength`/`maxLength` "are intended to be used only for types that have a numeric `length` or `size` property, such as strings, arrays or sets" (https://angular.dev/api/forms/Validators). The PR was tagged `target: minor` and explicitly `breaking change: No` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/45793).

**Affects:** Applications that bind a `Set` (or any object exposing only `size`) to a `FormControl` and apply `Validators.required`, `Validators.minLength`, or `Validators.maxLength`.

**Action:** none — the PR records no breaking change; existing `Validators.required`, `Validators.minLength`, and `Validators.maxLength` calls are unchanged, and validation now passes for `Set` values. Re-verify form validation behaviour for `FormControl`s whose value is a `Set`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// After (packages/forms/src/validators.ts)
function lengthOrSize(value: unknown): number | null {
  if (value == null) {
    return null;
  } else if (Array.isArray(value) || typeof value === 'string') {
    return value.length;
  } else if (value instanceof Set) {
    return value.size;
  }
  return null;
}

export function maxLengthValidator(maxLength: number): ValidatorFn {
  return (control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null => {
    const length = control.value?.length ?? lengthOrSize(control.value);
    if (length !== null && length > maxLength) {
      return {'maxlength': {'requiredLength': maxLength, 'actualLength': length}};
    }
    return null;
  };
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, `@angular/platform-server` no longer depends on `@angular/platform-browser/animations` or `@angular/animations`. `ServerModule` and `ServerTestingModule` dropped their `imports: [NoopAnimationsModule]`, and `provideServerRendering()` no longer calls `provideNoopAnimations()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fc5d187da5e8895d60caa35b7b59e234998eddf0.patch). `@angular/platform-server`'s `peerDependencies` no longer list `@angular/animations` (same patch). Instead, `@angular/platform-browser` animations now detect the server via the global `ngServerMode` flag (set by `provideServerRendering`/platform-server bootstrap): `provideAnimations()` resolves `AnimationDriver` to `NoopAnimationDriver` and `ANIMATION_MODULE_TYPE` to `'NoopAnimations'` on the server, and `provideAnimationsAsync()` forces `type = 'noop'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e6cb411e4393a4b1f5852d3d7c5b9622504399b1.patch). Previously `BROWSER_ANIMATIONS_PROVIDERS` always used `WebAnimationsDriver`/`'BrowserAnimations'` (same patch). PR: https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59762.

**Affects:** Applications using SSR (`@angular/platform-server`, `ServerModule`, `ServerTestingModule`, `provideServerRendering()`) together with `@angular/animations` — `BrowserAnimationsModule`, `NoopAnimationsModule`, `provideAnimations()`, `provideAnimationsAsync()`, or code reading `ANIMATION_MODULE_TYPE`; also apps relying on platform-server pulling in `@angular/animations` transitively.

**Action:** required: re-verify that animations are still no-ops during server rendering (unchanged behaviour is expected, now automatic); if build tooling or `package.json` depended on `@angular/animations` being a transitive peer of `@angular/platform-server`, add `@angular/animations` explicitly; for unit tests using `ServerTestingModule` that relied on the implicit noop animations module, import a animations provider explicitly.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// platform-server (v19) -> (v20): provide_server.ts
- return makeEnvironmentProviders([provideNoopAnimations(), ...PLATFORM_SERVER_PROVIDERS]);
+ return makeEnvironmentProviders([...PLATFORM_SERVER_PROVIDERS]);

// platform-server (v19): @NgModule({exports: [BrowserModule], imports: [NoopAnimationsModule], ...})
// platform-server (v20): @NgModule({exports: [BrowserModule], providers: PLATFORM_SERVER_PROVIDERS})
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/common/upgrade`, the `$locationShim` class (the drop-in `$location` service for hybrid AngularJS apps) previously registered a URL-change listener via `this.location.onUrlChange(...)` in its constructor and never unregistered it; only the internal `urlChanges` `ReplaySubject` was completed on `$rootScope.$on('$destroy', ...)`. Angular 20 now stores the unregister function returned by `Location.onUrlChange` (typed `private readonly removeOnUrlChangeFn: VoidFunction;`) and calls it inside the `$rootScope.$on('$destroy', ...)` handler, before `this.urlChanges.complete()` (`packages/common/upgrade/src/location_shim.ts`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c2de5f68b3d3cd8a0c43b3c4325a1a1db874e132, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60004, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/common/upgrade/src/location_shim.ts). `Location.onUrlChange` returns "A function that, when executed, unregisters a URL change listener" ().

**Affects:** Hybrid apps using `UpgradeModule` / `@angular/common/upgrade` with the `$locationShim` (`$location`) service, particularly ones that destroy and re-create the AngularJS root scope (e.g. multiple unmount/mount cycles of the downgraded app). No public API signature or property key changed.

**Action:** none — internal memory-leak fix; no code, config or dependency change required. Re-verify that `$locationChangeStart`/`$locationChangeSuccess` and `$location.onChange` behaviour is unchanged, and that the AngularJS root scope is actually destroyed if code relied on the old listener staying registered.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
this.location.onUrlChange((newUrl, newState) => {
  this.urlChanges.next({newUrl, newState});
});
// after
this.removeOnUrlChangeFn = this.location.onUrlChange((newUrl, newState) => {
  this.urlChanges.next({newUrl, newState});
});

$rootScope.$on('$destroy', () => {
  this.removeOnUrlChangeFn();
  this.urlChanges.complete();
});
```

**What changed:** Internal `compiler-cli` fix (#60152, commit `1dd94476b35d08e753766b1d0c5d8af5faa017a9`) to how template/type-check IDs (`tcb1`, `tcb2`, …) are allocated during incremental compilations. Previously `getTemplateId` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/diagnostics/src/id.ts` stored the ID directly on the declaration node via `Symbol('ngTemplateId')` and kept a running counter `Symbol('ngNextTemplateId')` on the `ts.SourceFile`; when a source file changed, a new `ts.SourceFile` object was created but retained (cached) nodes still carried their old `TEMPLATE_ID` symbol, so the "next ID" counter was lost and newly created nodes could be handed IDs already in use, producing ID collisions across incremental rebuilds (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1dd94476b35d08e753766b1d0c5d8af5faa017a9.patch). The fix replaces the per-node symbol plus counter with a per-source-file `Map<ts.Node, TemplateId>` stored under `Symbol('ngTemplateId')`, so IDs stay unique for as long as the map is retained. In the shipped 20.0.x source this code was further renamed: `TemplateId` → `TypeCheckId`, `getTemplateId` → `getTypeCheckId`, `Symbol('ngTemplateId')` → `Symbol('TypeCheckId')` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/diagnostics/src/id.ts vs https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/diagnostics/src/id.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using incremental builds (`ng build`, `ng serve`, watch mode, or the `ngtsc` program APIs) where the code previously read these internal symbols; no public API, template syntax, or configuration is involved.

**Action:** none — internal bug fix only; optionally re-verify that incremental rebuilds no longer surface stale/duplicate `tcb…` type-check IDs in template diagnostics.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// 19.0.x (old): per-node symbol + per-source-file counter
const TEMPLATE_ID = Symbol('ngTemplateId');
const NEXT_TEMPLATE_ID = Symbol('ngNextTemplateId');
function allocateTemplateId(sf: ts.SourceFile & Partial<HasNextTemplateId>): TemplateId {
  if (sf[NEXT_TEMPLATE_ID] === undefined) {
    sf[NEXT_TEMPLATE_ID] = 1;
  }
  return `tcb${sf[NEXT_TEMPLATE_ID]!++}` as TemplateId;
}

// 20.0.x (new): per-source-file node -> id map
const TYPE_CHECK_ID_MAP = Symbol('TypeCheckId');
if (sf[TYPE_CHECK_ID_MAP].get(clazz) === undefined) {
  sf[TYPE_CHECK_ID_MAP].set(clazz, `tcb${sf[TYPE_CHECK_ID_MAP].size + 1}` as TypeCheckId);
}
```

**What changed:**
- `packages/core/src/render3/component_ref.ts` (`@angular/core`) adds private cache fields on `ComponentFactory<T>`: `private cachedInputs: { propName: string; templateName: string; isSignal: boolean; transform?: (value: any) => any; }[] | null = null;` and `private cachedOutputs: { propName: string; templateName: string; }[] | null = null;` (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1b3b05bf7294963aa50f83b726db620b250b1a5c.diff>)
- The getters change from recomputing every access (`return toInputRefArray(this.componentDef.inputs);` / `return toOutputRefArray(this.componentDef.outputs);`) to assigning once and returning the cached array (`this.cachedInputs ??= toInputRefArray(this.componentDef.inputs); return this.cachedInputs;` / `this.cachedOutputs ??= toOutputRefArray(this.componentDef.outputs); return this.cachedOutputs;`) (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1b3b05bf7294963aa50f83b726db620b250b1a5c.diff>)
- Rationale: “The set of inputs and outputs of a component is static, but the getter for the `inputs` and `outputs` property was re-computing them every time which the user might not expect.” (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60156>)

**Affects:**
- Code that reads `inputs` / `outputs` off an `@angular/core` component factory (e.g. via `ComponentFactory`/`ComponentFactoryResolver` in `@angular/core`) and assumes every getter call returns a *new* array instance (for example mutating the returned array in place, relying on array-identity changes, or expecting a fresh `propName`/`templateName`/`isSignal`/`transform` object on each access). (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1b3b05bf7294963aa50f83b726db620b250b1a5c.diff>)

**Action:**
- Required: re-verify any code that reads `inputs` or `outputs` from the component factory and mutates the returned array or depends on the returned value’s reference identity. If mutation is currently relied on, it must stop, because from now on the returned array is the same cached instance for the factory (`cachedInputs` / `cachedOutputs` only populated once). No dependency coordinates, build configuration, or code identifiers are shown as needing changes in the PR description or commit diff; the PR is labelled as a patch-target fix and the 20.0.0 CHANGELOG does not list this commit under “Breaking Changes”. (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60156>, <https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1b3b05bf7294963aa50f83b726db620b250b1a5c>)

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (packages/core/src/render3/component_ref.ts)
override get inputs(): {
  propName: string;
  templateName: string;
  isSignal: boolean;
  transform?: (value: any) => any;
}[] {
  return toInputRefArray(this.componentDef.inputs);
}

override get outputs(): { propName: string; templateName: string; }[] {
  return toOutputRefArray(this.componentDef.outputs);
}

// After
private cachedInputs:
  | { propName: string; templateName: string; isSignal: boolean;
      transform?: (value: any) => any; }[]
  | null = null;
private cachedOutputs: { propName: string; templateName: string; }[] | null = null;

override get inputs(): {
  propName: string; templateName: string; isSignal: boolean;
  transform?: (value: any) => any;
}[] {
  this.cachedInputs ??= toInputRefArray(this.componentDef.inputs);
  return this.cachedInputs;
}

override get outputs(): { propName: string; templateName: string; }[] {
  this.cachedOutputs ??= toOutputRefArray(this.componentDef.outputs);
  return this.cachedOutputs;
}
```

**What changed:** In event replay during hydration, Angular now skips invoking replay listeners when the target element is no longer connected to the DOM. The internal `invokeListeners` function in `packages/core/src/event_delegation_utils.ts` adds a guard `!currentTarget?.isConnected` before invoking handlers stored in `__jsaction_fns`, and the previously exported `invokeRegisteredDelegationListeners` helper is removed from that module. Previously, events captured via `withEventReplay()` were replayed on nodes that had been destroyed (e.g., by a click triggering router navigation before hydration completed), which could cause components to emit to destroyed `OutputRef`s, throwing `NG0953` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59841). The fix prevents the replay from happening on disconnected nodes (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/330c24aed92e4b916fb7ac6a91fb17c3ae728869). The PR was merged as a patch-level core fix and is marked as not a breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60103).

**Affects:** Applications using `withEventReplay()` in SSR with hydration, where events may be replayed on elements that get removed/destroyed before hydration finishes. No impact on applications without event replay enabled.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/core/src/event_delegation_utils.ts
export function invokeListeners(event: Event, currentTarget: Element | null) {
  const handlerFns = currentTarget?.__jsaction_fns?.get(event.type);
  // Before: if (!handlerFns) { return; }
  if (!handlerFns || !currentTarget?.isConnected) {
    return;
  }
  for (const handler of handlerFns) {
    // ...
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular's event replay feature (`withEventReplay()` from `@angular/platform-browser`, used with SSR hydration) previously assumed every node passed to the internal `stashEventListener` stash function was an `Element`. When a directive with a `@HostListener` (e.g. `document:click`, `window:click`, `body:click`) was applied to an `<ng-container>`, the host native node is a comment node, and event replay code in `packages/core/src/hydration/event_replay.ts` threw during hydration (issue #60070) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60070, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60130). The fix in `packages/core/src/hydration/event_replay.ts` widens the stash function parameter from `RElement` to `RNode` and returns early when `(rEl as Node).nodeType !== Node.ELEMENT_NODE`, so stashing (`sharedStashFunction`/`sharedMapFunction` in `packages/core/src/event_delegation_utils.ts`) is skipped for comment nodes; `stashEventListener` in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/listener.ts` also now takes `RNode` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cfad089cc359cddf08d462662d2a6bf3acab9b22). The PR is marked non-breaking, `target: patch` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60130).

**Affects:** Applications using SSR hydration with `withEventReplay()` that have directives with `@HostListener` on global targets (`document:`, `window:`, `body:`) attached to `<ng-container>` hosts; previously these threw during hydration with event replay enabled.

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix; applications only need to re-verify that hydration with `withEventReplay()` no longer errors in the affected scenario.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// event_replay.ts — before
setStashFn((rEl: RElement, eventName: string, listenerFn: VoidFunction) => {
  sharedStashFunction(rEl, eventName, listenerFn);
  sharedMapFunction(rEl, jsActionMap);
});

// event_replay.ts — after
setStashFn((rEl: RNode, eventName: string, listenerFn: VoidFunction) => {
  // host listener on ng-container => comment node; skip stashing
  if ((rEl as Node).nodeType !== Node.ELEMENT_NODE) return;
  sharedStashFunction(rEl as RElement, eventName, listenerFn);
  sharedMapFunction(rEl as RElement, jsActionMap);
});
```

**What changed:** In `packages/language-service/src/quick_info.ts`, `QuickInfoBuilder` previously used `getDocumentationFromTypeDefAtLocation(...)`, which only returned `this.tsLS.getQuickInfoAtPosition(...)?.documentation` and silently dropped the `ts.QuickInfo` `tags` array coming from the TypeScript type definition. The method was renamed to `getQuickInfoFromTypeDefAtLocation(...)` and now returns the full `ts.QuickInfo | undefined`, so `getQuickInfoForVariableSymbol`, `getQuickInfoForLetDeclarationSymbol`, `getQuickInfoForReferenceSymbol` and the component/directive quick info path pass both `info?.documentation` and `info?.tags` to `createQuickInfo`. As a result, JSDoc tags — for example `@deprecated use the new thing` — are now surfaced in the `tags` of the `ts.QuickInfo` returned by the Angular Language Service for template symbols (variables, `@let` declarations, references, components and directives) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3f0116607dc3ad7e31cb4d895a56094f77c82f5d, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59524).

**Affects:** Editors/IDE integrations and tooling that consume Angular Language Service quick info (`QuickInfo` `tags`) for template positions; no changes to application templates, components or build configuration. Internal consumers of the private `QuickInfoBuilder` helper are the only code-level touch point, and it is private.

**Action:** none — behavior-only improvement in the language service; editor consumers will simply start receiving deprecation/other JSDoc tags in quick info tooltips.

