```ts
-  private getDocumentationFromTypeDefAtLocation(
-    tcbLocation: TcbLocation,
-  ): ts.SymbolDisplayPart[] | undefined {
+  private getQuickInfoFromTypeDefAtLocation(tcbLocation: TcbLocation): ts.QuickInfo | undefined {
     ...
-    return this.tsLS.getQuickInfoAtPosition(typeDefs[0].fileName, typeDefs[0].textSpan.start)
-      ?.documentation;
+    return this.tsLS.getQuickInfoAtPosition(typeDefs[0].fileName, typeDefs[0].textSpan.start);
   }
```

**What changed:** In `httpResource` (from `@angular/common/http`, experimental since 19.2), the `equal` option of `HttpResourceOptions` — the equality function (`ValueEqualityFn`) used to compare the resource's value, as in `resource()`'s `equal` option — was silently dropped: `HttpResourceImpl` in `packages/common/http/src/resource.ts` passed `undefined` instead of `equal` to the underlying `ɵResourceImpl` constructor, so custom equality logic never ran and the `value` signal always updated with the default `===` check (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60026, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/90a16a10888eee37d8a61cdbfad070e002a3cfdf.patch). The fix threads `options?.equal as ValueEqualityFn<unknown>` through `makeHttpResourceFn` into the `HttpResourceImpl` constructor and on to `super(...)`, so `equal` now governs whether new loader results (and `value.set` writes) replace the current value.

**Affects:** Applications passing `equal` in the options object of `httpResource()` (including `httpResource.text()`, `.blob()`, `.arrayBuffer()`), relying on it to suppress value updates.

**Action:** none — code needs no change; re-verify that `httpResource` value signals no longer update when `equal` reports the new response equal to the previous one (previously they always did), since components/effects depending on `value()` may now update less often.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// From the fix's test (packages/common/http/test/resource_spec.ts) — now effective in v20:
const res = httpResource<number>('/data', {
  injector: TestBed.inject(Injector),
  equal: (_a, _b) => true,
});
// ...
res.value.set(5);
expect(res.value()).toBe(1); // equality blocked writes
```

**What changed:** The Angular expression parser previously produced zero-length `span`/`sourceSpan` values for `TemplateLiteral` and `TemplateLiteralElement` AST nodes. In `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts`, `parseNoInterpolationTemplateLiteral(start)` now takes no argument and derives `start` from `this.inputIndex`, so a template literal with no interpolation (e.g. `` `abc` ``) gets a span covering the whole literal instead of a zero-length span at its start (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/56b551d273a0978e1f2e2ef914c1d7ae942a28a8). Inside `parseTemplateLiteral`, each `TemplateLiteralElement` is now built with `this.span(partStart)`/`this.sourceSpan(partStart)` captured before `this.advance()` (and `this.advance()` moved before the `elements.push(...)`), so the element's span covers the cooked text between `${...}` blocks rather than being empty. Before, tests asserted `['before ', '']`; they now assert `['before ', '`before ']` and `[' after', ' after`']` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60323). This fixes #60320 ("Wrong location information in `TemplateLiteralElement`") and #60319 ("Wrong location information when parsing `TemplateLiteral`"), both reported against `@angular/compiler` 19.2.1 by the author of `angular-estree-parser`, who had worked around the bad spans by locating elements via sibling expressions (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60320, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60319).

**Affects:** Only code that consumes `@angular/compiler` parser output directly — e.g. `new Parser(new Lexer()).parseBinding(...)` / `parseAction(...)` and then reads `span`/`start`/`end` or `sourceSpan` on `TemplateLiteral` and `TemplateLiteralElement` nodes (tooling: Prettier formatters, linters, code mods). Ordinary applications whose template expressions happen to contain template literals are not affected; runtime evaluation and generated code are unchanged (the change is internal to `parser.ts`).

**Action:** none — for applications; third-party tooling relying on the previously-empty spans must re-verify that it now receives real spans and can drop its workarounds.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts
-  private parseNoInterpolationTemplateLiteral(start: number): AST {
+  private parseNoInterpolationTemplateLiteral(): AST {
     const text = this.next.strValue;
+    const start = this.inputIndex;

       if (token.isTemplateLiteralPart() || token.isTemplateLiteralEnd()) {
+        const partStart = this.inputIndex;
+        this.advance();
         elements.push(
           new TemplateLiteralElement(
-            this.span(this.inputIndex),
-            this.sourceSpan(this.inputIndex),
+            this.span(partStart),
+            this.sourceSpan(partStart),
             token.strValue,
           ),
         );
-        this.advance();
```

**What changed:** Angular's `withEventReplay()` (from `@angular/platform-browser`, used via `hydrate(..., {hydrationFeatures: () => [withEventReplay()]})`) previously deferred event-replay setup until `appRef.whenStable()` resolved and then called `injector.get(JSACTION_EVENT_CONTRACT)` and `injector.get(JSACTION_BLOCK_ELEMENT_MAP)`, where the injector was obtained via `inject(Injector)`. If the application was destroyed before it became stable (e.g. a pending task such as an unresolved HTTP request kept it unstable), the injector was already destroyed and `injector.get` threw an "Injector has already been destroyed" error. The fix adds `if (appRef.destroyed) { return; }` inside the `appRef.whenStable().then(...)` callback in `packages/core/src/hydration/event_replay.ts`, and obtains the injector via `const appRef = inject(ApplicationRef); const {injector} = appRef;` instead of `inject(Injector)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59789, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/6dc41265fd47df3fd0462bcd8f451eb6aea972ef).

**Affects:** Applications using SSR hydration with `withEventReplay()` where the app can be destroyed before becoming stable; the internal error path no longer occurs. No public API changed.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before
const injector = inject(Injector);
const appRef = injector.get(ApplicationRef);
// ...
appRef.whenStable().then(() => {
  const eventContractDetails = injector.get(JSACTION_EVENT_CONTRACT);

// after
const appRef = inject(ApplicationRef);
const {injector} = appRef;
// ...
appRef.whenStable().then(() => {
  if (appRef.destroyed) {
    return;
  }
  const eventContractDetails = injector.get(JSACTION_EVENT_CONTRACT);
```

**What changed:** Before Angular 20, defer blocks using `hydrate on immediate` (and in some cases `hydrate on viewport`) inside a routed component — eager or `loadComponent`-lazy — were never hydrated with incremental hydration enabled (`withIncrementalHydration()`), because the trigger fired before the route resolved and the topmost parent defer block was not yet in the `DehydratedBlockRegistry` (issue #59997: "Immediate ko", "Viewport ko" for routed components) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59997). The fix in `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts` splits `triggerHydrationFromBlockName` and adds `triggerHydrationForBlockQueue`: if the topmost block of the hydration queue is not yet in the registry, hydration is queued via a new `DehydratedBlockRegistry.awaitParentBlock(topmostParentBlock, callback)` in `packages/core/src/defer/registry.ts`; when `add(blockId, info)` later registers that block, the awaiting callbacks fire and hydration proceeds. The affected triggers await app stability (a `PendingTasksInternal` task is registered) before initializing (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60203, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/bb12b30d5213912f50f53aff60a11e6d47349c82). The PR is marked "target: patch", not a breaking change.

**Affects:** Applications using incremental hydration (`withIncrementalHydration()`) with SSR and `@defer` blocks with `hydrate on immediate` (or `hydrate on viewport`) inside router-rendered components, especially lazy routes via `loadComponent`/`loadChildren`.

**Action:** none — internal `@angular/core` hydration fix; no code, config, or dependency changes required. Re-verify that previously non-hydrating `hydrate on immediate` defer blocks in routed components now hydrate after upgrade.

**Before/after:**
