**What changed:** In `@angular/compiler-cli`, `validateAndFlattenComponentImports` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/component/src/util.ts` previously reported the "wrong type in `imports`" diagnostic (created via `createValueHasWrongTypeError(expr, imports, errorMessage)`, `ErrorCode.VALUE_HAS_WRONG_TYPE` = 1010 / `COMPONENT_UNKNOWN_IMPORT` = 2012) on the *entire* `imports` array expression. It now resolves the offending entry: when the value is a `DynamicValue`, the diagnostic node is `ref.node`; when `expr` is a `ts.ArrayLiteralExpression` whose `elements.length === imports.length`, contains no `ts.isSpreadAssignment` and no nested arrays, the diagnostic is placed on `expr.elements[i]` (the exact array element). Otherwise it falls back to the previous behaviour of highlighting the whole array. Message text is unchanged, e.g. `'imports' must be an array of components, directives, pipes, or NgModules.` plus `Value is of type '{}'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/aa8ea7a5b227913e3f15270dac48479481c47f9a.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60455).

**Affects:** Only editor/CLI diagnostics and language-service squiggle ranges for components with an invalid entry in `@Component`/`@Directive` `imports`. No emitted code, API, or public error code changes.

**Action:** none.

**Before/after:**
```ts
@Component({
  template: '<div hello></div>',
  imports: [
    someVar,   // Angular 20: diagnostic highlights this element only
    HelloDir,
  ]
})
export class TestCmp {}
```

I have enough facts from the PR, commit diff, and issue. Writing the answer now.

**What changed:** The internal `TimerScheduler` in `@angular/core` (file `packages/core/src/defer/timer_scheduler.ts`), which backs the `@defer (on timer(...))` trigger as well as the `minimum`/`after` time values on `@placeholder` and `@loading`, no longer schedules its `setTimeout` from inside the Angular zone. `scheduleTimerTrigger(delay, callback, injector)` now resolves `NgZone` from the injector and calls `scheduler.add(delay, callback, ngZone)`; `scheduleTimer(ngZone)` wraps the timer creation in `ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() => setTimeout(() => ngZone.run(callback), timeout))` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/296aded9daaf04edd3cda623a220cbd2bf57e0f1.diff). Previously the `setTimeout` was registered as a macrotask tracked by `Zone.js`, so a pending defer timer kept `ApplicationRef.isStable`/`whenStable()` falsy for the whole delay — e.g. SSR/hydration cleanup was blocked until a long `@placeholder (minimum 20s)` finished, showing both server and browser markup (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60373). The PR marks this as non-breaking and notes it also affects `minimum`/`after` values in placeholder and loading templates, since they share the same `TimerScheduler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60392).

**Affects:** Applications that use `@defer` blocks with `on timer(...)`, or with `@placeholder (on minimum|after ...)` / `@loading (on minimum|after ...)` timings, and that observe app stability during that delay (`ApplicationRef.isStable`, `whenStable()`, SSR/hydration stability wait in `@angular/ssr`/`platform-server`, e2e or unit tests that wait for staleness).

**Action:** none — no code, template, dependency or configuration change is required; the change only makes the app reach a stable state sooner. Re-verify behaviour that intentionally relied on the app staying unstable while a defer timer was pending (e.g. SSR rendering gated on `whenStable()`, hydration of deferrable views, or tests that awaited the defer delay before considering the app stable).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/defer/timer_scheduler.ts (v19: timer scheduled inside the zone)
this.timeoutId = setTimeout(callback, timeout) as unknown as number;

// v20 (fix #60392): timer created outside the zone, callback re-entered via ngZone.run
this.timeoutId = ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
  return setTimeout(() => ngZone.run(callback), timeout) as unknown as number;
});
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core`, the runtime error thrown when a required signal input (`input.required()` from `createInputSignal` in `packages/core/src/authoring/input/input_signal.ts`) is read before a value is bound — `RuntimeError` with `RuntimeErrorCode.REQUIRED_INPUT_NO_VALUE` (NG0950) — now includes the input's name. The message is built as `Input${name ? ` "${name}"` : ''} is required but no value is available yet.` where `name` is `options?.debugName ?? options?.alias`, so e.g. `NG0950: Input "mySignal" is required but no value is available yet.` instead of the old plain `NG0950: Input is required but no value is available yet.` The message is only populated in dev mode (`ngDevMode`); the error code and thrown error type are unchanged (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0615ffb4f7a41cca2a408419830411897d1826e6, confirmed in the 20.0.x source at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/src/authoring/input/input_signal.ts). Fixes #60199 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60404).

**Affects:** Applications that read an unset required signal input and match on the exact NG0950 error message text (e.g. `toThrowError('Input is required but no value is available yet.')` in tests); the message gains ` "<debugName or alias>"` when the input was declared with `debugName` or `alias` in its `InputOptions`.

**Action:** required: re-verify any code or tests that assert on the NG0950 error message string (https://angular.dev/errors/NG0950); if the input declares `debugName` or `alias`, expect the name to appear quoted in the message.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Angular 19: NG0950 message
'Input is required but no value is available yet.'

// Angular 20 (packages/core/src/authoring/input/input_signal.ts):
const name = options?.debugName ?? options?.alias;
message = `Input${name ? ` "${name}"` : ''} is required but no value is available yet.`;

// Test expectation in Angular 20:
expect(() => signal()).toThrowError(
  /Input "mySignal" is required but no value is available yet\./,
);
```

**What changed:** In v20, APF bundling turned on Rollup tree-shaking for FESM bundles, which re-ordered imports inside the FESM bundles of `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic`. That surfaced that the package had historically relied on "accidentally" triggering the side effects of `@angular/compiler` registering itself globally; in affected custom setups, `@angular/common` (with its JIT decorators of directives/services) ran before the compiler was loaded, breaking JIT bootstrap. The fix (commit 1e06c8e, PR #60458) adds an explicit `import '@angular/compiler';` to `public_api.ts` of `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` and `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`, and changes the package's `package.json` from `"sideEffects": false` to `"sideEffects": ["./fesm2022/platform-browser-dynamic.mjs", "./fesm2022/testing.mjs"]`, plus `side_effect_entry_points` in its Bazel `ng_package` rule, so Rollup cannot swap the import order (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1e06c8e8b6473a19584ceb66945fd435ea4e6af9, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60458).

**Affects:** JIT applications using `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` in non-CLI/custom build setups (e.g. Analog). The PR explicitly notes "CLI users were not affected by this. This is only for custom setups" — the CLI wires up `@angular/compiler` before `-dynamic` is loaded (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60458).

**Action:** none — the fix is inside the shipped `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` v20 package; custom JIT setups should re-verify that JIT bootstrap works after upgrading (previously 19.0 pre-release bundles with reordered imports could throw "No component factory specified"/compiler-not-loaded style errors).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/platform-browser-dynamic/public_api.ts (after fix, v20)
// Note: Historically people relied on `platform-browser-dynamic` magically
// exposing the compiler for JIT. This is now made more explicit via this import.
import '@angular/compiler';

export * from './src/platform-browser-dynamic';
```
```json
// package.json of @angular/platform-browser-dynamic: before -> "sideEffects": false
"sideEffects": [
  "./fesm2022/platform-browser-dynamic.mjs",
  "./fesm2022/testing.mjs"
]
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/upgrade`, the internal `DowngradeComponentAdapter` (in `downgrade_component_adapter.ts`) previously assumed that every output of a downgraded component was an RxJS observable like `EventEmitter`, subscribing with an RxJS-style object (`emitter.subscribe({next: ...})`). Since `output()` (stable since v19.0) produces an `OutputEmitterRef` whose `subscribe()` takes a function, this threw `TypeError: listenerFn is not a function at OutputEmitterRef.emit` at runtime in hybrid apps (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60366). The fix in commit 9e1a1030 / PR #60369 casts the output as `EventEmitter<any> | OutputEmitterRef<any>` and calls `emitter.subscribe(...)` with a callback function instead of a `{next}` observer object, so AngularJS bindings set via `downgradeComponent` now fire correctly for both output styles (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9e1a1030c818e9849c00ded5f79535ea56976ea8, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60369).

**Affects:** Hybrid AngularJS/Angular applications that use `downgradeComponent` for a component with outputs declared via the `output()` signal API (`OutputEmitterRef`), wired to AngularJS via `$scope`/bindings; previously these threw a `TypeError` when emitting.

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix in `@angular/upgrade`; no application code changes are required. Apps that worked around the broken behavior (e.g. wrapping `output()` in an `EventEmitter`) should re-verify downgraded component output wiring after upgrading.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (downgrade_component_adapter.ts)
const emitter = componentRef.instance[output.prop] as EventEmitter<any>;
if (emitter) {
  const subscription = emitter.subscribe({
    next: isAssignment
      ? (v: any) => setter!(this.scope, v)
      : (v: any) => getter(this.scope, {'$event': v}),
  });

// after
const emitter = componentRef.instance[output.prop] as EventEmitter<any> | OutputEmitterRef<any>;
if (emitter) {
  const subscription = emitter.subscribe(
    isAssignment
      ? (v: any) => setter!(this.scope, v)
      : (v: any) => getter(this.scope, {'$event': v}),
  );
```

**What changed:** `platformBrowserTesting` (from `@angular/platform-browser/testing`) was previously built directly on `platformCore` with only a `PLATFORM_INITIALIZER` provider that called `BrowserDomAdapter.makeCurrent()`; it did not include any `platformBrowser` providers (`PLATFORM_ID`, `PLATFORM_INITIALIZER`/`initDomAdapter`, `DOCUMENT`). In v20 it is now `createPlatformFactory(platformBrowser, 'browserTesting')`, so the chain is `platformCore → platformBrowser → platformBrowserTesting` and `platformBrowser → platformBrowserDynamic → platformBrowserDynamicTesting` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60480, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8e8ccc792790c876cf5831f3d7a504290c665a1b). Side effects of the same commit: `platformBrowserDynamic` is now created from `platformBrowser` instead of `platformCoreDynamic`; the internal platform factories `ɵplatformCoreDynamic` and `ɵplatformCoreDynamicTesting`, and private exports `ɵINTERNAL_BROWSER_PLATFORM_PROVIDERS` and `ɵinitDomAdapter`, were removed; `ɵINTERNAL_BROWSER_DYNAMIC_PLATFORM_PROVIDERS` now contains only the compiler providers (`COMPILER_OPTIONS`, `ResourceLoader`, `CompilerFactory`) and `platformServerTesting` is built on `platformCore` again with combined browser-dynamic + server providers (patch of the commit). The PR notes this change "is needed to remove `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` from newly generated apps." Signatures of the public `platformBrowser`, `platformBrowserDynamic`, `platformBrowserTesting`, `platformBrowserDynamicTesting`, `platformServerTesting` are unchanged (v19/v20 `goldens/public-api/platform-browser/index.api.md`).

**Affects:** Applications whose test setup initializes the TestBed with `platformBrowserTesting()` via `getTestBed().initTestEnvironment(...)`, or that reference the removed private symbols `ɵplatformCoreDynamic`, `ɵplatformCoreDynamicTesting`, `ɵINTERNAL_BROWSER_PLATFORM_PROVIDERS`, `ɵinitDomAdapter`, `ɵDOMTestComponentRenderer` from `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing`, or that override platform-level providers (e.g. `PLATFORM_ID`, `DOCUMENT`) in `extraProviders`.

**Action:** required: re-verify unit tests that pass `platformBrowserTesting()` to `initTestEnvironment` — the platform now also installs `platformBrowser` providers (`PLATFORM_ID`, `DOCUMENT`, DOM adapter init), so custom `extraProviders` overriding them and any duplicate DOM-adapter initialization must be checked; remove any use of the deleted `ɵ` internal platform symbols; re-verify `platformServerTesting`-based server rendering tests.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// @angular/platform-browser/testing — BEFORE (v19)
export const platformBrowserTesting = createPlatformFactory(
  platformCore,
  'browserTesting',
  _TEST_BROWSER_PLATFORM_PROVIDERS,
);

// AFTER (v20)
export const platformBrowserTesting = createPlatformFactory(platformBrowser, 'browserTesting');
```

**What changed:** The `@angular/animations` package `package.json` added `@angular/common` to its `peerDependencies`. In the 19.0.0 source, `packages/animations/package.json` listed only `@angular/core` under `peerDependencies` (<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/animations/package.json>); in the 20.0.0 source it lists both `@angular/core` and `@angular/common` (<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/animations/package.json>). The PR explains that `@angular/animations` already depended on `@angular/common` in code, and this dependency was added to `package.json` "as otherwise pnpm strict builds will fail" (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60660>, <https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fa48f98d9f7e7b74deba65bea9bc90843b1c283b>).

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/animations` that install with `pnpm` in strict mode (or any other strict peer-dependency resolver), where a missing `@angular/common` peer would cause the install/build to fail.

**Action:** required: ensure `@angular/common` is listed as a dependency (or is otherwise resolvable as a peer) alongside `@angular/animations` in the application's `package.json` so that strict package managers like `pnpm` can satisfy the new `@angular/common` peer of `@angular/animations`; `@angular/animations` itself already ships the fix. For most apps this is already satisfied since `@angular/platform-browser` pulls in `@angular/common`.

**Before/after:**
```json
// @angular/animations package.json — peerDependencies (19.0.0)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/core": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER"
}

// @angular/animations package.json — peerDependencies (20.0.0)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/core": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "@angular/common": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER"
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds validation in the template compiler so the alias after `as` in an `@if` (and `@else if`) block parameter must be a valid JavaScript identifier. Previously the regex `CONDITIONAL_ALIAS_PATTERN = /^(as\s)+(.*)/` in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_control_flow.ts` silently accepted anything after `as`, so a template like `@if (foo; as var && !bar) {...}` compiled with no error or warning; the rest of the expression was silently swallowed as (invalid) alias text (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ca5aa4d55b352d1ead43d78b6a74d9e3b57f8777, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60580, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59939). Now the pattern is `/^(as\s+)(.*)/` and the captured name is tested against `IDENTIFIER_PATTERN = /^[$A-Z_][0-9A-Z_$]*$/i`; on failure the compiler emits a `ParseError` with the message `"as" expression must be a valid JavaScript identifier` (same URLs).

**Affects:** Templates using `@if (...) ; as <alias>` (or `@else if ... as`) where the text after `as` is not a single valid JS identifier — e.g. `@if (foo; as foo && bar) {hello}`, which now fails compilation with `NG` template parse error instead of building silently.

**Action:** required: fix any template that fails with `"as" expression must be a valid JavaScript identifier` — keep only the identifier after `as` and move the remaining expression logic into the condition, e.g. `@if (foo && bar; as foo)`. Applications whose `as` aliases are already valid identifiers need no change; re-verify that builds still pass.

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Before: compiled silently in v19; the "&& !bar" was swallowed as alias text -->
@if (foo; as var && !bar) { if is true }

<!-- After (v20): compiler error "as" expression must be a valid JavaScript identifier -->
<!-- Fix: keep only the identifier after "as" -->
@if (foo && !bar; as var) { if is true }
```

**What changed:** In `extractClassMetadata` (packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/common/src/metadata.ts), the check for duplicate decorated instance members previously threw a plain `throw new Error("Duplicate decorated properties found on class '...': ...")`, which crashed `ngc`/the Angular Language Service (assertion "Expected successfully analyzed compilation") instead of reporting an error. It now throws `new FatalDiagnosticError(ErrorCode.DUPLICATE_DECORATED_PROPERTIES, duplicateDecoratedMembers[0].nameNode ?? clazz, ...)`, so the compiler and language service emit a fatal diagnostic pointing at the offending member instead of crashing (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f4c4b10ea8dc263c30d1051a83a72486344d81e4.patch). A new public enum member `DUPLICATE_DECORATED_PROPERTIES = 1012` was added to `ErrorCode` in @angular/compiler-cli diagnostics. Diagnostic message text: `Duplicate decorated properties found on class 'AppComponent': test1` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60376, https://github.com/angular/vscode-ng-language-service/issues/2091).

**Affects:** Only sources that declare two decorated members with the same name on one class (e.g. two `@Input() test1?: string;` in a `@Component`) — these were already TypeScript errors (`Duplicate identifier 'test1'.`) and previously hard-crashed compilation/language service.

**Action:** none — no application change needed; upgrading applications merely get error `1012` reported instead of a crash. Fix any such duplicate decorated property, which is a genuine code bug.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before: crash
throw new Error(
  `Duplicate decorated properties found on class '${clazz.name.text}': ` +
    duplicateDecoratedMemberNames.join(', '),
);

// after: fatal diagnostic (error code 1012)
throw new FatalDiagnosticError(
  ErrorCode.DUPLICATE_DECORATED_PROPERTIES,
  duplicateDecoratedMembers[0].nameNode ?? clazz,
  `Duplicate decorated properties found on class '${clazz.name.text}': ` +
    duplicateDecoratedMembers.map((member) => member.name).join(', '),
);
```

**What changed:** The `ng generate @angular/core:output-migration` schematic (the optional migration that converts `@Output()` declarations to signal-based `output()`) previously dropped the event payload type when the `@Output` property's type argument was on the declaration rather than on the `new EventEmitter()` initializer. `calculateDeclarationReplacement` in `packages/core/schematics/migrations/output-migration/output-replacements.ts` only read `node.initializer.typeArguments`, so `@Output() eventMovement: EventEmitter<IResponse> = new EventEmitter();` became `readonly eventMovement = output();` (payload type `void`), causing errors like `TS2345: Argument of type ... is not assignable to parameter of type 'void'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59248). The fix also falls back to the declared property type (`node.type.typeArguments`) when the initializer carries no type arguments, producing `readonly eventMovement = output<IResponse>();` — including with an alias, e.g. `@Output('customEvent') ...` → `readonly eventMovement = output<IResponse>({ alias: 'customEvent' });` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60626, commit 8f68d1bec3, `output-replacements.ts` diff).

**Affects:** Applications that run the `@angular/core:output-migration` schematic (or ran it on an earlier 20.x) and have `@Output()` declarations typed via the property, e.g. `foo: EventEmitter<T> = new EventEmitter();` — i.e. without type arguments on the `new EventEmitter()` call.

**Action:** required: re-run `ng generate @angular/core:output-migration` on 20.0.0-containing-this-fix code, or manually re-add the lost payload type as `output<T>()` on any previously migrated outputs that lost their type; then re-verify `.emit(...)` call sites compile without `void` payload errors.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (schematic bug — type dropped):
// @Output() eventMovement: EventEmitter<IResponse> = new EventEmitter();
readonly eventMovement = output();          // wrong: emit() typed void

// after (fixed schematic):
readonly eventMovement = output<IResponse>();
// with alias:
readonly eventMovement = output<IResponse>({ alias: 'customEvent' });
```

**What changed:** Inside the internal `gatherDeferBlocksByJSActionAttribute` helper (`packages/core/src/hydration/utils.ts`), the `eventTypes` string was rebuilt from the `hoverEventNames` and `interactionEventNames` arrays. The old code `[...hoverEventNames.join(':;'), ...interactionEventNames.join(':;')].join('|')` spread each joined string, so it split the joined string into individual characters and joined those single characters with `|` — the PR author states "This regexp accidentally worked. It was splitting by individual character and putting a pipe in rather than splitting by set of event types." The new code `const eventTypes = [hoverEventNames.join(':;'), interactionEventNames.join(':;')].join('|');` builds the alternation from whole event names separated by `;`. This string is then used in `attr?.match(eventTypes)` against the `jsaction` attribute of `[jsaction]` nodes to map deferred blocks (`ngb` block id) during incremental hydration (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/bc79985c65c38dee17f5cb53f2a14768632f72dc, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60592).

**Affects:** Applications using `@defer` blocks with `hover` or `interaction` triggers together with incremental hydration (`withIncrementalHydration()` in `provideClientHydration()`); no public API, annotation, property key, or dependency coordinate changes — the change is entirely within internal core hydration code.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
const eventTypes = [...hoverEventNames.join(':;'), ...interactionEventNames.join(':;')].join(
  '|',
);
// after
const eventTypes = [hoverEventNames.join(':;'), interactionEventNames.join(':;')].join('|');
```

**What changed:** The `ng generate @angular/core:signal-queries-migration` schematic bug fix ensures accessibility modifiers (public, private, protected) are retained when converting decorator-based queries to signal queries (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60688). Previously, if you ran `ng generate @angular/core:signal-queries-migration` on code like `@ViewChild('sidenav') public sidenav: HTMLElement;`, the schematic removed the `public` keyword, producing `readonly sidenav = viewChild<HTMLElement>('sidenav');`. Now it preserves the accessibility modifier, producing `public readonly sidenav = viewChild<HTMLElement>('sidenav');`. This affects all query types: `@ViewChild()`, `@ViewChildren()`, `@ContentChild()`, and `@ContentChildren()` when they have explicit accessibility modifiers.

**Affects:** All applications using `ng generate @angular/core:signal-queries-migration` where `@ViewChild()`, `@ViewChildren()`, `@ContentChild()`, or `@ContentChildren()` decorated properties have explicit `public`, `private`, or `protected` accessibility modifiers.

**Action:** required: re-verify that after running `ng generate @angular/core:signal-queries-migration`, properties with explicit accessibility modifiers retain those modifiers in the generated signal query declarations.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before migration
@ViewChild('sidenav') public sidenav: HTMLElement;

// After migration (fixed behavior)
public readonly sidenav = viewChild<HTMLElement>('sidenav');
```

**What changed:** The `inject` schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`, file `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/migration.ts`) has an internal-only option, `_internalCombineMemberInitializers`, used when inlining constructor-body initializers into property declarations. Previously the new `ts.factory.createPropertyDeclaration(...)` node built with `cloneModifiers(declaration.modifiers)` / `cloneName(declaration.name)` lost the member's leading JSDoc comments when the declaration was removed and re-emitted in initialization order (`preserveInitOrder` path); doc comments such as `/** Value of Foo */` were silently deleted. The fix (PR #60588) builds the replacement node two ways: in the `preserveInitOrder` branch it passes `declaration.modifiers` and `declaration.name` directly so the comment is kept, and in the in-place `tracker.replaceNode` branch it strips comments via the clones (comment isn't part of the replaced range). Regressions were covered by a new test "should account for doc strings when inlining initializers and combining in initialization order" in `packages/core/schematics/test/inject_migration_spec.ts`. Note the emitted combined members can be slightly under-indented (a comment in the test says this is acceptable since the code path is internal-only) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/da6e93f4341804cd16327596c30d8f9258b40d7e, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60588). The public schema for the schematic only exposes `path`, `migrateAbstractClasses`, `backwardsCompatibleConstructors`, and `nonNullableOptional` — no `combineMemberInitializers` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/schema.json).

**Affects:** Effectively no external applications: the behaviour only applied to the internal-only `combineMemberInitializers` option, which is not part of the public `inject` migration schema, and the option only rewrites property declarations whose initializers were assigned in the constructor body. The PR is marked `target: patch`, `bugfix`, and "Does this PR introduce a breaking change: No".

**Action:** none. Applications upgrading to 20.0.0 need no code, configuration, or dependency changes for this entry; nothing about the public `inject` migration options changed.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (preserveInitOrder path) — single node built with cloned modifiers/name,
// so leading doc comments were dropped when the declaration was re-emitted:
const newProperty = ts.factory.createPropertyDeclaration(
  cloneModifiers(declaration.modifiers),
  cloneName(declaration.name),
  declaration.questionToken,
  declaration.type,
  initializer,
);

// after — original modifiers/name are used so the comment is preserved:
const newProperty = ts.factory.createPropertyDeclaration(
  declaration.modifiers,
  declaration.name,
  declaration.questionToken,
  declaration.type,
  initializer,
);
```

```typescript
/** Value of Foo */
private readonly value: number = this.foo.getValue();
/** ID of Foo */
id: string = this.value.toString();
```

**What changed:** In `packages/language-service/src/adapters.ts`, the Angular Language Service adapters no longer `throw new Error(...)`; they log with `console.error(...)` and return an empty string instead. Specifically `LanguageServiceAdapter.readResource()` previously threw `readResource() should not be called on TS file: ${fileName}` and `Failed to get script info when trying to read ${fileName}`, and `LSParseConfigHost.readFile()` previously threw `LanguageServiceFS#readFile called on unavailable file ${path}` (). The PR states: "This prevents errors from being thrown from the adapters. Throwing errors in the language service causes the extension to crash. Repeated errors will permanently break the extension." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60651, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0e9e0348dd972c96ecee8bb990aba8c604dc704f). It fixes a crash reported when a `.ts` file was referenced as `templateUrl` in `@Component`, producing "The Angular Language Service server crashed 5 times in the last 3 minutes. The server will not be restarted." (https://github.com/angular/vscode-ng-language-service/issues/2166). For this hop, the extension changelog shows the fix shipped in extension `19.2.3` (which upgraded `@angular/language-service` to v19.2.5), and the extension `20.0.0` release upgraded `@angular/language-service` to v20.0.0-rc.0 ().

**Affects:** Editor tooling only — editors/IDEs using `@angular/language-service` / `@angular/language-server` (e.g. the Angular VS Code extension) on projects with invalid or unavailable resource references, such as a `templateUrl` pointing at a `.ts` file. No application code, template compilation, or runtime behavior of an upgraded app is affected.

**Action:** none (this is a language-service robustness fix; no application code, config, or dependency changes are needed).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/language-service/src/adapters.ts
// Before:
throw new Error(`readResource() should not be called on TS file: ${fileName}`);
// ...
throw new Error(`Failed to get script info when trying to read ${fileName}`);
// ...
throw new Error(`LanguageServiceFS#readFile called on unavailable file ${path}`);

// After:
console.error(`readResource() should not be called on TS file: ${fileName}`);
return '';
// ...
console.error(`Failed to get script info when trying to read ${fileName}`);
return '';
// ...
console.error(`LanguageServiceFS#readFile called on unavailable file ${path}`);
return '';
```

**What changed:** This is a bug fix inside Angular's `inject` migration schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`, source at `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/analysis.ts`), shipped in the 20.0.x line. The `getSuperParameters` function in `analysis.ts` decides whether a constructor parameter is referenced inside a `super(...)` call — such parameters must be converted to a local `const x = inject(...)` declared *before* the `super` call rather than only a field initializer, because a field initializer cannot run before `super`. Previously that walk only recognised plain identifier references; a TypeScript *shorthand property assignment* such as `super({service})` was not detected, so the migration emitted code that referenced the parameter inside `super` before it was initialised. The fix adds an `else if (ts.isShorthandPropertyAssignment(decl) && topLevelParameterNames.has(decl.name.text))` branch in `getSuperParameters` that matches the shorthand name against `topLevelParameters` and adds the parameter to `usedParams` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/15f53f035ba64fa761db64d48dd9daa1499370e3.patch). The same PR also fixed related super-call handling for parameters referenced via `this` inside inline functions (`isInlineFunction`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60602).

**Affects:** Only applications that run the `inject` migration schematic and contain a subclass constructor (e.g. `@Directive()`/`@Component()` classes extending a parent) that passes a constructor parameter to `super(...)` via object shorthand (e.g. `super({service})`). No effect on `ng update` itself or on runtime behaviour.

**Action:** none — no application code, config or dependency change is needed; just re-run `ng generate @angular/core:inject` on the 20.x toolchain and verify output for subclasses with shorthand arguments in `super` calls.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// input handled by the fixed migration (from inject_migration_spec.ts)
@Directive()
export class MyDir extends Parent {
  constructor(private service: Service) {
    super({service});
  }
}

// expected migration output
import { Directive, inject } from '@angular/core';

@Directive()
export class MyDir extends Parent {
  private service: Service;

  constructor() {
    const service = inject(Service);

    super({service});

    this.service = service;
  }
}
```

**What changed:** This is a bug fix inside the `inject` schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`, code in `packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/analysis.ts` and `internal.ts`), not a change applications must migrate for. The migration previously treated constructor parameters referenced directly inside a `super(...)` call as un-migratable to `inject()`. Two bugs in that logic were fixed: (1) parameters referenced via `this` inside inline functions (arrow/function expressions) passed inside `super(...)` — e.g. `super({callback: () => this.service.doStuff()})` — are now allowed, since inside callbacks `this` access is not restricted; a new `isInlineFunction` helper stops the walker from descending into inline functions (4b161e6234) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4b161e62344a51e87f1b5c5778fd72e56fc37922). (2) shorthand property assignments in `super` calls — e.g. `super({service})` — are now recognized as parameter uses via `ts.isShorthandPropertyAssignment`, so the parameter is converted to a local `const service = inject(Service);` before `super(...)` with `this.service = service;` assigned after (15f53f035b) ().

**Affects:** Only applications that run the `@angular/core:inject` migration and have classes with `super(...)` calls that reference constructor parameters via `this` inside inline callbacks or via shorthand property assignment. No effect on application code that isn't run through the migration.

**Action:** none — the fix is inside the schematics package; upgrading applications need to do nothing beyond running the migration with the new version, which will now convert these previously-skipped cases.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before fix: this pattern was skipped by the migration. After fix, it is converted:
// input
@Directive()
export class MyDir extends Parent {
  constructor(private service: Service) {
    super({callback: () => this.service.doStuff()});
  }
}
// output
@Directive()
export class MyDir extends Parent {
  private service = inject(Service);

  constructor() {
    super({callback: () => this.service.doStuff()});
  }
}
```

Based on the retrieved commit patch, Pull Request, Issue, and Angular documentation:

**What changed:** The `ViewTransitionInfo` interface in `@angular/router` now exposes the native browser `ViewTransition` `types` property. Previously, this property was missing from the type definition, preventing TypeScript users from accessing it. The fix adds `readonly types: Set<string>` to the `transition` object within `ViewTransitionInfo` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/958e98e4f7ab8e708440f03eb68612d1802b9a71)). This allows developers to add custom types (e.g., `forward`, `backward`) to the browser's `ViewTransition` object, which can then be targeted in CSS using pseudo-classes like `:active-view-transition-type()` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60285)).

**Affects:** Applications using the `withViewTransitions()` router feature, specifically those providing an `onViewTransitionCreated` callback in `ViewTransitionsFeatureOptions`.

**Action:** required: Update code inside the `onViewTransitionCreated` callback to utilize `transitionInfo.transition.types` for adding navigation-specific transition types, or re-verify that existing code correctly accesses this property if it was previously casted or ignored due to missing types ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/958e98e4f7ab8e708440f03eb68612d1802b9a71)).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/router/src/utils/view_transition.ts
// Diff from commit 958e98e
@@ -80,6 +80,12 @@ export interface ViewTransitionInfo {
      * @see
      */
     skipTransition(): void;
+
+    /**
+     * @see
+     * @see
+     */
+    readonly types: Set<string>;
   };
```

**What changed:** The Angular service worker (`@angular/service-worker` / `packages/service-worker/worker/src/driver.ts`) now assigns a web worker the app version of the client that spawned it, instead of the newest installed version. A request with `event.request.destination === 'worker'` that has both `clientId` and `resultingClientId` is treated as a "worker script request": the driver resolves the version from `event.clientId` (the spawning tab) rather than `event.resultingClientId || event.clientId`, and registers the new worker client in `clientVersionMap` with that same hash (calling `await this.sync()`); a mismatch throws `Version mismatch between worker client <id> and requesting client <id>` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7cd89ad2c66adeb625f75d23fea32e762162d3d5). Previously the worker was pinned to `latestHash`, so a worker spawned by a stale, un-refreshed tab fetched its script from the network — a 404 when old-version files are no longer on the server — and worker init failed (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/57971). The PR notes: "No, unless someone was relying on a faulty behavior" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58131).

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/service-worker` (`ng add @angular/pwa`) that lazily create Web Workers whose scripts are cached by the service worker, in the window after a new app version is installed but an already-open tab has not been refreshed.

**Action:** none — internal service-worker fix; no code, config, or dependency change required. Re-verify that on-demand worker scripts are served from cache (no network fallback / 404) after a version update in a stale tab.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// driver.ts — version lookup for a fetch event
// before:
const clientId = event.resultingClientId || event.clientId;

// after:
const isWorkerScriptRequest =
  event.request.destination === 'worker' && event.resultingClientId && event.clientId;
const clientId = isWorkerScriptRequest
  ? event.clientId
  : event.resultingClientId || event.clientId;
```

**What changed:** The `TemplateLiteralElementExpr` no longer attempts to estimate the `rawText` from the node's `sourceSpan` when raw text is not explicitly provided, as `sourceSpan` estimates are unreliable with escape sequences (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3441f7b914c73ccdaacbcd935e945dc304c5962a). It now relies purely on a fallback that manually escapes characters (`\`, `` ` ``, `${`) from the `text` property (). This removes code depending on `sourceSpan.toString()`, preventing unrecoverable errors thrown by TypeScript when the estimated raw string did not match the cooked string during AST construction (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60753).

**Affects:** Applications using template literals with `@let` or `track`, or any template containing escape sequences within template literal expressions.

**Action:** required: Re-verify that template literal expressions, specifically those used with `@let` or `track` or containing escape sequences, no longer produce build errors related to raw text mismatches ().

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/compiler/src/output/output_ast.ts

// Before:
// this.rawText =
//   rawText ?? sourceSpan?.toString() ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text));

// After:
// Note that we can't rely on the `sourceSpan` here, because it may be incorrect
this.rawText = rawText ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text));
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/compiler-cli`, the `NgCompiler` transpilation of the generated HMR update module previously used the project's TypeScript `compilerOptions` verbatim; some `module` settings (e.g. `"NodeNext"` / `"Node16"`) made the emitted HMR module use CommonJS-style `exports`, so the code served by the dev server (request to `/@ng/component?c=src%2F…`) threw `ReferenceError: exports is not defined` when a component's template or styles changed (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60795). The fix in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/src/compiler.ts` now overrides `module: ts.ModuleKind.ES2022` and `target: ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022` when calling `ts.transpileModule` for the HMR update module, so it always uses a native `export` (e.g. `export default function Cmp_UpdateMetadata(Cmp, ɵɵnamespaces, Component) {`) regardless of the user's options (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fc946c5f7261ee3e49fa037bc55703b9ffcfbff3, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60797).

**Affects:** Applications using the experimental Angular HMR option (`_enableHmr: true` in `angularCompilerOptions`) together with a non-ES `module`/`moduleResolution` setting such as `"NodeNext"`; development-time behaviour only, no production impact.

**Action:** none — internal compiler-cli fix; applications need no code, configuration or dependency change. Only re-verify that template/style hot reload works with unusual `module` settings.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/src/compiler.ts — ts.transpileModule(...)

// before
compilerOptions: this.options,

// after
compilerOptions: {
  ...this.options,
  // Some module types can produce additional code (see #60795) whereas we need the
  // HMR update module to use a native `export`. Override the `target` and `module`
  // to ensure that it looks as expected.
  module: ts.ModuleKind.ES2022,
  target: ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022,
} as ts.CompilerOptions,
```

**What changed:** This commit is a documentation-only correction to the Angular output migration guide (adev/src/content/reference/migrations/outputs.md). The "Before" example for the `@angular/core:output-migration` schematic previously imported `{Component, Output}` from '@angular/core' but referenced `new EventEmitter<string>()` without importing EventEmitter; the fix adds `EventEmitter` so the import reads `{Component, Output, EventEmitter}`. The commit does not change the migration schematic itself or the application-facing API. The migration schematic (`ng generate @angular/core:output-migration`) converts `@Output()` members using `new EventEmitter<T>()` to `readonly name = output<T>();` imported as `output` from '@angular/core', migrates `event.next()` to `event.emit()` and removes `event.complete()` calls (https://angular.dev/reference/migrations/outputs).

**Affects:** none at runtime — this is a doc fix only. The underlying `@angular/core:output-migration` schematic, which this doc describes, affects applications that declare `@Output()` class members with `new EventEmitter<T>()`.

**Action:** none for this change itself (documentation fix). The described schematic remains run as `ng generate @angular/core:output-migration`, optionally scoped with `--path` or `--analysis-dir`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (the doc's "Before" example, with the missing import fixed)
import {Component, Output, EventEmitter} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  template: `<button (click)="someMethod('test')">emit</button>`
})
export class MyComponent {
  @Output() someChange = new EventEmitter<string>();

  someMethod(value: string): void {
    this.someChange.emit(value);
  }
}

// After (what the migration produces)
import {Component, output} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  template: `<button (click)="someMethod('test')">emit</button>`
})
export class MyComponent {
  readonly someChange = output<string>();

  someMethod(value: string): void {
    this.someChange.emit(value);
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The `ng generate @angular/core:output-migration` schematic (the migration that converts `@Output() eventEmitter = new EventEmitter<T>();` to `eventEmitter = output<T>();`) previously transformed parameterless calls like `eventEmitter.emit();` into `output.emit();` with no argument. Because `emit(value: T)` requires a mandatory argument, the migrated code fails to compile with `TS2554: Expected 1 arguments, but got 0. An argument for 'value' was not provided.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58650). PR #60763 fixes this so that when a migrated typed output is emitted with no arguments, the migration inserts a `// TODO: The 'emit' function requires a mandatory <type> argument` comment on the line above the `emit()` call instead of leaving invalid code. For outputs declared as `new EventEmitter()` with no type argument, no TODO is inserted (the argument-less emit is left as-is) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f2bfa3151ee7ecb335665d55741387bd67ebee9d). This is a bug fix in the migration tooling, not a runtime behavior change.

**Affects:** Applications that run the outputs migration (`ng generate @angular/core:output-migration`, or the update-time equivalent) and have an `@Output()` `EventEmitter<T>` whose `.emit()` is called with no arguments.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {Directive, Output, EventEmitter} from '@angular/core';

@Directive()
export class TestDir {
  @Output() someChange = new EventEmitter<string>();

  someMethod(): void {
    this.someChange.emit();
  }
}
```
```typescript
import {Directive, output} from '@angular/core';

@Directive()
export class TestDir {
  readonly someChange = output<string>();

  someMethod(): void {
    // TODO: The 'emit' function requires a mandatory string argument
    this.someChange.emit();
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Tsurge migration runner inside `angular-devkit` (`packages/core/schematics/utils/tsurge/helpers/angular_devkit/run_in_devkit.ts`) no longer retains all TypeScript `Program`s across the whole schematic run (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9241615ad0825156f4bf31bc4308372e4789e902). Previously `runMigrationInDevkit` built a program for every tsconfig up front (`const programInfos = [...buildPaths, ...testPaths].map(...)`), keeping them all alive; now analysis iterates tsconfigs one at a time and, for non-funnel (`TsurgeFunnelMigration`) "complex" migrations, a program is re-created during the migrate phase. A new `export enum MigrationStage { Analysis, Migrate }` was added and the `TsurgeDevkitMigration` callbacks gained a stage parameter: `beforeProgramCreation?: (tsconfigPath: string, stage: MigrationStage) => void` and `afterProgramCreation?: (info: ProgramInfo, fileSystem: FileSystem, stage: MigrationStage) => void`. Affected schematics: `ng-generate/cleanup-unused-imports`, `output-migration`, `self-closing-tags-migration`, `signal-input-migration`, `signal-queries-migration`. This closes #59813 ("FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory" on `ng generate @angular/core:cleanup-unused-imports`); funnel migrations get a pure performance win, complex migrations may take longer but avoid Node running out of memory (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60776).

**Affects:** Internal CLI/schematics behaviour only — applications that run the Tsurge-based schematics (`ng generate @angular/core:cleanup-unused-imports`, `:signal-input-migration`, `:signal-queries-migration`, `:output-migration`, `:self-closing-tags-migration`), or the same migrations bundled with `ng update`, especially on large workspaces. No application source code, template, configuration or public Angular API is affected.

**Action:** none — this is a non-public performance/memory fix inside the migration tooling. Optionally re-verify that the migration schematics above complete without the previous `JavaScript heap out of memory` failure, and note that "complex" (non-funnel) migrations may log an additional `Running migration for: <tsconfig>...` phase and take somewhat longer.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// run_in_devkit.ts — old: all programs created and retained up front
const programInfos = [...buildPaths, ...testPaths].map((tsconfigPath) => {
  config.beforeProgramCreation?.(tsconfigPath);
  const baseInfo = migration.createProgram(tsconfigPath, fs);
  const info = migration.prepareProgram(baseInfo);
  config.afterProgramCreation?.(info, fs);
  return {info, tsconfigPath};
});

// new: one program per tsconfig during analysis; re-created for non-funnel migrate
export enum MigrationStage { Analysis, Migrate }
beforeProgramCreation?: (tsconfigPath: string, stage: MigrationStage) => void;
afterProgramCreation?: (info: ProgramInfo, fileSystem: FileSystem, stage: MigrationStage) => void;
```

**What changed:** In the Angular Language Service, template element completions are no longer offered when the cursor is inside an element's end tag or between the start and end tags. Previously (v19), placing the cursor inside `</div>` or between `<div>|</div>` triggered the element-completion builder, which surfaced mostly attributes/events "suggested in the wrong place" (99% of them, per the report) (https://github.com/angular/vscode-ng-language-service/issues/2157). The fix, in `CompletionBuilder` in `packages/language-service/src/completions.ts`, returns `undefined` when `element.endSourceSpan` exists and `isWithin(this.position, element.endSourceSpan)`, with a guard for self-closing tags where `element.endSourceSpan.start !== element.startSourceSpan.start`, so no completions are computed in those positions (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0e82d427743c1d22e1683da11f66e33846f38663). Tests added confirm: no element completions in `</¦button>` or `<button>¦</button>`, while event completions such as `(click)` are still offered for self-closing tags like `<br /¦` (same commit). It shipped in v20 as a `fix`/patch-target change and is not listed among the v20 breaking changes (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Editor/IDE authoring experience only, via the Angular Language Service (`@angular/language-service`, used by the VSCode ng-language-service extension): requests for completions at positions inside an end tag or between start and end tags of an element in templates. No runtime, compilation, or build behaviour of applications changes.

**Action:** none — this is an editor-completion quality fix; no code, configuration, dependency or build change is required in upgrading applications.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/language-service/src/completions.ts (v20, new guard in CompletionBuilder)
if (
  element.endSourceSpan &&
  isWithin(this.position, element.endSourceSpan) &&
  // start and end spans are the same for self closing tags
  element.endSourceSpan.start !== element.startSourceSpan.start
) {
  return undefined;
}
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0e82d427743c1d22e1683da11f66e33846f38663)

**What changed:** The Angular Language Service now escapes `$` characters in attribute/binding completion snippets. In `packages/language-service/src/attribute_completions.ts`, the `buildSnippet` helper was changed from `insertSnippet ? `${text}="$1"` : undefined` to `insertSnippet ? `${text.replace(/\$/gi, '\\$')}="$1"` : undefined` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60597/files; present in 20.0.0 at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/language-service/src/attribute_completions.ts). Previously an unescaped `$` in a completion name (e.g. an input/output named `$inputValue` for signal naming conventions) was interpreted by the editor as a snippet placeholder during snippet expansion, so accepting the completion dropped the typed text and left `[]=""` or `()=""` — fixes https://github.com/angular/vscode-ng-language-service/issues/2139 (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fcdef1019fd28c7261590ba484a949c809b9ceaf).

**Affects:** Editor/IDE autocomplete only (Angular Language Service in VS Code etc.), specifically completing inputs/outputs/attributes whose names contain a `$`, e.g. signal inputs named `$inputValue`. No effect on compiled application code, templates, or build.

**Action:** none — no code, config, or dependency change is needed; optionally re-verify that completing a `$`-prefixed input/output in a template now inserts e.g. `[$inputValue]="$1"` correctly.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (19.x)
return insertSnippet ? `${text}="$1"` : undefined;
// after (20.0.0)
return insertSnippet ? `${text.replace(/\$/gi, '\\$')}="$1"` : undefined;
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/common`, the `NgOptimizedImage` directive's internal `PreloadLinkCreator.createPreloadLinkTag` no longer throws a `RuntimeError` (`RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES`, error `NG02961`) when more than `DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT` (5) images are marked `priority` in dev mode. It now calls `console.warn` with `ɵformatRuntimeError(...)`, and only once per injector instance (a new `errorShown` flag gates repeated messages) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8f25d4a0331908d1b47da38b1359dc8b3760ec42.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60883). The message text is unchanged: "NG02961: The `NgOptimizedImage` directive has detected that more than 5 images were marked as priority. This might negatively affect an overall performance of the page. To fix this, remove the \"priority\" attribute from images with less priority." The change was made because exceeding the limit "doesn't break the application but merely degrades performance", fixing issue #60871 where the thrown error caused SSR to output `<!--container-->` placeholder comments instead of rendered HTML (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/60871). Note this is a cherry-pick/patch-target commit backporting the fix.

**Affects:** Applications using `NgOptimizedImage` (`ngSrc`) with more than 5 images marked `priority`, in dev mode (`ngDevMode`), especially under SSR — previously rendering threw `NG02961`; apps whose tests asserted on the thrown error are also affected.

**Action:** none for application code (the error becoming a warning is strictly more permissive; SSR rendering of over-preloaded pages now succeeds instead of failing). Apps with unit tests asserting `toThrowError('NG02961: ...')` around `NgOptimizedImage` usage must re-verify them, since the error is no longer thrown.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19, preload-link-creator.ts): threw a hard error
throw new RuntimeError(
  RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES,
  ngDevMode &&
    `The \`NgOptimizedImage\` directive has detected that more than ` +
      `${DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT} images were marked as priority. ` +
      ...
);

// After (Angular 20): single console warning via ɵformatRuntimeError
console.warn(
  formatRuntimeError(
    RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES,
    `The \`NgOptimizedImage\` directive has detected that more than ` +
      `${DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT} images were marked as priority. ` +
      ...
  ),
);
```

I have enough from the PR, commit, and diff. Final answer:

**What changed:** This is an internal-only improvement to the `inject-migration` schematic (`packages/core/schematics/ng-generate/inject-migration/`), which runs as part of `ng update` to convert constructor-based injection to `inject()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60713). Previously, when code in a constructor referenced a constructor parameter (e.g. `foo`), the tool inserted `const foo = this.foo` at the top of the constructor, which blocked that member's initializer from being hoisted out of the constructor by the internal `combineMemberInitializers` step. Now a new internal option `_internalReplaceParameterReferencesInInitializers` (in `MigrationOptions`, alongside `_internalCombineMemberInitializers`) makes the tool instead rewrite references to `this.foo` directly inside member initializers, provided `foo` is a `readonly` parameter property and the reference is not inside a nested scope that changes `this` (`function`, `class`, or object method). Per the commit message: "This allows more properties to be moved out of the constructor with combineMemberInitializers. For now, it only touches initializers, not all of the code in the constructor." (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b144126612e2cd14cbccc8d3cf4e2136a2e540ff)

**Affects:** Only output quality of the automatic `inject` migration during `ng update @angular/core@20 @angular/cli@20`, and only for classes with `readonly` parameter properties referenced in constructor member initializers. The option is prefixed `_internal`, so no application source code, API, or configuration is affected.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (old migration output): readonly bar stayed in the constructor
readonly bar = inject<Bar>(BAR_TOKEN);
private otherValue: string;
constructor() {
  const bar = this.bar;
  this.otherValue = bar.getOtherValue();
}

// After (new migration output): initializer hoisted out
readonly bar = inject<Bar>(BAR_TOKEN);
private otherValue: string = this.bar.getOtherValue();
```
(from the migration spec in https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b144126612e2cd14cbccc8d3cf4e2136a2e540ff)

**What changed:** In `httpResource` (from `@angular/common/http`), the `statusCode()` and `headers()` signals on the returned `HttpResourceRef` were previously only populated when the request succeeded. Angular 20 now also populates them when the request fails: in `packages/common/http/src/resource.ts`, the error handler was changed from `error: (error) => send({error})` to a block that, when the error is an `HttpErrorResponse`, calls `this._headers.set(error.headers)` and `this._statusCode.set(error.status)` before `send({error})` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d39e09da413732385a12ed21eb468649233e26d0.diff). Per the PR, "Currently the HTTP status code and headers are only included if the request succeeded. Given status codes convey more information in case of a request error vs. success, this makes it more useful than inspecting what is contained in `.error()`" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60802). The PR is classified as a bugfix and marked as not a breaking change.

**Affects:** Applications using `httpResource` that read `.statusCode()` or `.headers()` and assumed they stay `undefined`/unchanged when a request errors (e.g. treating a non-`undefined` `statusCode()` as proof of success), for errors delivered as `HttpErrorResponse`.

**Action:** required: re-verify any logic that branches on `statusCode()` or `headers()` being empty/undefined as an indicator of an errored request; error responses now expose the failing status (e.g. 429) and response headers, so success checks must not rely on these signals alone — use `.error()` / `.hasValue()` instead.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (resource.ts):
error: (error) => send({error}),

// After (resource.ts):
error: (error) => {
  if (error instanceof HttpErrorResponse) {
    this._headers.set(error.headers);
    this._statusCode.set(error.status);
  }

  send({error});
},
```

**What changed:** Before Angular 20, only forms created with `FormGroupDirective` (reactive forms) emitted `FormSubmittedEvent` and `FormResetEvent` on the `AbstractControl.events` observable. This fix (shipped in v20) makes template-driven `NgForm` emit them too: `NgForm.onSubmit()` now calls `this.form._events.next(new FormSubmittedEvent(this.control))` after `this.ngSubmit.emit($event)`, and `NgForm.resetForm()` calls `this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form))` after `this.form.reset(value)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ea4a21121681c78652f314c78c58390dca25f266.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60887). This resolved issue #58894, where `FormResetEvent` was never emitted when resetting via the API (only via a native `<input type="reset">`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58894). The change was classified as a non-breaking bugfix in the PR.

**Affects:** Applications using template-driven forms (`NgForm` / `ngForm` directive) that subscribe to the form's `events` observable and count, filter, or react to emitted `ControlEvent`s (e.g. `ValueChangeEvent`, `TouchedChangeEvent`, `StatusChangeEvent`, `FormResetEvent`, `FormSubmittedEvent`).

**Action:** none — no code or config changes are required; only re-verify any event-stream logic that assumed extra `FormSubmittedEvent`/`FormResetEvent` events were absent for template-driven forms (subscribing code may now receive these events on submit via `NgForm.onSubmit()` and on `NgForm.resetForm()`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/forms/src/directives/ng_form.ts (v20)
onSubmit($event: Event): boolean {
  this.submittedReactive.set(true);
  syncPendingControls(this.form, this._directives);
  this.ngSubmit.emit($event);
  this.form._events.next(new FormSubmittedEvent(this.control)); // new in v20
  ...
}

resetForm(value: any = undefined): void {
  this.form.reset(value);
  this.submittedReactive.set(false);
  this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form)); // new in v20
}
```

**What changed:** A fix to `EventEmitter`'s async error handling. An `EventEmitter` created with `isAsync: true` registers a task via `PendingTasks` before invoking subscriber callbacks inside `setTimeout`. Prior to this fix, if a subscriber callback threw an error, `pendingTasks.remove(taskId)` was skipped because it was placed after the `fn(value)` invocation, causing the application to remain unstable indefinitely. The fix wraps the `fn(value)` call with `try-finally`, ensuring `pendingTasks.remove(taskId)` always executes, allowing the app to reach the stable state even when an error is thrown. This is particularly relevant for server-side rendering, where errors should not silently prevent the application from rendering. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61028, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/946b844e0db7e8f2cabcaf4cb63abced62c01fc7)

**Affects:** Applications using an async `EventEmitter` (e.g., `new EventEmitter<T>(true)`) with subscribers that throw errors. Under normal circumstances, such errors should be handled by the RxJS error handler or Angular's error handler, but previously these uncaught errors could block stability.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
  private wrapInTimeout(fn: (value: unknown) => any) {
    return (value: unknown) => {
      const taskId = this.pendingTasks?.add();
      setTimeout(() => {
        try {
          fn(value);
        } finally {
          if (taskId !== undefined) {
            this.pendingTasks?.remove(taskId);
          }
        }
      });
    };
  }
```

**What changed:** `NodeInjectorDestroyRef.onDestroy` previously threw `NG0911: View has already been destroyed` if the view was already destroyed. In this version it checks `isDestroyed(lView)` and runs the callback synchronously, returning a no-op unregister (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5f7f04634f57c0fb669e2b81c85a0dff972868d8). This makes `takeUntilDestroyed` complete/unsubscribe the subscription instead of erroring when the view is already destroyed (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/54527).
**Affects:** Code calling `DestroyRef.onDestroy` or `takeUntilDestroyed` after the view has been destroyed (e.g. inside `ngOnDestroy` or via `Promise.resolve().then(...)`)
**Action:** none (no-op for well-behaved code). Re-verify that `takeUntilDestroyed` subscriptions complete cleanly instead of throwing when scheduled after the view is destroyed.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: registering on a destroyed view threw
fixture.destroy();
destroyRef.onDestroy(() => {}); // throws NG0911: View has already been destroyed.

// After: callback is invoked immediately; returns a noop unregister
if (isDestroyed(lView)) {
  callback();
  return () => {};
}
storeLViewOnDestroy(lView, callback);
return () => removeLViewOnDestroy(lView, callback);
```

**What changed:** Previously the event-replay "stash" listener was a single module-level global function in `@angular/core` internals; calling `withEventReplay()` (via `provideClientHydration(withEventReplay())`) replaced that one global via `setStashFn(fn)`, so when several Angular apps are bootstrapped on one page (micro-frontends, or one server-rendered app hydrated alongside a client-only app), the last app to initialize overrode the other's stash function and events were no longer stashed/replayed correctly (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59635). The fix replaces the single function with a `Map` keyed by `APP_ID`: `setStashFn(appId, fn)` registers and a new `clearStashFn(appId)` removes entries; `clearStashFn(appId)` is now called on app destroy instead of the old `setStashFn(() => {})` no-op reset, and the listener lookup reads `lView[INJECTOR].get(APP_ID)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2e140a136a044a965da7f55e0d83731860671a05, ).

**Affects:** Applications using SSR hydration with `withEventReplay()`, especially where more than one Angular application is bootstrapped on the same page, and code that destroyed an app and expected replay stashing to be globally disabled afterwards.

**Action:** none — internal, non-public API change (these symbols are not exported from `@angular/core`). Apps relying on event replay with multiple bootstrapped apps should re-verify that click/interaction events buffered during SSR are still replayed after hydration, and that each app now gets a distinct `APP_ID`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (19.x): single global stash function
let stashEventListener = (el: RNode, eventName: string, listenerFn: (e?: any) => any) => {};
export function setStashFn(fn: typeof stashEventListener) {
  stashEventListener = fn;
}

// After (20.x): per-APP_ID map
type StashEventListener = (el: RNode, eventName: string, listenerFn: (e?: any) => any) => void;
const stashEventListeners = new Map<string, StashEventListener>();
export function setStashFn(appId: string, fn: StashEventListener) {
  stashEventListeners.set(appId, fn);
}
export function clearStashFn(appId: string) {
  stashEventListeners.delete(appId);
}
```

**What changed:** In `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts`, the `triggerResourceLoading` function previously registered a pending task via `injector.get(PendingTasksInternal)` and removed it (`pendingTasks.remove(taskId)`) at completion inside the loading `Promise` chain, which marked the application as stable too early — before the deferred dependencies finished loading (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61056, ). Now the task is added via `injector.get(PendingTasks)` (`const removeTask = injector.get(PendingTasks).add();`) and removed only in the `.finally(...)` of the returned `tDetails.loadingPromise`, so the app is not marked stable until the defer block resource loading truly completes. The symptom (issue #61038) was incorrect/incomplete prerendered or SSR HTML for components inside `@defer(hydrate never)` with incremental hydration and zoneless change detection; regression started in Angular 19.0.5, fixed in 20.0.0-next.4 and backported to 19 (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/61038).

**Affects:** applications using `@defer` blocks together with SSR/SSG or prerendering (notably with incremental hydration and zoneless/experimental zoneless change detection), where rendering waited on the premature "stable" signal.

**Action:** none (framework-side fix). Applications must re-verify that SSR/prerendered HTML now fully resolves deferred content and resolved input data on first render, instead of producing incomplete templates.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before: task removed inside the chain, marking app stable too early
const pendingTasks = injector.get(PendingTasksInternal);
const taskId = pendingTasks.add();
// ...
tDetails.loadingPromise = null;
pendingTasks.remove(taskId);

// After: task removed only in .finally() of the returned loading promise
const removeTask = injector.get(PendingTasks).add();
// ...
return tDetails.loadingPromise.finally(() => {
  tDetails.loadingPromise = null;
  removeTask();
});
```

**What changed:** Previously, `platformServer` always assigned `globalThis['ngServerMode'] = undefined` in the `platform.onDestroy` hook regardless of how the variable was initially defined (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a6f0d5bc20382689b7336a7e1c79c0685252cc21). Now, `platformServer` tracks whether `ngServerMode` was originally undefined via `const noServerModeSet = typeof ngServerMode === 'undefined';` and only registers the `platform.onDestroy` cleanup that forces it to `undefined` if it was not already set externally. When set to undefined, browser-specific code paths could run on the server causing hard debug issues (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a6f0d5bc20382689b7336a7e1c79c0685252cc21).
**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/platform-server` or SSR that set `ngServerMode` externally (globally or via a bundler) before calling `platformServer`.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (19.0.0)
export function platformServer(extraProviders?: StaticProvider[] | undefined): PlatformRef {
  if (typeof ngServerMode === 'undefined') {
    globalThis['ngServerMode'] = true;
  }

  // ... platform creation ...

  platform.onDestroy(() => {
    globalThis['ngServerMode'] = undefined;
  });

  return platform;
}

// After (20.0.0)
export function platformServer(extraProviders?: StaticProvider[] | undefined): PlatformRef {
  const noServerModeSet = typeof ngServerMode === 'undefined';
  if (noServerModeSet) {
    globalThis['ngServerMode'] = true;
  }

  // ... platform creation ...

  if (noServerModeSet) {
    platform.onDestroy(() => {
      globalThis['ngServerMode'] = undefined;
    });
  }

  return platform;
}
```

**What changed:** `FetchBackend` (`packages/common/http/src/fetch.ts`, used by `provideHttpClient(withFetch())`) now tracks Angular app/injector destruction and cancels the in-flight response stream instead of leaving it pending. v20 `FetchBackend` injects `DestroyRef` and sets a `destroyed` flag in `this.destroyRef.onDestroy(...)`; inside the `while (true)` chunk-reading loop it checks `if (this.destroyed) { await reader.cancel(); canceled = true; break; }`, and after the loop `if (canceled) { observer.complete(); return; }` so `parseBody` never runs on partially-read chunks. Rationale: "Streams left in a pending state (due to `break` without cancel) may continue consuming or holding onto data behind the scenes. Calling `reader.cancel()` allows the browser or the underlying system to release any network or memory resources associated with the stream." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61528, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/126efc9972e18806e71977d51a55f8ec2f0514d6.patch; v19.0.0 loop had no destroyed check: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/common/http/src/fetch.ts)

**Affects:** applications using the Fetch-backed `HttpClient` (`withFetch()`) that destroy the Angular app (e.g. micro-frontend unmount, `ApplicationRef.destroy()`, test teardown) while a request body is still streaming. Those observables now complete (no `HttpResponse`) and the browser/Node releases the stream's network and memory resources; previously the reader kept reading/holding data.

**Action:** none — internal fix; no code, config, or dependency change. Only re-verify teardown behaviour of pending `HttpClient` requests in code that unmounts apps (expect the observable to complete silently rather than emit a response or error).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19.0.0 fetch.ts: loop had no destroyed check
await this.ngZone.runOutsideAngular(async () => {
  while (true) {
    const {done, value} = await reader.read();
    if (done) {
      break;
    }
// v20.0.0 fetch.ts
if (this.destroyed) {
  await reader.cancel();
  canceled = true;
  break;
}
...
if (canceled) {
  observer.complete();
  return;
}
```

**What changed:** `FetchBackend` in `@angular/common/http` now tracks teardown and stops streaming response bodies after the app is destroyed. v19's `fetch.ts` read the response `ReadableStream` in an unbounded `while (true) { await reader.read() }` loop; v20 injects `DestroyRef` (`private readonly destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);` plus a `destroyed` flag set in `this.destroyRef.onDestroy(...)`) and, inside the `ngZone.runOutsideAngular` read loop, checks `if (this.destroyed)` — then calls `await reader.cancel()`, sets `canceled = true`, and breaks. When `canceled`, it calls `observer.complete()` and returns *without* calling `parseBody` or emitting a final `HttpResponse`, so no chunks are combined/parsed and no `DownloadProgress`/`HttpResponse` events are emitted after teardown (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61354, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/common/http/src/fetch.ts vs https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/common/http/src/fetch.ts). The PR is labeled `action: merge, area: common, target: patch`.

**Affects:** Applications using `FetchBackend` (default `provideHttpClient(withFetch())`) that destroy the application (e.g. `PlatformRef.destroy()`, `TestBed.reset()`, unmounted micro-frontends, SSR) while an HTTP response body is still streaming, especially with `reportProgress`/`observe: 'events'` subscribers that relied on terminal events.

**Action:** none — internal behavioral fix; no code, config, or dependency change required. Re-verify tests or code that assert on the final `HttpResponse`/completion of requests that were in-flight when the injector/app was destroyed: such streams now complete silently instead of emitting a response built from partially read chunks.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19 (19.0.0) — read loop had no teardown guard
await this.ngZone.runOutsideAngular(async () => {
  while (true) {
    const {done, value} = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    chunks.push(value);
    // ...

// v20 (20.0.0)
if (this.destroyed) {
  await reader.cancel();
  canceled = true;
  break;
}
const {done, value} = await reader.read();
// ...
});

if (canceled) {
  observer.complete();
  return;
}
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` template type-checking engine (`TemplateTypeCheckerImpl`) no longer discards prior incremental results when a single file or component is requested. Previously, private methods `ensureAllShimsForOneFile` and `ensureShimForComponent` called `maybeAdoptPriorResultsForFile`, which only retained results for the specific file being processed, discarding prior results for other files. The fix introduces `maybeAdoptPriorResults()`, which iterates over all source files in `this.originalProgram.getSourceFiles()` and adopts prior complete type-checking results from `this.priorBuild.priorTypeCheckingResultsFor(sf)` into `this.state`, regardless of whether those files are the current request target. This ensures that all prior results are retained during incremental operations like single-file diagnostics or component-specific shim generation. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/107180260f2ac4ca8e8995e123f36944c8bec2f3)

**Affects:** All applications using incremental compilation or the Angular Language Service.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (in ensureShimForComponent and ensureAllShimsForOneFile)
this.maybeAdoptPriorResultsForFile(sf);

// After (in ensureAllShimsForAllFiles, ensureAllShimsForOneFile, and ensureShimForComponent)
this.maybeAdoptPriorResults();
```

**What changed:** When the Angular compiler emits runtime class metadata (i.e., the `setClassMetadata` calls), decorated members are now filtered by `ClassMemberAccessLevel`. ECMAScript private (`#`-prefixed) members containing decorators are excluded from the emitted `setClassMetadata` call. Previously, these members were included, which produced invalid JavaScript syntax (e.g., `{ #foo: [] }`) at runtime or build time because the class metadata emit structure does not support private fields (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61227, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1191e62d70ee16f3b083b635dd60a9f2e0c2d4c7). This class member usage is only permitted if standard ECMAScript decorators are used (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61227, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/61228).

**Affects:** Applications using standard ECMAScript decorators (TypeScript's `experimentalDecorators: false`) that apply Angular decorators to `#`-prefixed private fields (such as `#prop: ...;`). The Angular CLI development build config enables this behavior via class metadata emit for template type-checking (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/61228, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1191e62d70ee16f3b083b635dd60a9f2e0c2d4c7).

**Action:** required: re-verify Angular features applied to ECMAScript private (`#`) fields. Since they are excluded from metadata, any functionality depending on runtime reflection of these decorated fields (such as Angular DI, Input, or Output bindings) may no longer work as expected, despite eliminating the invalid syntax build error.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {Directive, Input} from '@angular/core';

@Directive() class Target {
  @Input() #privateName = 123;
  @Input() publicName = 456;
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, the internal implementation of `rxResource` (in `packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/rx_resource.ts`) now removes its `abort` listener (`params.abortSignal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort)`) inside the Observable's `error` notification handler, in addition to the existing removal in `complete`. Previously the `error` handler only did `send({error})`, so because an RxJS Observable terminates immediately on `error` (no further emissions or `complete` notification), the `onAbort` listener attached via `params.abortSignal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort)` was never cleaned up after an errored load — a memory-leak/leaked-listener bug (commit message: "we have to remove the `abort` listener in both the `error` and `complete` notifications") (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58306, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2b1b14f4d3751b9b3c351ddc412ecdcb2aea4781). The same fix was applied to the internal `HttpResourceImpl` in `packages/common/http/src/resource.ts` used by `httpResource` (diff on the commit page).

**Affects:** Applications using the experimental `rxResource` (or `httpResource`) from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`, particularly when resource loader Observables error; the public API is unchanged — this is a purely internal listener-cleanup fix.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// rx_resource.ts — before
error: (error) => send({error}),

// rx_resource.ts — after (Angular 20)
error: (error) => {
  send({error});
  params.abortSignal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
},
```

**What changed:** The `Testability` class in `@angular/core` now cleans up its `NgZone` subscriptions (`onUnstable` and `onStable`) when the application is destroyed. It optionally injects `DestroyRef` (if available in the injection context) and registers an `onDestroy` callback to unsubscribe from the zone events. Previously, these subscriptions were never explicitly unsubscribed, leading to memory leaks if subscriptions remained alive after app destruction (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8f9b05eaaabf14d7570fde16e26a73d69f78dc14, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/src/testability/testability.ts).

**Affects:** All applications using `Testability`, particularly those in test environments or where applications are destroyed and recreated, preventing memory leaks.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (19.0): Subscriptions created in _watchAngularEvents were not stored or unsubscribed.
this._ngZone.onUnstable.subscribe({ ... });

// After (20.0): Subscriptions are stored and unsubscribed via DestroyRef in _watchAngularEvents.
const onUnstableSubscription = this._ngZone.onUnstable.subscribe({ ... });
// ...
this._destroyRef?.onDestroy(() => {
  onUnstableSubscription.unsubscribe();
  onStableSubscription.unsubscribe();
});
```

**What changed:** This is an internal Angular core change (PR #61077, RC branch; patch port PR #61352, commit `eb53bda470`) that makes the SSR event-replay "stashing" logic tree-shakable and active only when `withEventReplay()` is invoked (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61077, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61352). Previously `setStashFn`/`clearStashFn` and a module-level `stashEventListeners` map lived in `render3/view/listeners.ts` / `render3/instructions/listener.ts`, and every listener registration eagerly looked up the stash function via `lView[INJECTOR].get(APP_ID)`, keeping the code in bundles even without event replay (the `stashEventListeners` symbol was in `bundle.golden_symbols.json`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/eb53bda470312d449039ef9b1494e3b6cc081e42.patch). Now `setStashFn`, `stashEventListenerImpl`, and a new `enableStashEventListenerImpl()` live in `event_delegation_utils.ts`; `_stashEventListenerImpl` is a no-op until `withEventReplay()` calls `enableStashEventListenerImpl()`, `setStashFn` returns a cleanup function that is run via `appRef.onDestroy(clearStashFn)`, and `stashEventListeners` was removed from the golden bundle symbol lists.

**Affects:** All applications upgrading to Angular 20.0.0, but there is no public API surface change — the moved symbols (`setStashFn`, `clearStashFn`, `stashEventListeners`, `EventCallback` in `view/listeners`) are internal, non-exported framework code. Behavior is only observable for SSR apps using `withEventReplay()` (listener stashing is now enabled lazily) and bundle size (event-replay stashing code is now tree-shaken out when `withEventReplay()` is not used).

**Action:** none. No application code, configuration, or dependency changes are needed; SSR apps using `withEventReplay()` should re-verify that events fired before hydration are still replayed correctly.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Internal Angular core code (illustrative of the change; not application code)
// Before (render3/instructions/listener.ts):
const appId = lView[INJECTOR].get(APP_ID);
const stashEventListener = stashEventListeners.get(appId);
stashEventListener?.(target as RElement, eventName, listenerFn);

// After (listener.ts delegates to event_delegation_utils.ts):
stashEventListenerImpl(lView, target, eventName, listenerFn);
// ...and withEventReplay() now calls:
enableStashEventListenerImpl();
const clearStashFn = setStashFn(appId, (rEl, eventName, listenerFn) => { /* ... */ });
appRef.onDestroy(clearStashFn);
```

**What changed:** In v20.0.0 the internal helper `resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists()` in `@angular/core/testing` (`packages/core/testing/src/fake_async.ts`) changed its guard from `if (fakeAsyncTestModule)` to `if (fakeAsyncTestModule && (Zone as any)['ProxyZoneSpec']?.isLoaded())`, so the fake-async zone is only reset when Zone.js's `ProxyZoneSpec` is actually loaded. Previously, if `zone.js` was on the page but the testing patches were not applied to the test framework (Zone has no patching of Vitest APIs, so `fakeAsync` cannot work automatically), this reset threw during test setup and broke every test. Now `core/testing` silently skips the reset in that situation (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/94f5a4b4d6ee195e05e7d2683ab386ee02d60a06, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61376, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/testing/src/fake_async.ts). Note the public `resetFakeAsyncZone()`, `fakeAsync()`, `tick()`, `flush()`, `discardPeriodicTasks()`, `flushMicrotasks()` still throw `zone-testing.js is needed for the fakeAsync() test helper but could not be found. Please make sure that your environment includes zone.js/testing` when the module is missing (same file).

**Affects:** Applications whose tests load `zone.js` while using a test framework that Zone does not patch (e.g. Vitest) and that use `@angular/core/testing`; the error path is removed, no API signature changes.

**Action:** none (behavioral fix; no code change required. Re-verify that Zone-patched test suites using `fakeAsync`/`tick` still reset correctly, and remember that Vitest + Zone still cannot use `fakeAsync` — the change only stops the thrown error.)

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (v19)
export function resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists(): void {
  if (fakeAsyncTestModule) {
    fakeAsyncTestModule.resetFakeAsyncZone();
  }
}

// after (v20)
export function resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists(): void {
  if (fakeAsyncTestModule && (Zone as any)['ProxyZoneSpec']?.isLoaded()) {
    fakeAsyncTestModule.resetFakeAsyncZone();
  }
}
```

**What changed:** `toSignal()` (from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`, file `packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_signal.ts`) used to register its unsubscription callback via `cleanupRef?.onDestroy(sub.unsubscribe.bind(sub))` and never removed it. When the Observable completed before the enclosing `DestroyRef`/injector was destroyed, the callback stayed registered, holding references and leaking memory (issue #61511 reported on 19.2.11). PR #61514 (commit `c0c69a5`) captures the unregister function — `destroyUnregisterFn = cleanupRef?.onDestroy(sub.unsubscribe.bind(sub));` — and calls `destroyUnregisterFn?.()` in a new `complete:` handler of the subscription, so the `onDestroy` hook is deregistered once the Observable completes (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c0c69a5abc7262887eaa1f0b84a6ec22be225994.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61514, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/61511).

**Affects:** Applications using `toSignal()` without `manualCleanup: true` on Observables that complete before the surrounding injector/view is destroyed, especially long-lived components creating many such signals.

