**What changed:** In server-side rendering, the public render APIs `renderModule` and `renderApplication` (from `@angular/platform-server`) gained a new optional `allowedHosts?: Readonly<string>[]` option in their `options` argument (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8569db88758d189544b03ec4474fd5334ff29346). When a parseable absolute `url` is passed, the hostname is now validated via `validateAllowedHosts` against the `allowedHosts` list; if it is not allowed, bootstrap throws `Host <url> is not allowed. You can configure \`allowedHosts\` option.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8569db88758d189544b03ec4474fd5334ff29346). Matching supports exact hostnames, wildcards like `*.example.com`, or `*` to allow all (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8569db88758d189544b03ec4474fd5334ff29346). Note the option is exposed on the `renderModule`/`renderApplication` options per the public-api golden; the 20.0.x `PlatformConfig` interface (`@angular/platform-server`) still only declares `document` and `url` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/platform-server/src/tokens.ts).

**Affects:** SSR applications that call `renderModule` or `renderApplication` directly from `@angular/platform-server` and pass a `url`; previously these calls were unchecked.

**Action:** required: add an `allowedHosts` array listing the valid hostnames (e.g. `allowedHosts: ['localhost']` or a wildcard like `['*.example.com']`, or `['*']` to allow all) to the `options` passed to `renderModule` / `renderApplication`, so the request `url` hostname passes host validation; otherwise rendering throws. Re-verify host validation when running behind a proxy or load balancer.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (no host validation)
renderApplication(bootstrap, {
  document: indexHtml,
  url: `${protocol}://${headers.host}${originalUrl}`,
});

// After (allowedHosts added)
renderApplication(bootstrap, {
  document: indexHtml,
  allowedHosts: ['localhost'],
  url: `${protocol}://${headers.host}${originalUrl}`,
});
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8569db88758d189544b03ec4474fd5334ff29346)

**What changed:** In `@angular/platform-server`, the internal `parseUrl(urlStr, origin)` helper in `packages/platform-server/src/location.ts` (used by the server `PlatformLocation` to resolve request URLs) was rewritten. Old behaviour concatenated the origin and url directly: `const urlToParse = urlStr.length === 0 || urlStr[0] === '/' ? origin + urlStr : urlStr;`. Because a browser/Node origin such as `` has no trailing slash, relative inputs (e.g. the hash-routing path `#/` produced by `provideRouter(withHashLocation())`) were mangled into ``, throwing `TypeError: Invalid URL` and hanging SSR builds (issue #68322). New behaviour: if `URL.canParse(urlStr)` the absolute URL is returned as-is ignoring origin; otherwise a missing leading `/` is added and the value is resolved against `origin` (`new URL(origin + urlStr)`) (commit diff, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/837a7102172502dd3d92793ec15b2d4e533a573d).

**Affects:** SSR applications (`@angular/platform-server` / `CommonEngine`, `renderApplication`, `renderModule`) that render URLs not starting with `/` — notably `provideRouter(withHashLocation())`, and any host that passes request URLs without a leading slash.

**Action:** none — the fix is internal and automatic; applications using hash location under SSR should just re-verify that server rendering completes without the `TypeError: Invalid URL`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
function parseUrl(urlStr: string, origin: string): URL {
  const urlToParse = urlStr.length === 0 || urlStr[0] === '/' ? origin + urlStr : urlStr;
  return new URL(urlToParse);
}

// after
export function parseUrl(urlStr: string, origin: string): URL {
  if (URL.canParse(urlStr)) {
    return new URL(urlStr);
  }
  if (urlStr && urlStr[0] !== '/') {
    urlStr = `/${urlStr}`;
  }
  return new URL(origin + urlStr);
}
```

**What changed:** The `NgOptimizedImage` directive (from `@angular/common`) now escapes the value used in the generated CSS `url(...)` for its `placeholder` attribute to prevent CSS/content injection. In the internal `generatePlaceholder` method, the previously unescaped `url(<value>)` output became a quoted and escaped `url("<value>")`, where `<value>` is produced by a new `escapeCssUrl(input: string): string` helper that performs `input.replace(/\\/g, '\\\\').replace(/"/g, '\\"')`. This applies both when `placeholder="true"` (the value comes from the configured image loader) and when `placeholder` is given as a string URL. Generated test cases confirm output such as `url(""); color: red;")` where injection characters are now neutralized (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/17326725baae510a420b9a22b4c2d25927dd91b4)

**Affects:** Applications that set `placeholder` on the `NgOptimizedImage` directive (`ngSrc`), either as `placeholder="true"` or as a URL string, and especially those whose `imageLoader` returns placeholder URLs containing `"` or `\`.

**Action:** required: re-verify rendering of `NgOptimizedImage` placeholder `background-image` output and inspect any `imageLoader` configured with `placeholderResolution` that returns URLs containing `"` or `\`, since such values are now escaped in the generated `url("...")`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// ng_optimized_image.ts (NgOptimizedImage.generatePlaceholder)
// before
return `url(${placeholderInput})`;
// after
return `url("${escapeCssUrl(placeholderInput)}")`;
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 changed the compiler's and core's DOM security schema so that dynamic bindings to the href and xlink:href attributes on `<svg><a>` (`<svg:a>`) elements are registered under `SecurityContext.URL`, which enables template compilation to emit runtime URL sanitization checks (`ɵɵsanitizeUrl`) for these attributes. Previously these attributes were unmapped in the DOM security schema and bypassed sanitization, allowing `javascript:` URLs (the commit describes this as an XSS risk) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2e3d0371ab7726cec2979d8b8dc8f2a8393f9591). Static (non-bound) `href`/`xlink:href` values remain allowed as-is, per the test case verifying static `href="javascript:alert(1)"` stays unchanged (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2e3d0371ab7726cec2979d8b8dc8f2a8393f9591.patch).

**Affects:** applications that bind a dynamic value (`[attr.href]` or `[attr.xlink:href]`) to an `<a>` element inside an SVG (SVG namespace).

**Action:** required: re-verify any SVG `<a>` link bindings that pass intentionally non-`http(s)`/safe URLs, because Angular v20 now prefixes unsafe schemes with `unsafe:` (e.g. a bound `javascript:alert(1)` becomes `unsafe:javascript:alert(1)`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
@Component({
  template: '<svg><a [attr.href]="url"></a></svg>',
})
class TestCmp { url = 'javascript:alert(1)'; }
// bound href becomes 'unsafe:javascript:alert(1)'
```

**What changed:** The template preparser (`preparseElement` in `packages/compiler/src/template_parser/template_preparser.ts`) previously only matched the plain tag names `style` and `script`, so namespaced internal tag names `:svg:script` (and `:svg:style`) were not classified as `PreparsedElementType.SCRIPT`/`STYLE` and were not stripped from compiled templates. The fix replaces the `STYLE_ELEMENT`/`SCRIPT_ELEMENT` constants with `SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script'])` and `STYLE_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:style', 'style'])`, so namespaced SVG `<script>` elements are now stripped during template compilation (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fe1207e8c5d2e55dd7a3a77a3caeb12f91a409bf.patch). Because such elements never reach compiled output, the `SecurityContext.RESOURCE_URL` mappings for `['script', ['src', 'href', 'xlink:href']]` in the SVG namespace were removed from `SECURITY_SCHEMA()` in `packages/compiler/src/schema/dom_security_schema.ts`, and the `script`/`:svg:script` entries were removed from the runtime `RESOURCE_MAP` in `packages/core/src/sanitization/sanitization.ts` (same patch). Tests show that bindings like `<svg><script [attr.xlink:href]="attr" [attr.href]="attr"></script></svg>` no longer generate `ɵɵsanitizeResourceUrl` sanitizer calls, and `<svg><script src=""></script></svg>` renders no `script` element. This commit is not listed in the v20.0.0 release notes breaking-changes section (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0), and it relates to the SVG-script XSS advisory GHSA-jrmj-c5cx-3cw6 / CVE-2026-22610.

**Affects:** Applications whose component templates contain `<script>` (or `<style>`) elements inside an SVG namespace — e.g. `<svg><script src="..."></script></svg>` or bindings to `attr.href` / `attr.xlink:href` on an SVG script — where previously the element (and its generated `ɵɵsanitizeResourceUrl` bindings) survived compilation.

**Action:** required: re-verify any template that intentionally rendered an SVG `<script>` element or relied on the old sanitizer output for `href`/`xlink:href` on `script`; such elements are now removed from compiled templates and no `<script>` sanitizer is emitted. Dynamically mounting a component onto a `<script>` host element throws `"<script>" tag is not allowed as a component host element`.

**Before/after:**
```diff
-const STYLE_ELEMENT = 'style';
-const SCRIPT_ELEMENT = 'script';
+const STYLE_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:style', 'style']);
+const SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script']);
...
-  } else if (nodeName == STYLE_ELEMENT) {
+  } else if (STYLE_ELEMENTS.has(nodeName)) {
     type = PreparsedElementType.STYLE;
-  } else if (nodeName == SCRIPT_ELEMENT) {
+  } else if (SCRIPT_ELEMENTS.has(nodeName)) {
     type = PreparsedElementType.SCRIPT;

-    registerContext(SecurityContext.RESOURCE_URL, SVG_NAMESPACE, [
-      ['script', ['src', 'href', 'xlink:href']],
-    ]);
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds a runtime guard in core's `locateHostElement` (`packages/core/src/render3/instructions/shared.ts`): after `renderer.selectRootElement(elementOrSelector, preserveContent)` resolves the host, if `rootElement.tagName.toLowerCase() === 'script'` it throws `new RuntimeError(RuntimeErrorCode.UNSAFE_VALUE_IN_SCRIPT, ...)` with the dev-mode message `"<script>" tag is not allowed as a component host element.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c6bb0692e2f6fd8f7e59e60fa964d173c60e3a74, PR https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/68713). The commit message states this prevents accidental or malicious script execution when "dynamically mounting a component via `createComponent` directly onto a `<script>` element"; SVG `<script>` elements are also rejected, and the error message is tree-shakable in production builds where `ngDevMode` is disabled. Note a later hardening (follow-up PR #70089, "reject prefixed SVG script hosts") plus CVE-2026-52725 show the original 20.0.0 check was incomplete for namespaced/prefixed script elements, patched in 20.3.22.

**Affects:** Applications that mount components dynamically onto a host that resolves to a `<script>` (or `<svg:script>`) element — e.g. passing a `'script'` selector string or a `<script>` DOM element to the standalone `createComponent()` function, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, or `bootstrapApplication`/component bootstrapping whose root selector matches a script tag. In v20.0.0–20.3.x such mounting throws in dev mode; a bypass remained until 20.3.22 (CVE-2026-52725).

**Action:** required: audit calls to `createComponent` / `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` and component selectors for hosts that could resolve to a `<script>` element and change them to a non-script host element; re-verify dynamic-component mounting tests (the throw is dev-mode only, so production builds are silently unaffected). Since the 20.0.0 guard was bypassable via namespaced script elements, also verify the app runs on `@angular/core` >= 20.3.22 rather than 20.0.0.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Angular 20 (packages/core/src/render3/instructions/shared.ts)
const rootElement = renderer.selectRootElement(elementOrSelector, preserveContent);
if (rootElement.tagName.toLowerCase() === 'script') {
  throw new RuntimeError(
    RuntimeErrorCode.UNSAFE_VALUE_IN_SCRIPT,
    ngDevMode && `"<script>" tag is not allowed as a component host element.`,
  );
}
```
```ts
// Angular 19 — no check; mounting onto a <script> host was allowed
const rootElement = renderer.selectRootElement(elementOrSelector, preserveContent);
```

**What changed:** The `Meta` service in `@angular/platform-browser` now escapes values used to build CSS attribute selectors and filters query results, preventing CSS-selector injection through meta tag values. In `packages/platform-browser/src/browser/meta.ts`: (1) `Meta._parseSelector()` now returns `` `${attr}=${this._escapeSelectorValue(String(tag[attr]))}` `` via a new private `_escapeSelectorValue()` that escapes backslashes and double quotes (`value.replace(/\\/g, '\\\\').replace(/"/g, '\\"')`) instead of the previous raw `` `${attr}="${tag[attr]}"` `` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3960b2155847c52e4f56d4b3c6532be700155891). (2) `getTag()` and `getTags()` now only return elements whose `nodeName.toLowerCase() === 'meta'`, so a payload like `'name="description"], body, meta[name="pwned"'` passed as the `selector` argument of `updateTag` (or as an `attrSelector`) can no longer break out of the `meta[{attrSelector}]` query built on `document.querySelector` and match arbitrary elements such as `body` (https://angular.dev/api/platform-browser/Meta documents this `meta[{attrSelector}]` format).

**Affects:** Applications passing attacker- or user-controlled strings as `name`/`property` values of `MetaDefinition` to `Meta.addTag`/`addTags`/`updateTag`/`removeTag`, or as the `attrSelector`/`selector` strings to `getTag`/`getTags`/`updateTag`/`removeTag`. Values containing `"` or `\` (e.g. `property: 'fb:app_id"][content="123456789'`) previously matched unintended tags/elements; now they are confined to a single meta element.

**Action:** required: re-verify meta tag management via `Meta` — confirm no code relies on quotes/backslashes inside `name`/`property` values (or hand-built `attrSelector` strings) matching other elements or attributes; such selectors now match only the escaped literal value on `meta` elements.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (Angular 19)
private _parseSelector(tag: MetaDefinition): string {
  const attr: string = tag.name ? 'name' : 'property';
  return `${attr}="${tag[attr]}"`;
}

// after (Angular 20)
private _parseSelector(tag: MetaDefinition): string {
  const attr: string = tag.name ? 'name' : 'property';
  return `${attr}=${this._escapeSelectorValue(String(tag[attr]))}`;
}

private _escapeSelectorValue(value: string): string {
  // Escape backslashes and double quotes to prevent CSS selector injection.
  return `"${value.replace(/\\/g, '\\\\').replace(/"/g, '\\"')}"`;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 (patch commit d31f84116c) wraps the `elementPropertyInternal` call inside `applyUpdateOpCodes` in `packages/core/src/render3/i18n/i18n_apply.ts` within a `setSelectedIndex(nodeIndex)` try/finally block, restoring the previous `getSelectedIndex()` value afterwards. This coordinates input/property reflection lookups with the correct element node at runtime, preventing ICU and renderer element dynamic update errors. A guard was also added to `locateHostElement` to check for defined targets during dynamic targetless testing contexts. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d31f84116cafcf48462c2b230fa9c4469761292a)
**Affects:** Applications using i18n with dynamic element property updates inside ICU messages or dynamic targetless testing contexts that previously encountered renderer or reflection lookup errors due to incorrect active index states.
**Action:** required: re-verify i18n dynamic element property update behavior and ICU dynamic update rendering, ensuring inputs/properties reflect correctly with the active element index.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
elementPropertyInternal(
  tView,
  tNodeOrTagName,
  lView,
  propName,
  value,
  lView[RENDERER],
  sanitizeFn,
  false,
);

// After
const prevSelectedIndex = getSelectedIndex();
setSelectedIndex(nodeIndex);
try {
  elementPropertyInternal(
    tView,
    tNodeOrTagName,
    lView,
    propName,
    value,
    lView[RENDERER],
    sanitizeFn,
    false,
  );
} finally {
  setSelectedIndex(prevSelectedIndex);
}
```

**What changed:** `@angular/common` HttpTransferCache in `@angular/platform-browser` (`withHttpTransferCache()`) previously skipped `Cookie`/`Authorization` header requests by default but included them when `includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders: true`; it did **not** look at `HttpRequest.withCredentials`, so requests carrying credentials via `withCredentials` (without those headers set on the request object) were cached (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9940ffd781de4da118848eabeeedb765f4747416). The transfer cache check now unconditionally excludes all requests sent with the `withCredentials` flag, even when `includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders` is set to true, to keep user-specific responses out of the cache (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9940ffd781de4da118848eabeeedb765f4747416).

**Affects:** SSR/hydration applications that enable HttpTransferCache and issue HTTP requests with `withCredentials: true` (e.g. cookie/session-based calls to a different origin); previously such responses could be transferred to and reused by the client.

**Action:** required: re-verify that any response fetched with `withCredentials: true` is now refetched on the client instead of being served from the transfer cache, so those requests are re-issued after hydration. No code/dependency change is documented as required.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Request now always excluded from HttpTransferCache, regardless of
// includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders (commit 9940ffd):
this.http.get('/api/user', { withCredentials: true });
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0, the `HttpTransferCache` behavior was modified to skip caching requests that contain a `Cookie` header by default. Previously, only requests with `Authorization` or `Proxy-Authorization` headers were excluded from the cache when `includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders` was false (the default). The `hasAuthHeaders` function in `packages/common/http/src/transfer_cache.ts` was updated to return `true` if the request headers include `authorization`, `proxy-authorization`, or `cookie` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0f67f0b9627f67d55b857f17d6796bea89d88765). This prevents user-specific, cookie-dependent responses from being cached on the server and incorrectly served to other users during hydration. The JSDoc for `HttpTransferCacheOptions.includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders` was updated to reflect that it enables caching for requests containing `Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, or `Cookie` headers (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0f67f0b9627f67d55b857f17d6796bea89d88765).

**Affects:** Applications using Angular SSR with `HttpTransferCache` enabled that send `GET` or `HEAD` requests containing a `Cookie` header.

**Action:** required: Re-verify the behavior of HTTP requests with `Cookie` headers in SSR contexts. If the application relied on these requests being cached and transferred to the client, explicitly set `includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders: true` in the `HttpTransferCacheOptions` provided via `provideClientHydration()` or `withHttpTransferCache()`. Note that this option affects `Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, and `Cookie` headers collectively.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/common/http/src/transfer_cache.ts

// Before
function hasAuthHeaders(req: HttpRequest<unknown>): boolean {
  return req.headers.has('authorization') || req.headers.has('proxy-authorization');
}

// After
function hasAuthHeaders(req: HttpRequest<unknown>): boolean {
  return (
    req.headers.has('authorization') ||
    req.headers.has('proxy-authorization') ||
    req.headers.has('cookie')
  );
}
```

**What changed:** `ServerPlatformLocation` path parsing in `parseUrl` normalizes multiple consecutive leading slashes and backslashes (e.g. `//`, `/\`, `\\`) down to a single `/`, replacing a prior check that only prefixed a leading `/` when `urlStr[0] !== '/'`. This ensures consistent resolution of relative path segments and prevents unexpected absolute path overrides during URL parsing (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d187e8aedaf7c79c9837849ab5f0b775344b14b5). This normalization is not listed among the v20.0.0 Breaking Changes (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/CHANGELOG.md).
**Affects:** SSR applications that pass URLs beginning with multiple slashes or backslash-slash combinations (e.g. `//example.com/...`, `/\example.com/...`) into `ServerPlatformLocation` / `platformServer()` via the `INITIAL_CONFIG` `url`.
**Action:** required: re-verify SSR URL/pathname resolution for any code path that supplies request URLs with multiple leading slashes or backslashes; such URLs now resolve as relative path segments rather than protocol-relative absolute overrides. No code, dependency or configuration changes are stated on the cited pages for the general case.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/platform-server/src/location.ts
// Before:
if (urlStr && urlStr[0] !== '/') {
  urlStr = `/${urlStr}`;
}
// After:
const LEADING_SLASHES_REGEX = /^[/\\]+/;
if (urlStr) {
  urlStr = urlStr.replace(LEADING_SLASHES_REGEX, '/');
  if (urlStr[0] !== '/') {
    urlStr = `/${urlStr}`;
  }
}
```

**What changed:** `@angular/platform-server` consolidated SSR URL handling into a new unified `parseUrl` helper in `packages/platform-server/src/url.ts` (moved out of `location.ts`). It now: collapses multiple consecutive leading slashes/backslashes (`//` or `/\`) to a single `/` so path-like inputs can't be parsed as protocol-relative URLs and hijack the origin; throws `Invalid URL: <url>` for malformed absolute URLs (e.g. ``, `http://[google.com]/path`, ``) that lenient parsers like Domino accept but WHATWG rejects, closing SSRF / `allowedHosts` bypasses; and passes the fully parsed/normalized URL to `parseDocument` (falling back to `parseUrl(config.url, '').href`) instead of raw `config.url`, preventing virtual document hostname adoption. `validateAllowedHosts` in `utils.ts` now uses `parseUrl` instead of `URL.canParse`/`new URL` (commit c75f60ef8a, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c75f60ef8a7a690c47dc7c473ee214c5f166d281).

**Affects:** SSR applications passing attacker-controllable request URLs to `renderApplication`, `renderModule`, or `INITIAL_CONFIG` `url`, especially those using `allowedHosts` or relying on `PlatformLocation.hostname` / relative HttpClient calls during SSR; malformed URLs that previously rendered now throw.

**Action:** required: re-verify SSR rendering — confirm server code only passes valid, sanitized request URLs to `renderApplication`/`renderApplication` options and `INITIAL_CONFIG`, that `allowedHosts` still rejects disallowed hosts, and that `document.location` / `PlatformLocation` values are the expected origin for URLs with leading `//` or `/\` (these now resolve as paths under the configured origin).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// after — packages/platform-server/src/url.ts
if (/^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+.-]*:(\/\/|\\\\)/.test(urlStr)) {
  throw new Error(`Invalid URL: ${urlStr}`);
}
let normalizedPath = urlStr.replace(LEADING_SLASHES_REGEX, '/');
if (normalizedPath[0] !== '/') {
  normalizedPath = `/${normalizedPath}`;
}
return new URL(normalizedPath, origin);
```

**What changed:** The `@angular/service-worker` package internal helper `newRequestWithMetadata()` in `packages/service-worker/worker/src/assets.ts` was changed to preserve the request's `redirect` mode (`options.redirect`) when reconstructing network requests, whereas previously only `headers` were preserved (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/37ee9ffd9eb7c51913dba80e37b2f55950d8436b). This fixes a bug where a client request built with `redirect: 'error'` (for a redirected lazy asset) was rebuilt by the service worker into a request with the default `redirect: 'follow'`, thereby defeating the caller's explicit policy and letting the service worker follow redirects it was supposed to reject. The commit is dated 2026-03-06, which is after the 20.0.0 release line, so the corrected behaviour is not present in 20.0.0 itself.

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/service-worker` whose assets are served through redirect responses, and only when a request explicitly sets `redirect: 'error'`. The `ngsw-config.json` `assetGroups` schema exposes no `redirect` option, so this only affects requests issued with an explicit `RequestInit` redirect policy.

**Action:** none for the 20.0.0 upgrade (the fix is a bug fix landed outside this hop); re-verify that service-worker-served assets which return HTTP redirects still cache and load correctly.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (newRequestWithMetadata): preserved only headers
return this.adapter.newRequest(url, { headers: options.headers });

// After (newRequestWithMetadata): also preserves redirect policy
return this.adapter.newRequest(url, {
  headers: options.headers,
  redirect: options.redirect,
});
```

**What changed:** The internal `AssetGroup` method `newRequestWithMetadata(url: string, options: RequestInit)` in `packages/service-worker/worker/src/assets.ts` previously created reconstructed asset requests preserving only `headers` and `redirect`, explicitly not preserving credentials ("Things like credential inclusion are intentionally omitted to avoid issues with opaque responses.") (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/97f796203f0605f9a168b76e7ac2f1e6b8d3ddc4.patch). Commit 97f796203f adds: `if (options.credentials === 'omit') { init.credentials = 'omit'; }`, so an explicitly passed `credentials: 'omit'` on an asset request is now forwarded when the service worker rebuilds the request (including across redirects); `credentials: 'same-origin'` and `credentials: 'include'` are still not preserved, and unknown options remain dropped (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/97f796203f0605f9a168b76e7ac2f1e6b8d3ddc4.patch). Note: the 20.0.0 GitHub release notes list no `service-worker` entry for this change (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0), so the commit's presence in the 20.0.0 hop cannot be confirmed from the fetched release page.
**Affects:** applications using `@angular/service-worker` where asset requests are issued with `RequestInit` containing `credentials: 'omit'`; no application code, template, or config changes are required.
**Action:** required: re-verify that asset requests configured with `credentials: 'omit'` still reach the server without cookies/`Authorization` headers, including when the asset request is served across a redirect (behavior now preserved by the fix).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/service-worker/worker/src/assets.ts - AssetGroup.newRequestWithMetadata()
// before
return this.adapter.newRequest(url, {
  headers: options.headers,
  redirect: options.redirect,
});

// after
const init: RequestInit = {
  headers: options.headers,
  redirect: options.redirect,
};

if (options.credentials === 'omit') {
  init.credentials = 'omit';
}

return this.adapter.newRequest(url, init);
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/service-worker`, `AssetGroup`'s private request-reconstruction helper `newRequestWithMetadata` now preserves the HTTP cache mode (`cache`) from an incoming request when it builds the outbound fetch request for an asset. Previously only `headers`, `redirect policy`, and an explicit `credentials: 'omit'` were preserved, so an explicit `cache` value (e.g. `no-store`) on the incoming request was dropped and effectively replaced by the default `'default'`. Now, if `options.cache !== undefined`, `init.cache = options.cache` is set, so the explicit cache mode is forwarded, including across redirect reconstruction (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/561912093104f1a3f07eedae83edaf63650beb84). A code comment notes `cache: 'only-if-cached'` with `mode !== 'same-origin'` is already short-circuited earlier in `Driver.onFetch()`, so it needs no special handling (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/561912093104f1a3f07eedae83edaf63650beb84).

**Affects:** Applications registered with the Angular service worker that request assets matching an `assetGroups` entry (or `dataGroups` assets handled by the worker) using an explicit HTTP cache mode such as `cache: 'no-store'`, `cache: 'reload'`, `cache: 'no-cache'`, or `cache: 'force-cache'`; the change is observable when the asset is not already cached and must be fetched from the network.

**Action:** none. No code, configuration, dependency, or build change is required. Applications that intentionally pass an explicit cache mode on asset requests should re-verify that those network fetches now honor the requested cache mode instead of the browser default.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// newRequestWithMetadata (packages/service-worker/worker/src/assets.ts)
// After: the incoming request's cache mode is forwarded
if (options.cache !== undefined) {
  init.cache = options.cache;
}
return this.adapter.newRequest(url, init);
```

**What changed:** In the v20.3.x patch line (20.3.22), a security fix to the template preparser made namespaced SVG tags match element sets, so `<style>` inside `<svg>` — internally named `:svg:style` — was added to `STYLE_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:style', 'style'])` in `packages/compiler/src/template_parser/template_preparser.ts` and classified as `PreparsedElementType.STYLE`. That caused such elements to be "completely stripped from the final template DOM tree during the Render3 template transform and pushed into standard component stylesheets", so icons/`<defs>` content rendered incorrectly — reported in issue #68977 after updating to "the latest v20 version (v20.3.22)" (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/68977). Commit `6ea6379123a7b0e191f8b9f66dfb74ca29659a6c` reverted this: the set was replaced by `const STYLE_ELEMENT = 'style'` and the check became `} else if (STYLE_ELEMENT === nodeName) {`, while `SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script'])` was left intact, so namespaced SVG style tags "remain safely in the template AST as normal DOM elements, preserving local SVG styling" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/6ea6379123a7b0e191f8b9f66dfb74ca29659a6c).

**Affects:** Component templates containing `<style>` elements nested inside `<svg>` (e.g. inside `<defs>`) that were compiled with the affected v20.3.22 compiler; applications on 20.0.0 through 20.3.21 are unaffected, and a plain top-level `<style></style>` in a template is still ignored by the transform.

**Action:** none — this is a compiler regression fixed within the v20 patch line; verify that SVG markup with embedded `<style>` still appears in the rendered DOM (no code, config or dependency changes described in the sources).

**Before/after:**
```diff
-const STYLE_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:style', 'style']);
+const STYLE_ELEMENT = 'style';
 const SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script']);
 ...
-  } else if (STYLE_ELEMENTS.has(nodeName)) {
+  } else if (STYLE_ELEMENT === nodeName) {
     type = PreparsedElementType.STYLE;
```

**What changed:** `@angular/platform-server` now performs strict validation on resolved URLs (in `parseUrl`, used by `ServerPlatformLocation`). If a resolved URL starts with `//` (protocol-relative), an error is thrown unless the input explicitly permits it via `allowProtocolRelative` (defaults to false). Additionally, if a relative or obfuscated URL resolves to a different origin than the provided base origin, Angular throws error NG05703 ("Suspicious URL Origin Change"). This change protects against SSRF and Open Redirect vulnerabilities. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/69018)

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/platform-server` for SSR, especially those where URL resolution may involve user-supplied inputs or paths resulting in protocol-relative (`//`) or cross-origin changes. This specifically impacts the use of `renderApplication` or `renderModule` when they trigger internal URL resolution via `ServerPlatformLocation`.

**Action:** If the application intentionally uses protocol-relative URLs in SSR, ensure that the `allowProtocolRelative` option is set to true where applicable. For other cases, review application logic to ensure that URLs passed to Angular's SSR environment do not inadvertently attempt to shift the origin or use suspicious formatting. (https://angular.dev/errors/NG05703)

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Behavior change:
// Before: Resolved URLs could become //other-origin/path or shift origins without immediate exception
// After: Resolved URLs starting with // or shifting origins unexpectedly cause errors unless explicitly allowed

// If an SSR resolution previously yielded //evil.com:
// Now throws error NG05703 or a generic URL error, preventing SSRF/redirects.
// To intentionally allow // relative URLs in SSR:
// ensure allowProtocolRelative is set to true in the relevant context.
```

**What changed:** `NodeInjectorDestroyRef.onDestroy` now checks whether the underlying `lView` is already destroyed; if so, it invokes the callback immediately instead of throwing (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5f7f04634f57c0fb669e2b81c85a0dff972868d8, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58008). Previously `takeUntilDestroyed` would throw `NG0911: View has already been destroyed` at `storeLViewOnDestroy` / `NodeInjectorDestroyRef.onDestroy` when subscribing after view teardown (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/54527). (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58008)
**Affects:** applications using `takeUntilDestroyed` or `DestroyRef.onDestroy` where the operator is subscribed (or the callback registered) after the component/directive's `lView` has already been destroyed.
**Action:** none — the new behavior silently runs the cleanup callback instead of throwing `NG0911`; re-verify subscription-completion behavior in such cases.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/take_until_destroyed.ts (unchanged across 19.0/20.0)
const destroyed$ = new Observable<void>((observer) => {
  const unregisterFn = destroyRef!.onDestroy(observer.next.bind(observer));
  return unregisterFn;
});

return <T>(source: Observable<T>) => {
  return source.pipe(takeUntil(destroyed$));
};
```

