**What changed:** The `InjectFlags` enum from `@angular/core` (members `Default`, `Host`, `Self`, `SkipSelf`, `Optional`) was deprecated in v14.1 and is now removed in Angular 20.0.0, together with every API that accepted it as an argument: `inject` no longer accepts `InjectFlags`, `Injector.get` no longer accepts `InjectFlags`, `EnvironmentInjector.get` no longer accepts `InjectFlags`, and `TestBed.get` / `TestBed.inject` no longer accept `InjectFlags` (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0). Flags such as `InjectFlags.Optional | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.SkipSelf` are replaced by the options-object form `InjectOptions`, whose fields are `optional`, `skipSelf`, `self`, `host` (https://v19.angular.dev/api/core/InjectOptions). Note the old `InjectFlags.Default` ("Check self and check parent injector if needed") has no options-object field — it was the default behaviour (https://v19.angular.dev/api/core/InjectFlags). A separate removal in the same release is `TestBed.get`, replaced by `TestBed.inject`. Angular ships a migration schematic that rewrites the flag form to the options object during `ng update` (https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new).

**Affects:** Application, library and spec code that imports `InjectFlags` from `@angular/core` or passes a flag/bitwise flag combination as the second argument to `inject()`, `Injector.get()`, `EnvironmentInjector.get()`, `TestBed.get()` or `TestBed.inject()`. Code that already uses `InjectOptions` object literals, or that omits the flags argument, is unaffected.

**Action:** required: Run `ng update` so the bundled migration schematic converts the flag calls to the options-object syntax; then remove all remaining `InjectFlags` imports and replace each flag argument with an `InjectOptions` literal — `InjectFlags.Optional` → `{ optional: true }`, `InjectFlags.SkipSelf` → `{ skipSelf: true }`, `InjectFlags.Self` → `{ self: true }`, `InjectFlags.Host` → `{ host: true }`, and combined flags → one object with each property set to `true` (e.g. `{ optional: true, host: true, skipSelf: true }`). Drop `InjectFlags.Default` entirely (no equivalent property). Also replace any `TestBed.get(...)` call with `TestBed.inject(...)`, since `TestBed.get` was removed in the same release. Because `optional: true` types the result as `T | null`, re-verify null handling/typing (the docs' `inject()` migration shows a trailing `!` for previously non-null-typed results) (https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new, ).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19 and earlier)
import { inject, InjectFlags, Directive, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
@Directive()
export class Dir {
  el = inject(ElementRef, InjectFlags.Optional | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.SkipSelf);
}

// After (Angular 20 - options-object syntax)
import { inject, Directive, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
@Directive()
export class Dir {
  el = inject(ElementRef, { optional: true, host: true, skipSelf: true });
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0, `TestBed.get` was removed from the public API of `@angular/core/testing`. It had been deprecated since Angular v9 and, per the PR description, "isn't type safe and has been deprecated for several years now", so it was dropped from the public API (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60414 — commit "remove TestBed.get (#60414)"). The v20 release notes state: "`TestBed.get` has been removed. Use `TestBed.inject` instead." (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0). Related removals in the same release: `InjectFlags` was removed and `TestBed.get` / `TestBed.inject` "no longer accepts `InjectFlags`" — the replacement is an `InjectOptions` object, which is the signature now documented for `TestBed.inject(token: ProviderToken<T>, notFoundValue?: T, options?: InjectOptions & {optional?: false})` in the v20+ API reference (https://angular.dev/api/testing/TestBed). Because `TestBed.get` was typed loosely (`get<T>(token: ProviderToken<T>|string, notFoundValue?: any)`-style / `any`-returning) while `TestBed.inject` is generic on the token, call sites can newly surface type errors in test specs.

**Affects:** Test files (typically `*.spec.ts`) that call `TestBed.get(...)`, including third-party/spec helpers imported from `@angular/core/testing` or `@angular/core`.

**Action:** required: replace every `TestBed.get(...)` call in test code with `TestBed.inject(...)`, and re-verify that the injected instance's inferred type still matches subsequent assertions/property access (add an explicit type argument if the compiler complains). Do not pass an `InjectFlags` value (`Optional`, `SkipSelf`, `Self`, `Default`) as the second/third argument, since `InjectFlags` is also removed in v20 — use `TestBed.inject(token, notFoundValue, {optional: true})` style options per the `InjectOptions` signature. Re-run `ng test` after the change.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (Angular ≤ 19)
const service = TestBed.get(MyService);

// after (Angular 20)
const service = TestBed.inject(MyService);
```

**What changed:** `AsyncPipe` now directly catches unhandled errors in subscriptions and promises and reports them to the application's `ErrorHandler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60057). Previously, `AsyncPipe` would leave promise rejections unhandled and subscription errors uncaught. In Zone-based applications, errors inside the Angular Zone were caught by ZoneJS and reported to `ErrorHandler` so the result is generally the same. However, in zoneless applications, these errors were never caught or reported by the framework and could reach the node process in SSR and cause it to shut down (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60057). The change to the exact mechanism for reporting can result in differences in test environments that will require test updates (https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0).
**Affects:** All applications using `AsyncPipe` with Observables or Promises that may emit errors, particularly zoneless applications or SSR setups. Also affects tests where `AsyncPipe` errors are triggered, as `TestBed` rethrows unexpected errors by default.
**Action:** required: Verify that custom `ErrorHandler` implementations correctly receive and handle errors from `AsyncPipe` subscriptions and promises. Update tests that may now fail or log errors due to the change in reporting mechanism; if errors are intentional for a test case, catch them or use `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `configureTestingModule`. Ensure SSR applications handle promise rejections to prevent node process shutdowns, as `AsyncPipe` forwards these errors to `ErrorHandler`.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// No code change required in AsyncPipe usage, but ErrorHandler behavior changes
// Before: Errors in AsyncPipe subscriptions/promises were not directly caught by AsyncPipe in zoneless apps.
// After: AsyncPipe directly catches these errors and passes them to ErrorHandler.
```

**What changed:** PR #60993 introduced `TestBed.tick()` (in `@angular/core/testing`, marked `@publicApi 20.0`, "Execute any pending work required to synchronize model to the UI") with `BREAKING CHANGE: the TestBed.flushEffects()` was removed - use the `TestBed.tick()` instead.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60993). It was then re-introduced in the same major via PR #61462 `refactor(core): re-introduce TestBed.flushEffects()`: "Our intention was to remove TestBed.flushEffects() in Angular v20 as it was a developer preview API… the angular.dev documentation wasn't displaying the @developerPreview annotation properly… This commits re-introduces the TestBed.flushEffects() API. This API call will delegate to TestBed.tick()." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61462). At the 20.0.0 tag, `TestBed.flushEffects()` is declared `@deprecated use TestBed.tick() instead` and both the static and instance versions just call `TestBedImpl.INSTANCE.tick()` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/testing/src/test_bed.ts). `TestBed.tick()` mirrors `ApplicationRef.tick()` — change detection plus root/component effects — instead of only flushing root effects, so it is not a drop-in replacement and tests relying on the narrower old behavior can break (https://cookbook.marmicode.io/angular/testing/flushing-flusheffects).

**Affects:** Unit tests that call `TestBed.flushEffects()` (or that call it on a fixture-less `TestBed` to run root effects only), especially tests that asserted state mid-synchronization without running change detection.

**Action:** required: replace every `TestBed.flushEffects()` call with `TestBed.tick()` (same file, no import change — both are on `TestBed` from `@angular/core/testing`), and re-run affected specs: `tick()` now also runs change detection and component effects, so assertions made between the old `flushEffects()` and a later `fixture.detectChanges()` may now observe synchronized DOM/state earlier. The deprecated `flushEffects()` still exists in 20.x as a thin alias for `tick()`, so leaving it in place compiles but emits a deprecation and already changes behaviour.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19): flushEffects only ran root effects
TestBed.flushEffects();
fixture.detectChanges();

// After (Angular 20): TestBed.tick() from @angular/core/testing
// "Execute any pending work required to synchronize model to the UI."
TestBed.tick();
```
```ts
// Angular 20.0.0 test_bed.ts — flushEffects is deprecated and delegates to tick()
/** @deprecated use `TestBed.tick()` instead */
flushEffects(): void;
/** @publicApi 20.0 */
tick(): void;

static flushEffects(): void {
  return TestBedImpl.INSTANCE.tick();
}
```

