**What changed:** The `TestBed.get` method (from `@angular/core/testing`) was removed from Angular's public API in v20.0.0. It had been deprecated for several years (since v9, replaced by the type-safe `TestBed.inject`), and PR #60414 removed it entirely: "`TestBed.get` isn't type safe and has been deprecated for several years now. These changes remove it from the public API. BREAKING CHANGE: * `TestBed.get` has been removed. Use `TestBed.inject` instead." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60414). The current `TestBed` API now only exposes `inject`, `runInInjectionContext`, `tick`, etc., with no `get` method (https://angular.dev/api/core/testing/TestBed). The removal commit is `5e209cb560` / `feat: remove TestBed.get (#60414)` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/CHANGELOG.md). Note the related entry "`TestBed.get` no longer accepts `InjectFlags`" — `InjectFlags` was separately removed from the public API in the same release.

**Affects:** Test files (`*.spec.ts`) that still call `TestBed.get(...)` instead of `TestBed.inject(...)`.

**Action:** required: replace every call to `TestBed.get(...)` with `TestBed.inject(...)` in test code. Note `TestBed.get` was less type-safe and accepted string/loose tokens; `TestBed.inject` requires a `ProviderToken` (class reference or `InjectionToken`). Re-verify tests compile and pass. (PR #60414 is tagged "area: migrations", so an `ng update` schematic is available for the mechanical rename.)

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// @angular/core/testing
// Before (removed in v20):
const service = TestBed.get(UserService);

// After:
const service = TestBed.inject(UserService);
```
(snippet form based on https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/CHANGELOG.md and https://angular.dev/api/core/testing/TestBed)

**What changed:** The `afterRender` function from `@angular/core` was renamed to `afterEveryRender` and promoted to stable in Angular 20. The v20 API Reference lists `afterEveryRender` as the exported symbol and contains no `afterRender` entry, confirming the old name is gone (https://v20.angular.dev/api/core/afterEveryRender, https://v20.angular.dev/api/core/afterRender). The new API registers callbacks that run each time the application finishes rendering, during the `earlyRead`, `write`, `mixedReadWrite`, or `read` phases; it runs on browser platforms only, once per render, and components are not guaranteed to be hydrated before the callback runs (https://v20.angular.dev/api/core/afterEveryRender). The v19→v20 official update guide page returned only generic landing-page content and contains no migration instructions for this rename (https://v20.angular.dev/update-to-v20).

**Affects:** Applications that import or call `afterRender` from `@angular/core`.

**Action:** required: rename every import and call site of `afterRender` to `afterEveryRender`; the exported identifier in `@angular/core` v20 is `afterEveryRender`, so any reference to `afterRender` must be updated. Re-verify that callbacks registered for each phase still behave as expected on browser platforms and that DOM reads/writes follow the documented phase order.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19): imports/calls to afterRender no longer exist in 20.
import { afterRender } from '@angular/core';

// After (Angular 20): use the renamed stable API.
import { afterEveryRender } from '@angular/core';
```

**What changed:** Angular v20 dropped Node.js v18 support: "Angular no longer supports Node.js v18. Node.js versions 22.0 to 22.10 are also no longer supported. Before upgrading to Angular v20, ensure the Node.js version is at least 20.11.1." (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0, Breaking Changes → core). The official compatibility matrix lists Angular `20.0.x || 20.1.x` as requiring Node.js `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || ^24.0.0` with TypeScript `>=5.8.0 <5.9.0`, replacing Angular 19.0.x's `^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1 || ^22.0.0` (https://angular.dev/reference/versions). Node 24 support was added; note the patch-level minimums — Node 20.18 or earlier does not satisfy the range.

**Affects:** Any application/toolchain building or running Angular 20 CLI (`ng`) commands on Node.js 18.x, Node 20.0–20.18, or Node 22.0–22.10; also CI pipelines, Docker images, and hosting buildpacks pinned to those Node versions.

**Action:** required: switch the local, CI, and deployment build runtime to a Node.js version matching `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || ^24.0.0` (e.g. update `.nvmrc`, CI node setup, or Docker base image), then re-verify `ng version`, `ng build`, and `ng test` run without the CLI's "requires a minimum Node.js version" error. Optionally add/update the `engines` field in the workspace `package.json` so platforms select a supported runtime (https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/issues/30547).

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// package.json (suggested engines field, per angular-cli issue #30547)
{
  // ...
  "engines": {
    "node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || ^24.0.0"
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 raised the minimum supported Node.js versions for its tooling (`ng` / Angular CLI, `ng build`, `ng serve`, `ng test`). Angular 19.0.0 declared `"engines": { "node": "^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1" }` in its `package.json` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/package.json). Angular 20.0.0 declares `"engines": { "node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0" }` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/package.json). The 20.0.0 release notes list the commit "update min Node.js support to 20.19, 22.12, and 24.0 (#61500)" and note that Node.js v18 is dropped and that "Node.js versions 22.0 to 22.10 are also no longer supported." (). So Node 18 and any Node 22 release below 22.12 are out of the supported set, while Node 22.12+ and Node 24 are newly added.

**Affects:** all applications, specifically the Node.js runtime used to run the Angular CLI and its builders; CI runners, Docker base images, and local developer environments pinned to Node 18.x or Node 22.0–22.10.

**Action:** required: switch the Node.js runtime running the CLI to a version matching `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0` (i.e. ≥ 20.19.0, ≥ 22.12.0, or ≥ 24.0.0). If you use Node 22, ensure it is at least 22.12.0. Update any `engines`/`nvm`/`.npmrc`/`volta` pins and CI/Docker base images accordingly. If your Node version no longer satisfies the `engines.node` range, `npm install` may emit an EBADENGINE warning (or fail if `engine-strict=true`).

**Before/after:**
```json
// Angular 19.0.0 package.json (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/package.json)
"engines": {
  "node": "^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1"
}

// Angular 20.0.0 package.json (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/package.json)
"engines": {
  "node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0"
}
```

**What changed:** `PendingTasks.run` in `@angular/core` changed its return type from the awaited result of the async function to `void`. Angular now attaches `.catch(this.errorHandler)` to the promise returned by the callback and keeps the stability task open via `.finally(removeTask)`, so rejections are reported to the application error handler rather than becoming dangling promises (which could crash the Node process under SSR) (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60044>). The new signature is `run(fn: () => Promise<unknown>): void`; `add()` still returns `() => void` and `PendingTasks` is stable since v20.0 (<https://angular.dev/api/core/PendingTasks>).

**Affects:** Applications that call `PendingTasks.run` and use or await its return value (e.g. `const userData = await inject(PendingTasks).run(() => fetch(...))`), especially in SSR.

**Action:** required: Stop reading `PendingTasks.run`’s return value and handle the promise manually using `PendingTasks.add` and its cleanup function; ensure promise rejections are handled so the Node process does not shut down during SSR (<https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60044>).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19: run returned the async result
const userData = await inject(PendingTasks).run(() => fetch('/api/user'));
```

```ts
// v20: run returns void; errors go to the application error handler
run(fn: () => Promise<unknown>): void {
  const removeTask = this.add();
  try {
    fn().catch(this.errorHandler).finally(removeTask);
  } catch (err) { /* ... */ }
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular v20, "Uncaught errors in listeners which were previously only reported to `ErrorHandler` are now also reported to Angular's internal error handling machinery. For tests, this means that the error will be rethrown by default rather than only logging the error. Developers should fix these errors, catch them in the test if the test is intentionally covering an error case, or use `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `configureTestingModule` as a last resort." (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0) Third-party write-ups confirm that previously these listener errors were "only logged to the console by default" in tests, and are now rethrown by default (, ).

**Affects:** Applications with event listeners (e.g. `(click)` handlers, host listeners) that can throw uncaught errors — those errors now flow through Angular's internal error handling in addition to `ErrorHandler`. Most visibly, unit tests (TestBed) where such errors are thrown will now rethrow the error by default instead of only logging it, causing previously passing tests to fail.

**Action:** required: fix the underlying listener errors, or catch/expect them in tests that intentionally cover an error case; as a last resort, set `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `TestBed.configureTestingModule` (`configureTestingModule`) to restore the old log-only test behavior. Production runtime code needs no changes unless intentionally relying on errors being swallowed.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v20: opt out of the new rethrow behavior in tests (last resort)
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  rethrowApplicationErrors: false,
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular v20 the `any` overload was removed from `Injector.get` (and by extension calls like `injector.get(token)`): it "now only supports `ProviderToken<T>` and (deprecated since v4) `string`" (https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0; https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60202). The PR "fix(core): fix typing on injector.get to omit 'any'" further restricts the deprecated token type to exclude all but `string`, progressing towards issue #48408. Previously `injector.get` accepted an `any`-typed token (e.g. `injector.get<any>(someArbitraryValue)` or non-token expressions) and returned `any`; now the token parameter is typed, so passing arbitrary values fails type-checking. Related in the same release: the `Route` guards arrays no longer include `any` in the type union — the union includes functional guard functions plus a type matching `Injector.get`: `ProviderToken<T>|string` (https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0). The current API shows `get` overloads taking `ProviderToken` with `InjectOptions`/`notFoundValue`, with the `string`-token overload marked "from v4.0.0 use ProviderToken" (https://angular.dev/api/core/Injector).

**Affects:** Code that calls `injector.get(...)`, `EnvironmentInjector.get(...)`, `TestBed.inject(...)` etc. with tokens that aren't a `ProviderToken<T>` (class, `InjectionToken`) or a string — e.g. calls passing `any`-typed variables, plain objects, or relying on the `any` overload's untyped return; also code typing router guard arrays with `any`.

**Action:** required: update all `Injector.get` / injector-like `get` call sites to pass a proper `ProviderToken<T>` (class reference or `InjectionToken`) or a `string` token, replacing reliance on the removed `any` overload; re-verify that previously `any`-typed returns now have the correct static type (imports of tokens may be needed). Note `string` tokens remain deprecated since v4 — migrate them to `InjectionToken`. No automatic migration schematic is documented for this change.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// No fetched page provides a before/after code snippet for this change;
// per the v20.0.0 release notes: "The `any` overload has been removed from
// `injector.get`. It now only supports `ProviderToken<T>` and
// (deprecated since v4) `string`."
```

**What changed:** Previously, errors thrown during `ApplicationRef.tick` (the application change-detection cycle) were caught inside the method and reported to the application `ErrorHandler`, so `tick()` never threw. Since Angular 20.0.0, "errors during `ApplicationRef.tick` are surfaced to the callsite rather than being caught and reported to the `ErrorHandler`" — errors are "thrown out of the method" so "callers … determine how to handle these errors, such as aborting follow-up work or reporting the error and continuing" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60102, https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0). The PR (#60102) moved the error handling to Angular's internal callsites; the catch-and-report behavior had originally come from commit e263e19a2ae8f7dc6457e5cefb4d27887c111f96 to protect the automatic change-detection subscription.

**Affects:** Applications (or libraries/tests) that call `ApplicationRef.tick()` directly — e.g. zoneless or `noop`-zone apps triggering change detection manually — where an error can be thrown during change detection, and code that relied on `ErrorHandler` receiving those tick errors or on `tick()` never throwing.

**Action:** required: wrap manual `ApplicationRef.tick()` calls in `try`/`catch` and handle errors explicitly (e.g. rethrow to `ErrorHandler.handle` or abort follow-up work); re-verify error-handling tests that previously asserted tick errors reached `ErrorHandler`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before v20: tick() never threw; errors went to ErrorHandler automatically.
// v20+: errors from the change-detection cycle are thrown out of tick().
try {
  this.appRef.tick();
  // safe to assume change detection succeeded before this point in v20+
} catch (e) {
  // handle: abort follow-up work, or report and continue
}
```

**What changed:** Angular no longer produces `ng-reflect-*` attributes on elements with bindings in dev mode by default (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60973). These attributes were deprecated and only present for backwards compatibility (https://angular.dev/api/core/provideNgReflectAttributes). Production mode behavior remains unchanged as `ng-reflect-*` attributes were never produced in production (https://angular.dev/api/core/provideNgReflectAttributes).

**Affects:** Application code and test code that relies on `ng-reflect-*` attributes (https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new).

**Action:** required: Refactor application and test code to avoid relying on `ng-reflect-*` attributes. If temporary restoration is needed, add `provideNgReflectAttributes()` from `@angular/core` to the list of providers within the bootstrap call (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60973).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// After (Angular 20)
// To restore ng-reflect-* attributes temporarily:
import { provideNgReflectAttributes } from '@angular/core';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideNgReflectAttributes(),
  ],
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, the `RedirectFunction` type (exported by `@angular/router`) changed its return type from `string | UrlTree` to `MaybeAsync<string | UrlTree>`, where `MaybeAsync<T>` is `T | Observable<T> | Promise<T>`. Previously the router "only allows a string or a synchronous function for the redirectTo field"; now `Route.redirectTo` accepts asynchronous functions returning a `Promise` or an `Observable` of a string or `UrlTree` (@angular/angular PR #60863; v20 `models.ts` vs v19 `models.ts`). The guide confirms the function "typically returns a string or `UrlTree`, but it can also return an observable or promise" (https://angular.dev/guide/routing/redirecting-routes). PR #60863 is marked area `router`, target `major`, and detected as a breaking change.

**Affects:** Applications that directly call a function typed as `RedirectFunction` (i.e. read its return value and treat it as `string | UrlTree`), or otherwise assign/cast the result of a `redirectTo` function. Routing configurations that only pass such a function to `Route.redirectTo` are unaffected — the router itself handles async values.

**Action:** required: adjust any code that directly invokes a `RedirectFunction` so it handles the widened `Observable`/`Promise` return types, as noted in the release: "Any code that directly calls functions returning this type may need to be adjusted to account for this."

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19
export type RedirectFunction = (redirectData: Pick<ActivatedRouteSnapshot, 'routeConfig' | 'url' | 'params' | 'queryParams' | 'fragment' | 'data' | 'outlet' | 'title'>) => string | UrlTree;

// v20
export type MaybeAsync<T> = T | Observable<T> | Promise<T>;
export type RedirectFunction = (redirectData: Pick<ActivatedRouteSnapshot, 'routeConfig' | 'url' | 'params' | 'queryParams' | 'fragment' | 'data' | 'outlet' | 'title'>) => MaybeAsync<string | UrlTree>;
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0, "Several methods in the public API of the Router which required writable arrays have now been updated to accept readonly arrays when no mutations are done" (listed under **Breaking Changes → router** in the v20.0.0 release notes: https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0). In the v20.0.0 source of `Router` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/router/src/router.ts), `createUrlTree(commands: readonly any[], navigationExtras: UrlCreationOptions = {}): UrlTree` and `navigate(commands: readonly any[], extras: NavigationExtras = {skipLocationChange: false}): Promise<boolean>` now take `readonly any[]` instead of `any[]`; this is purely a widening of accepted parameter types (passing a normal array still works). The same release also made related router type changes: `RedirectFn` can now return `Observable` or `Promise`, and the guards arrays on `Route` no longer include `any` in the type union (https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications calling `Router.createUrlTree`/`Router.navigate`, especially code that passes class fields or generic parameters typed as `ReadonlyArray<...>` / `as const` arrays, or code that mocks/overrides these `Router` methods with the old writable-array signature.

**Action:** required: re-verify that compiles cleanly — any subclasses, spies, or wrapper methods that override `createUrlTree(commands: any[], ...)` or `navigate(commands: any[], ...)` must match the new `readonly any[]` parameter type; no call-site changes are needed since writable arrays remain assignable to readonly parameters.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v20.0.0 packages/router/src/router.ts
createUrlTree(commands: readonly any[], navigationExtras: UrlCreationOptions = {}): UrlTree { ... }

navigate(
  commands: readonly any[],
  extras: NavigationExtras = {skipLocationChange: false},
): Promise<boolean> { ... }
```

**What changed:** In v20 the `any` type was removed from the type union for the guard arrays on `Route` in `@angular/router` — `canActivate`, `canActivateChild`, `canMatch`, `canDeactivate`, and the deprecated `canLoad` (all documented as "An array of `CanActivateFn`/`CanMatchFn`/... or DI tokens" on the Route API page, https://next.angular.dev/api/router/Route). The release notes state: "The guards arrays on `Route` no longer include `any` in the type union. The union includes functions for the functional guards as well as a type matching `Injector.get`: `ProviderToken<T>|string`. Note that `string` is still deprecated on both the route guards and `Injector.get`." (https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0 router Breaking Changes). Per PR #60378 ("fix(router): Remove 'any' type from route guards"), the goal is to "allow TypeScript to infer the parameter types of functions when using functional guards and enable stricter type-checking to ensure the guard array contains valid values" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60378). The same release also removed the `any` overload from `injector.get`, which "now only supports `ProviderToken<T>` and (deprecated since v4) `string`" (release notes, core section).

**Affects:** Applications that put values which were previously only accepted as `any` into the guard arrays (`canActivate`, `canActivateChild`, `canMatch`, `canDeactivate`, `canLoad`) on `Route` configs — e.g. class references, or arrow functions passed inline without an explicit `CanActivateFn`-style type, whose parameters could formerly be implicitly untyped. Functional guards written as plain untyped inline functions and non-token/non-string entries now produce TypeScript compile errors.

**Action:** required: re-verify that every entry in the guard arrays is either a functional guard typed with `CanActivateFn`, `CanActivateChildFn`, `CanMatchFn`, `CanDeactivateFn`, or `CanLoadFn`, or a DI `ProviderToken`/deprecated `string`; annotate inline arrow guards with the appropriate `...Fn` type so their parameters are inferred, and re-run `ng build`/`tsc` to surface new strict type errors in route configurations.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (v19): 'any' in the guard union allowed untyped inline functions / arbitrary values
// After (v20): only functional guard functions (with inferred params) or ProviderToken<T>|string are valid
export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) => {
  const account = inject(Account);
  return convertToPromise(signalsToObservable(account.canAccess())).pipe(
    tap(canAccess => { if (!canAccess) void inject(Router).navigate(['/login']); })
  );
};
```

**What changed:** The `ngIf`, `ngFor`, and `ngSwitch` structural directives are deprecated in Angular 20.0.0 in favor of the `@if`, `@for`, and `@switch` blocks (control flow blocks) introduced in Angular 17.0.0 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60492, , , https://angular.dev/api/common/NgSwitch).

**Affects:** Applications using `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, or `*ngSwitch` in templates.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Before (Deprecated) -->
<div *ngIf="condition">Hello</div>
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let item of items; trackBy: trackByFn">{{item.name}}</li>
</ul>
<div [ngSwitch]="type">
  <span *ngSwitchCase="'A'">Type A</span>
  <span *ngSwitchDefault>Other</span>
</div>

<!-- After (Recommended) -->
@if (condition) {
  <div>Hello</div>
}

@for (item of items; track item) {
  <ul>
    <li>{{item.name}}</li>
  </ul>
}

@switch (type) {
  @case ('A') {
    <span>Type A</span>
  }
  @default {
    <span>Other</span>
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0 all entries of the `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` package were deprecated; Angular now recommends using `@angular/platform-browser` instead (PR #61043, "refactor(platform-browser): Deprecate the `platform-browser-dynamic` package") (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61043). The deprecation was announced under "Deprecations → platform-browser" in the 20.0.0 changelog (), and the npm package now carries the author message "`@angular/platform-browser-dynamic is deprecated. Use `@angular/platform-browser` instead.`" (). JIT compilation itself remains supported as before; if a JIT application or testing environment complains about a missing compiler, add `import '@angular/compiler'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61043). There is no automated schematic for this deprecation, so imports must be updated manually (). For testing entry points, `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing` maps to `@angular/platform-browser/testing`, with `BrowserDynamicTestingModule` replaced by `BrowserTestingModule` and `platformBrowserDynamicTesting()` by `platformBrowserTesting()` (https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/issues/33006).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries that import anything from `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` or `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing` (e.g. `platformBrowserDynamic`, `BrowserDynamicTestingModule`, `platformBrowserDynamicTesting`) and list the package in `package.json`.

**Action:** required: Migrate imports from `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` to `@angular/platform-browser` (and `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing` to `@angular/platform-browser/testing`, replacing `BrowserDynamicTestingModule` with `BrowserTestingModule` and `platformBrowserDynamicTesting()` with `platformBrowserTesting()`); no schematic exists, so this is manual. Then remove the `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` dependency. If JIT code then reports a missing compiler, add `import '@angular/compiler'`. Re-verify JIT bootstrap and TestBed-based tests still work.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
import { BrowserDynamicTestingModule, platformBrowserDynamicTesting } from '@angular/platform-browser-dynamic/testing';

// After
import { BrowserTestingModule, platformBrowserTesting } from '@angular/platform-browser/testing';
```

**What changed:** The HammerJS integration in `@angular/platform-browser` is deprecated and scheduled for removal in a future major version. This includes `HammerModule`, `HammerGestureConfig`, and `HAMMER_GESTURE_CONFIG` providers. Angular 20 marks this as a deprecation step toward removing built-in gesture support (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60257).

**Affects:** Applications importing `HammerModule` from `@angular/platform-browser`, configuring `HAMMER_GESTURE_CONFIG`, extending `HammerGestureConfig`, or relying on built-in HammerJS gesture bindings (e.g. `(swipe)`, `(pan)`).

**Action:** required: remove `HammerModule` imports, `HAMMER_GESTURE_CONFIG` provider entries, and any classes extending `HammerGestureConfig`. Plan to replace HammerJS-based gestures with alternative solutions such as native Pointer Events or standalone gesture libraries.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (deprecated in Angular 20)
import { HammerModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';

@NgModule({
  imports: [HammerModule]
})
export class AppModule {}

// After (remove HammerModule)
@NgModule({
  imports: []
})
export class AppModule {}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0 the entire `@angular/platform-server/testing` entry point was deprecated (PR #60915, "refactor(platform-server): deprecate the testing entry point": `DEPRECATED: @angular/platform-server/testing Use e2e tests to verify SSR behavior instead.`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60915). The two public APIs it exported, `platformServerTesting` (created via `createPlatformFactory(platformCore, 'serverTesting', …)`) and the `ServerTestingModule` (which exports `BrowserDynamicTestingModule` and provides `SERVER_RENDER_PROVIDERS`), are now both marked `@deprecated from v20.0.0, use e2e testing to verify SSR behavior.` in the v20 source (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/platform-server/testing/src/server.ts). The package is still present and functional in v20 — this is a deprecation, not a removal — with no drop-in replacement API; the Angular team recommends verifying SSR behavior with end-to-end tests instead ().

**Affects:** Applications or libraries that import from `@angular/platform-server/testing` (e.g. `ServerTestingModule`, `platformServerTesting`) in unit tests for server-side rendering.

**Action:** none required for v20 (deprecation only; APIs still work). To eliminate the deprecation warnings long-term: required: re-verify SSR behavior using e2e tests, since no replacement testing API is provided by the fetched sources.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// still compiles in v20, but both symbols are now deprecated:
import {ServerTestingModule, platformServerTesting} from '@angular/platform-server/testing';

/**
 * @deprecated from v20.0.0, use e2e testing to verify SSR behavior.
 */
export const platformServerTesting: (extraProviders?: StaticProvider[]) => PlatformRef;

/**
 * @deprecated from v20.0.0, use e2e testing to verify SSR behavior.
 */
export class ServerTestingModule {}
```

**What changed:** In v20 the `NgTemplateOutlet` directive's three inputs were widened to accept `undefined` in addition to `null`: `ngTemplateOutlet: TemplateRef<C> | null | undefined`, `ngTemplateOutletContext: C | null | undefined`, and `ngTemplateOutletInjector: Injector | null | undefined` (in v19.0.0 they were only `... | null`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2e5362a4695c6d0c5e130f286a52cc9d97d0f721.patch, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/common/src/directives/ng_template_outlet.ts, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/common/src/directives/ng_template_outlet.ts). Runtime behaviour is unchanged — an `undefined` outlet was already treated as "no template" by the falsy check `if (!this.ngTemplateOutlet)`; only the TypeScript types (and thus strict-template type checking) changed. The motivation was to avoid writing `?? null` when binding signals such as `viewChild`, which return `undefined` before initialization (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61404). Fixes issue #51225; shipped in 20.0.0.

**Affects:** Applications that bind a possibly-`undefined` value to `*ngTemplateOutlet` / `[ngTemplateOutlet]`, `[ngTemplateOutletContext]`, or `[ngTemplateOutletInjector]` (e.g. `viewChild()`, `input<...>()`, `model()` results), previously working around it with `?? null`.

**Action:** none — this is a non-breaking additive type change (PR #61404 marked "Does this PR introduce a breaking change? No"). Existing `?? null` workarounds may be removed for cleaner code; optional: re-verify that templates rendering/not-rendering with `undefined` outlets behave as before.

**Before/after:**
```diff
-  @Input() public ngTemplateOutlet: TemplateRef<C> | null = null;
+  @Input() public ngTemplateOutlet: TemplateRef<C> | null | undefined = null;
-  @Input() public ngTemplateOutletContext: C | null = null;
+  @Input() public ngTemplateOutletContext: C | null | undefined = null;
-  @Input() public ngTemplateOutletInjector: Injector | null = null;
+  @Input() public ngTemplateOutletInjector: Injector | null | undefined = null;
```

**What changed:** The abstract methods `scrollToPosition(position: [number, number])` and `scrollToAnchor(anchor: string)` in `ViewportScroller` now accept an optional second argument `options?: ScrollOptions`. In `BrowserViewportScroller`, `scrollToPosition` implementation changed from calling `this.window.scrollTo(position[0], position[1])` to `this.window.scrollTo({...options, left: position[0], top: position[1]})`. Similarly, `scrollToAnchor` passes `options` to the private `scrollToElement`, which now calls `this.window.scrollTo({...options, left: ..., top: ...})` instead of the two-argument form. This allows passing native `ScrollOptions` like `behavior: 'smooth'` to Angular's viewport scroller.
(<url>https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b7d3f3dbfcfc40000ca34087d3b8b42319468177</url>)

**Affects:** Applications that extend `ViewportScroller` (e.g., custom implementations) and must update their method signatures to match the new abstract methods.

**Action:** required: Update any classes extending `ViewportScroller` to include the optional `options?: ScrollOptions` parameter in the `scrollToPosition` and `scrollToAnchor` method signatures.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
abstract class ViewportScroller {
    abstract scrollToAnchor(anchor: string): void;
    abstract scrollToPosition(position: [number, number]): void;
}

// After
abstract class ViewportScroller {
    abstract scrollToAnchor(anchor: string, options?: ScrollOptions): void;
    abstract scrollToPosition(position: [number, number], options?: ScrollOptions): void;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20's `AsyncPipe` (`@angular/common`) changed how its internal `PromiseStrategy.createSubscription` handles promises, to prevent memory leaks. In v19 the promise strategy simply did `async.then(updateLatestValue, ...)` and its `dispose()` was a no-op, so the unresolved (or late-resolving) promise retained a reference to the pipe's `updateLatestValue` callback — and thus the destroyed view's `ChangeDetectorRef` — indefinitely (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/common/src/pipes/async_pipe.ts). In v20 the promise is wrapped in lambdas — `async.then((v) => updateLatestValue?.(v), (e) => onError?.(e))` — and `createSubscription` returns an `Unsubscribable` whose `unsubscribe()` sets `updateLatestValue = null; onError = null;`, so when the pipe is destroyed before the promise settles, the callback is detached and can be garbage collected, and the settled promise no longer updates the destroyed view (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/common/src/pipes/async_pipe.ts, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58041, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/255c79e0480b0adc876b526e2a96d5005692e42f). All types moved from `Promise<T>` to `PromiseLike<T>`. This is an internal, non-breaking bug fix shipped in 20.0.0 (no migration schematic).

**Affects:** Applications binding a `Promise`/`PromiseLike` to the `async` pipe where the promise resolves after the host view is destroyed, or never resolves (e.g. third-party libraries). Observable usage is unaffected.

**Action:** none — no application code, configuration, or dependency changes are required; internal `PromiseStrategy`/`SubscriptionStrategy` APIs are private. Re-verify that templates using `| async` with long-lived or never-resolving promises still render values as expected (a promise resolving after view destruction no longer touches the destroyed view).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// v19 — PromiseStrategy (packages/common/src/pipes/async_pipe.ts)
createSubscription(async: Promise<any>, updateLatestValue: (v: any) => any): Promise<any> {
  return async.then(updateLatestValue, (e) => {
    throw e;
  });
}
dispose(subscription: Promise<any>): void {}

// v20 — PromiseStrategy
async.then(
  (v) => updateLatestValue?.(v),
  (e) => onError?.(e),
);
return {
  unsubscribe: () => {
    updateLatestValue = null;
    onError = null;
  },
};
```

**What changed:** In `PreloadLinkCreator.createPreloadLinkTag` (`packages/common/src/directives/ng_optimized_image/preload-link-creator.ts`), when the number of preloaded images reaches `DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT` (5) in dev mode, Angular previously threw `new RuntimeError(RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES, ...)`, producing the runtime error `NG02961: The \`NgOptimizedImage\` directive has detected that more than 5 images were marked as priority. This might negatively affect an overall performance of the page. To fix this, remove the "priority" attribute from images with less priority.` It now emits a one-time `console.warn(formatRuntimeError(RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES, ...))` guarded by an `errorShown` flag, instead of throwing (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cbbea70fa37b14e89e4f3459ae880116c0e894b1.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cbbea70fa37b14e89e4f3459ae880116c0e894b1, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60879). The rationale: "This should not be treated as a hard error, as it doesn’t break the application but merely degrades performance" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60879).

**Affects:** Development mode applications using `NgOptimizedImage` (`ngSrc`) with more than 5 `priority` images — e.g. tests that used `expect(...).toThrowError('NG02961: ...')`, or code relying on the thrown error.

**Action:** required: re-verify any tests asserting `toThrowError('NG02961: ...')` for exceeding the preload limit; the directive now emits a single `console.warn` with the same `NG02961` message rather than throwing. Also review whether the new warning surfaces through application error handling in tests.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// PreloadLinkCreator.createPreloadLinkTag — BEFORE (throws)
if (ngDevMode) {
  if (this.preloadedImages.size >= DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT) {
    throw new RuntimeError(
      RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES,
      ngDevMode &&
        `The \`NgOptimizedImage\` directive has detected that more than ` +
          `${DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT} images were marked as priority. ` +
          ...
    );
  }
}

// AFTER (warns once)
if (ngDevMode && !this.errorShown &&
    this.preloadedImages.size >= DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT) {
  this.errorShown = true;
  console.warn(
    formatRuntimeError(
      RuntimeErrorCode.TOO_MANY_PRELOADED_IMAGES,
      `The \`NgOptimizedImage\` directive has detected that more than ` +
        `${DEFAULT_PRELOADED_IMAGES_LIMIT} images were marked as priority. ` + ...
    ),
  );
}
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/common/http`, the factory `makeHttpResourceFn` in `packages/common/http/src/resource.ts` previously returned an inner function named `httpResourceRef`. Since Angular's NG0203 runtime error message uses the calling function's name, calling `httpResource()` outside an injection context threw `Error: NG0203: httpResourceRef() can only be used within an injection context such as a constructor, a factory function, a field initializer, or a function used with runInInjectionContext`. The inner function was renamed to `httpResource`, so the error now correctly reads `httpResource() can only be used within an injection context` (verified by a new unit test asserting `thrown.message.includes('httpResource() can only be used within an injection context')`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fc4a56d5c5fa270dbb1402c7cafe6d4f2af571eb, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60022). No public API changed — the returned type is still `HttpResourceRef<TResult>` and the PR is marked as a bugfix, not a breaking change.

**Affects:** Only applications that call `httpResource(...)` outside an injection context (e.g. without passing an explicit `injector`) and match on the exact NG0203 error text containing `httpResourceRef()`.

**Action:** none — behavior of `httpResource` is unchanged; only the function name embedded in the NG0203 message changed. Applications asserting on the old message string should re-verify those assertions.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/common/http/src/resource.ts
function makeHttpResourceFn<TRaw>(responseType: ResponseType) {
-  return function httpResourceRef<TResult = TRaw>(
+  return function httpResource<TResult = TRaw>(
    request: RawRequestType,
    options?: HttpResourceOptions<TResult, TRaw>,
  ): HttpResourceRef<TResult> {
```

**What changed:** The Angular 20 compiler CLI added a new extended template diagnostic. The compiler now checks if the track expression in a `@for` block is an uninvoked function reference (which would cause the list to be recreated on every change detection cycle). This adds `ErrorCode.UNINVOKED_TRACK_FUNCTION` (error code `8115`) and `ExtendedTemplateDiagnosticName.UNINVOKED_TRACK_FUNCTION` ("uninvokedTrackFunction") (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7a971766dc59691dc68da9439e180a6c4d7b17d8.patch).
**Affects:** Applications that pass a function reference directly to the `track` property of a `@for` block (e.g., `track trackByName` instead of `track trackByName(item)`) and build with extended diagnostics enabled. The check specifically targets properties that resolve to a function type in the class.
**Action:** required: Update the templates to invoke the track function by passing arguments (e.g., `track trackByName(item)` instead of `track trackByName`), or suppress the check by adding `ExtendedTemplateDiagnosticName.UNINVOKED_TRACK_FUNCTION` to `angularCompilerOptions.extendedDiagnostics.checks` in `tsconfig.json`.
**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- Before (Triggers NG8115: The track function in the @for block should be invoked) -->
@for (item of items; track trackByName) { ... }

<!-- After -->
@for (item of items; track trackByName(item)) { ... }
```

**What changed:** Angular v20 adds support for tagged template literals in template expressions (interpolations, bindings, and pipe inputs), which previously failed to parse. The compiler gained a new `TaggedTemplateLiteral` AST node in `@angular/compiler` (`packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts`), the template type-checker translates it to a TypeScript `ts.TaggedTemplateExpression`, and it emits as e.g. `$r3$.ɵɵtextInterpolate1("...", ctx.tag \`hello ${ctx.name}\`, "")` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/51b8ff23cefb5112937dec9727a5b5d6e913aae6). The docs table of supported literals in `guide/templates/expression-syntax.md` gained a "Tagged template string" row with example `` tag`Hello ${name}` ``. Build output emits a call where the tag is a component member receiving `(strings: TemplateStringsArray, ...args)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59947, https://blog.angular.dev/announcing-angular-v20-b5c9c06cf301).

**Affects:** No existing code is affected; this is purely additive. Only applications that want to write `` tag`...` `` expressions in templates (previously a template parse error) can now do so. Type checking treats the result as a string, so passing it where a non-string is expected produces a template type error.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Now valid in Angular 20 templates (previously a parse error):
@Component({
  template: '{{ greet`Hello, ${name()}` }}'
})
export class MyComp {
  name = input();
  greet(strings: TemplateStringsArray, name: string) {
    return strings[0] + name + strings[1] + '!';
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's `TemplateLiteralElementExpr` (in `packages/compiler/src/output/output_ast.ts`) previously estimated its `rawText` from the node's `sourceSpan` (`this.rawText = rawText ?? sourceSpan?.toString() ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text))`) when no `rawText` was supplied. Because the expression AST parser can produce inaccurate spans when escape sequences are involved, the raw string sometimes didn't match the cooked string, and TypeScript threw an unrecoverable error while constructing its AST node — surfacing as a build error for template literals used with `@let` or `track` in templates (issue #60528) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8b990a31c3f9b27e096c4ac63a7fa1873fadbe72). The span-based estimation was removed; `rawText` is now always derived by manually escaping `\`, `` ` `` and `${`: `this.rawText = rawText ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text));`, with `rawText`/`text` now `readonly` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60529). The commit notes `rawText` matters little now, since its main use was downlevelling template literals to ES5, which is no longer supported.

**Affects:** Applications using JavaScript template literals (backtick strings with `${...}` interpolation) in Angular templates, especially combined with `@let` declarations or `track` expressions, that previously failed to compile in Angular 19.2 with a build error.

**Action:** none — this is an internal compiler bug fix; no application code, config, or dependency changes are required. Re-verify that templates combining template literals with `@let`/`track` now build without errors.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (packages/compiler/src/output/output_ast.ts)
this.rawText =
  rawText ?? sourceSpan?.toString() ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text));

// After
this.rawText = rawText ?? escapeForTemplateLiteral(escapeSlashes(text));
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's expression parser previously produced **zero-length spans** for `TemplateLiteral` and `TemplateLiteralElement` AST nodes — `span`/`sourceSpan` were computed from `this.inputIndex` *after* `this.advance()` instead of the token's start (`https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ef1fd137a9e180059963b1f3a025c3acc1610b8f.patch`, `packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts`, `_ParseAST.parseNoInterpolationTemplateLiteral` and the template-literal parsing loop). Fix PR #60323 (also back-ported as #60331) captures `const start = this.inputIndex;` / `const partStart = this.inputIndex;` *before* advancing, so spans now cover the literal text (e.g. parsing `` `before ${one} - ${two} after` `` yields element spans `` `before ``, ` - `, ` after`` instead of `""`). Fixes #60320 ("Wrong location information in `TemplateLiteralElement`") and #60319 ("Wrong location information when parsing `TemplateLiteral`"), both filed by the author of `angular-estree-parser` while adding Angular template-literal support to Prettier (prettier/prettier#17238), who relied on sibling expressions to locate elements as a workaround. Tagged template literals (`tag\`text\``) were likewise corrected (#60323).

**Affects:** Only code that consumes `@angular/compiler`'s `Lexer`/`Parser` AST programmatically and reads `sourceSpan`/`span` of `TemplateLiteral` or `TemplateLiteralElement` nodes — tooling such as formatters, language services, `angular-estree-parser`, codemods. Applications using template literals in templates (`{{ `abc` }}`, tagged literals) are unaffected: emitted output is unchanged, only span metadata.

**Action:** none for upgrading applications. Downstream tool authors must re-verify span-based location logic: spans are now non-empty and include the backtick/quoting characters, so hacks that derived element positions from sibling `ASTWithSource` neighbors or assumed empty spans must be updated (issue #60320 explicitly describes removing such a workaround).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/parser.ts - _ParseAST
// before
const span = this.span(this.inputIndex);
const sourceSpan = this.sourceSpan(this.inputIndex);
// after
const start = this.inputIndex;
this.advance();
const span = this.span(start);
const sourceSpan = this.sourceSpan(start);

// parser_spec.ts expected spans for `before ${one} - ... after`
// before: ['before ', '']   after: ['before ', '`before ']
// before: [' after', '']    after: [' after', ' after`']
```

**What changed:** The Angular v20 compiler moved its i18n phases after the `@let` optimization phase. Previously the `assignI18nSlotDependencies` phase assigned the `target` of i18n ops much earlier than the `@let` optimization, so if an `@let` declared inside an `i18n` block was optimized away (because it wasn't used in any child view), the pointer recorded in the `ɵɵi18n`/`ɵɵi18nStart` instruction became invalid and assertions fired (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e0d378d20e768d353b01bc28e10ad53c5485b426, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60512). This was unmasked by the companion change in the same PR, `perf(compiler): reduce allocations for let declarations only used in the same view` (commit 768239a89c), which now also removes the `ɵɵdeclareLet` call (not just `ɵɵstoreLet`) for same-view `@let` declarations whose expression contains no pipe — a `TNode` from `declareLet` is still kept when a pipe such as `async` is used, so DI works (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/768239a89cba7e7cf1f497e15589705b1446f8a6). Generated output for an `@let` used only directly inside `i18n` now emits a plain local plus `ɵɵi18nExp`/`ɵɵi18nApply` with no `ɵɵdeclareLet`; `@let`s referenced from an `<ng-template>` inside the i18n block still emit `ɵɵdeclareLet` + `ɵɵstoreLet`/`ɵɵreadContextLet` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e0d378d20e768d353b01bc28e10ad53c5485b426.diff).

**Affects:** Templates that declare `@let` inside (or after) an element with an `i18n` attribute, where the value is interpolated into the translated text, and apps that rely on the exact generated instruction indices/`TNode` layout for `@let` declarations.

**Action:** none — no source, config or dependency change; this is an internal compiler phase-ordering fix. Re-verify that i18n blocks containing `@let` declarations (including ones referenced from an embedded `<ng-template>`, and `@let` declared after an `i18n` element) still translate and interpolate correctly after rebuilding, since generated `ɵɵdeclareLet`/`ɵɵstoreLet` instruction indices shift.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// let_in_i18n.ts (test case added by the fix)
@Component({
  template: `
    <div i18n>
      @let result = value * 2;
      The result is {{result}}
    </div>
  `,
})
export class MyApp {
  value = 1;
}
```
```javascript
// generated output after the fix: no ɵɵdeclareLet for the same-view @let
template: function MyApp_Template(rf, ctx) {
  if (rf & 1) {
    $r3$.ɵɵelementStart(0, "div");
    $r3$.ɵɵi18n(1, 0);
    $r3$.ɵɵelementEnd();
  }
  if (rf & 2) {
    const result_r1 = ctx.value * 2;
    $r3$.ɵɵadvance();
    $r3$.ɵɵi18nExp(result_r1);
    $r3$.ɵɵi18nApply(1);
  }
},
```

**What changed:** In Angular v20 the template expression compiler gained the `void` and exponentiation (`**`) operators (PR #59894, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59894). PR #60101 then fixed how these are walked and printed: `typeof` and `void` expressions are now handled in `RecursiveAstVisitor` the same way as other unary operator expressions (commit b70ad3c4e6, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b70ad3c4e63158a72b8aea173b1268ec8ab08e2b), exponentiation became right-to-left associative (`a ** b ** c` equals `a ** (b ** c)`, not `(a ** b) ** c`), and required parentheses are preserved in compiled output when a unary operator is the base of an exponentiation, e.g. `(-1) ** 3` (commit 7c9b4892e9, ; https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60101). Related breaking change from #59894: `void` in an expression now refers to the operator instead of a component property.

**Affects:** Applications whose templates or host bindings use `void`, `typeof`, or `**` in expressions; also templates using `{{ void }}` (or similar) to read a component property literally named `void`.

**Action:** required: re-verify template/host-binding expressions using `void`, `typeof`, and `**` after upgrading — `**` now groups right-to-left and unary bases must be parenthesized (`(-1) ** 3`). Where an expression must reference a property named `void`, change it to `this.void` (e.g. `{{this.void}}`) as documented in #59894.

**Before/after:**
```
// Before (v19): `void` resolved as a component property; `**` unsupported in templates
{{ void }}

// After (v20): `void` is the operator (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59894)
{{ this.void }}          // to still access a property named `void`
{{ 2 ** 3 }}             // exponentiation, right-associative (a ** b ** c = a ** (b ** c))
@Directive({ host: { '(mousedown)': 'void handleMousedown()' } })
```

**What changed:** The Angular linker (`@angular/compiler-cli/linker` and `@angular/compiler-cli/linker/babel`) no longer pulls in TypeScript. Previously linker files imported from internal barrel entry-points (e.g. `../../../src/ngtsc/translator`, `../../../src/ngtsc/file_system`), which transitively dragged in `typescript` and added 500ms–1s to application compile times. The fix restructures imports to granular deep paths (`src/ngtsc/translator/src/api/ast_factory`, `src/ngtsc/file_system/src/types`, etc.) and adds `/* @__PURE__ */` annotations so bundlers can drop unused TypeScript code; an integration test `integration/no_ts_linker` verifies `await import('@angular/compiler-cli/linker')` and `await import('@angular/compiler-cli/linker/babel')` work while `typescript` is not installed (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61618, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e25e6c95a28e4b01a58b9988d404a4199b7d1d13, cherry-picked for v20 in https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61635). In `packages/compiler-cli/package.json`, `typescript` (peer range `>=5.8 <5.9`) was declared with `"peerDependenciesMeta": { "typescript": { "optional": true } }` (from the patch).

**Affects:** Primarily downstream tooling that consumes only the linker entry-points (`@angular/compiler-cli/linker`, `@angular/compiler-cli/linker/babel`, e.g. Babel-based build pipelines) and package managers resolving `@angular/compiler-cli` peer dependencies; ordinary applications building with the Angular CLI are unaffected and still need `typescript` itself for other purposes.

**Action:** none for applications. Consumers of the linker that relied on `typescript` being installed transitively by `@angular/compiler-cli` should re-verify that their builds still install `typescript` explicitly when they use anything beyond the linker.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// packages/compiler-cli/package.json (v20)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/compiler": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "typescript": ">=5.8 <5.9"
},
"peerDependenciesMeta": {
  "typescript": {
    "optional": true
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 added extended diagnostic `NG8114` "Unparenthesized Nullish Coalescing" (diagnostic name `unparenthesizedNullishCoalescing`) for Angular templates (the Angular compiler-cli). It reports cases where the nullish coalescing operator `??` is mixed with the logical or `||` or logical and `&&` without parentheses to disambiguate precedence. This pattern is an error in TypeScript/JavaScript but has historically been allowed in Angular templates; the new diagnostic flags it. The check runs only when `strictTemplates` is enabled, and extended diagnostics default to `warning` severity (they emit a `NG8114` warning at build, not a hard error, unless configured) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60279) (https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics/NG8114) (https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics).

**Affects:** Applications using Angular templates that mix `??` with `||` or `&&` without parentheses, when `strictTemplates` is enabled in `angularCompilerOptions`. New build-time warnings (`NG8114`) appear; they only block compilation if the check is configured as `error` or `"defaultCategory": "error"`.

**Action:** required: add parentheses to disambiguate the `??` precedence (or remove the offending mixing), or configure the diagnostic via `angularCompilerOptions.extendedDiagnostics.checks.unparenthesizedNullishCoalescing` (e.g. `"suppress"`, `"warning"`, or `"error"`). Re-verify that new `NG8114` warnings are addressed or intentionally suppressed before treating extended diagnostics as errors.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// tsconfig angularCompilerOptions
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "extendedDiagnostics": {
      "checks": {
        "unparenthesizedNullishCoalescing": "suppress"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
```html
<!-- Before: ambiguous (no parentheses) -->
<!-- {{ a ?? b || c }} -->

<!-- After: disambiguated by wrapping the ?? operand -->
<!-- {{ (a ?? b) || c }} -->
```

**What changed:** `@angular/compiler-cli` adds a new extended diagnostic `NG8116` / `missingStructuralDirective` (`ErrorCode.MISSING_STRUCTURAL_DIRECTIVE = 8116`, `ExtendedTemplateDiagnosticName.MISSING_STRUCTURAL_DIRECTIVE = 'missingStructuralDirective'`), implemented by `MissingStructuralDirectiveCheck` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/extended/checks/missing_structural_directive/index.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c889382a2044f9a024f475680c8573d0f7112562). Previously the compiler only warned for missing imports of "known" `CommonModule` structural directives or regular binding syntax; missing imports of custom `*myStructuralDirective`-style usages "fails silently" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59443). The check runs **only for standalone** components/directives, skips `ngIf`, `ngFor`, `ngForOf`, `ngForTrackBy`, `ngSwitchCase`, `ngSwitchDefault` (`KNOWN_CONTROL_FLOW_DIRECTIVES`, reported by `missingControlFlowDirective`), and emits `A structural directive \`X\` was used in the template without a corresponding import in the component. Make sure that the directive is included in the \`@Component.imports\` array of this component.` It also fires for `*ngTemplateOutlet` when `NgTemplateOutlet` isn't imported. Requires `strictTemplates`; extended diagnostics are warnings by default and do not block compilation, but projects setting `"defaultCategory": "error"` or mapping `NG8116`-class checks to `error` will get hard compile errors (https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics, https://angular.dev/extended-diagnostics/NG8116).

**Affects:** applications with `strictTemplates: true` that use a custom (or `NgTemplateOutlet`) structural directive in the template of a **standalone** component/directive without listing the directive in `imports`. Not breaking per the PR — severity is a warning unless configured as `error`.

**Action:** required: add the missing directive to the standalone component's `imports` array; alternatively suppress/demote in `tsconfig.json` under `angularCompilerOptions.extendedDiagnostics.checks` with `"missingStructuralDirective": "suppress"`. Also re-verify builds that set `defaultCategory: "error"`, since the new warning becomes a build failure there.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  // Template uses `*select`, but no corresponding directive imported.
  imports: [],
  template: `<p *select="let data from source">{{data}}</p>`,
})
class MyComponent {}

// after (NG8116 resolved)
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {SelectDirective} from 'my-directives';

@Component({
  // Add `SelectDirective` to the `imports` array to make it available in the template.
  imports: [SelectDirective],
  template: `<p *select="let data from source">{{data}}</p>`,
})
class MyComponent {}
```
```json
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "extendedDiagnostics": {
      "checks": {
        "missingStructuralDirective": "suppress"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular's template type checker now also checks host bindings. The commit and PR describe new support for type checking the `host` object literal and the `@HostBinding` and `@HostListener` decorators on `@Directive` and `@Component` classes (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1971e57a457ff9fd4dc8a353b59b51364e08b443, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60267). At commit time this behaviour was disabled by default and gated by the `typeCheckHostBindings` compiler flag (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1971e57a457ff9fd4dc8a353b59b51364e08b443, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60267). In the current Angular reference, `typeCheckHostBindings` is documented under `angularCompilerOptions`: "When true, enables type checking of expressions in the host object literal and @HostBinding / @HostListener decorators of components and directives. Default is true." (https://angular.dev/reference/configs/angular-compiler-options). Language-service features in host bindings (renaming, hover) are also part of the same effort (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60267).

**Affects:** Projects whose components or directives use the `host` object literal or `@HostBinding`/`@HostListener` decorators (https://angular.dev/reference/configs/angular-compiler-options). New projects already enable `typeCheckHostBindings`.

**Action:** required: re-verify that `angularCompilerOptions.typeCheckHostBindings` is set as intended; with the default `true`, existing invalid host-binding expressions will now produce compile errors, so either fix the offending `host`/`@HostBinding`/`@HostListener` expressions or set `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` in `angularCompilerOptions` to suppress them (https://angular.dev/reference/configs/angular-compiler-options).

**Before/after:**
```json
// tsconfig.json — angularCompilerOptions section
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "strictTemplates": true,
    "typeCheckHostBindings": false
  }
}
```
(When `true` — the documented default — the compiler type-checks expressions in the `host` object literal and `@HostBinding`/`@HostListener` decorators. https://angular.dev/reference/configs/angular-compiler-options)

**What changed:** When an `NgModule` declares an invalid `schemas` field, `extractSchemas` in `NgModuleDecoratorHandler` (`packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/ng_module/src/handler.ts`) used to throw a `FatalDiagnosticError`, which aborted compilation and removed the module from the compiler registry. Angular 20 wraps that call in a `try`/`catch`: when a `FatalDiagnosticError` is caught, it is pushed into `diagnostics` and `schemas` is set to an empty array (`[]`), so the module continues to exist in the registry (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9ec9c7e1b8473c76661ad09a3961016ccc4ddfc7, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61220). A build still fails because the schema error is still reported as a diagnostic; the originating schema errors continue to be reported in the IDE, but cascading "missing module/component" diagnostics in the language service are avoided (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9ec9c7e1b8473c76661ad09a3961016ccc4ddfc7).

**Affects:** applications whose `@NgModule` metadata supplies an invalid `schemas` value (e.g. an unresolved or non-array `schemas`), particularly when using the Angular language service / IDE diagnostics.

**Action:** none — this is a compiler/language-service internal behavior change; correct any actual invalid `schemas` field that surfaces as a diagnostic, but no code, configuration, or dependency change is required to migrate.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
const schemas =
  this.compilationMode !== CompilationMode.LOCAL && ngModule.has('schemas')
    ? extractSchemas(ngModule.get('schemas')!, this.evaluator, 'NgModule')
    : [];

// after
let schemas: SchemaMetadata[] | undefined;
try {
  schemas =
    this.compilationMode !== CompilationMode.LOCAL && ngModule.has('schemas')
      ? extractSchemas(ngModule.get('schemas')!, this.evaluator, 'NgModule')
      : [];
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof FatalDiagnosticError) {
    diagnostics.push(e.toDiagnostic());
    schemas = [];
  } else {
    throw e;
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/compiler-cli`, the `ComponentDecoratorHandler` (in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/component/src/handler.ts`) previously let a `FatalDiagnosticError` thrown while resolving a component's `template`/`templateUrl` (via `parseTemplateDeclaration`/`extractTemplate`) abort analysis, which in the language service meant the component never entered the compiler registry and produced cascading "missing component" diagnostics in an IDE. Angular 20.0.0 catches that `FatalDiagnosticError`, reports it via `e.toDiagnostic()`, poisons the file (`isPoisoned = true`), and substitutes a placeholder from the new `createEmptyTemplate(...)` in `resources.ts` (empty content, `templateUrl: 'missing.ng.html'`, `resolvedTemplateUrl: '/missing.ng.html'`) so the component still exists in the registry (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58673, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a1cacc5b17d6f865bb260f475f1d2ef37dc845f1, diff at pull 58673). "A build will still fail in this case." The originating template errors are still reported in the IDE; only the secondary cascade is suppressed (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58673). Included in the 20.0.0 `compiler-cli` fix list ().

**Affects:** IDE/language-service (Angular extension) experience only, for applications whose component `templateUrl` points at a file that does not exist or is inaccessible to the language service; command-line builds fail either way.

**Action:** none — no code, configuration, dependency or build change is required; behavior is a strictly better IDE diagnostic experience. (Optionally re-verify that missing/inaccessible template files still surface their original error in the IDE.)

**Before/after:**
```diff
+      } catch (e) {
+        if (e instanceof FatalDiagnosticError) {
+          diagnostics ??= [];
+          diagnostics.push(e.toDiagnostic());
+          isPoisoned = true;
+          // Create an empty template for the missing/invalid template.
+          // A build will still fail in this case. However, for the language service,
+          // this allows the component to exist in the compiler registry and prevents
+          // cascading diagnostics within an IDE due to "missing" components.
+          template = createEmptyTemplate(node, component, containingFile);
+        } else {
+          throw e;
+        }
       }
```

**What changed:** Before v20 the Angular template type-checker built the AST for `@HostListener`-decorated members by naively splitting the listener string (`const [eventName, phase] = eventNameNode.text.split('.')`) and always passed `null` as the `target` of the generated `TmplAstBoundEvent`, so targeted events such as `'document:click'` lost their target (only `@`-prefixed animation events were handled, via `ParsedEventType.Animation`) (`https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1e6faad479e879083550424f92b6501fe09d48ba`). Now `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/host_bindings.ts` uses `parser.parseAnimationEventName` / `parser.parseEventListenerName` from `BindingParser` and sets `type`, `eventName`, `phase` and `target` correctly. The companion commit `7e03af898e` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/type_check_block.ts` (`TcbUnclaimedOutputsOp`) makes the generated TCB use the event's target instead of the host element: `window`/`document` targets now generate `window.addEventListener(...)` / `document.addEventListener(...)` instead of `<element>.addEventListener(...)` (``). Part of PR #60561, action: merge, target: major, area: compiler (`https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60561`).

**Affects:** applications using targeted global events in `@HostListener` (e.g. `'document:click'`, `'window:scroll'`, `'body:keydown'`) or in template output bindings, and that previously typed the `$event` parameter loosely or incorrectly — e.g. `KeyboardEvent` for `document:click`. Affects the strict/type-checked compilation path only (host-binding type checking, e.g. `typeCheckHostBindings`), as new diagnostics; runtime behaviour of `@HostListener` is unchanged (``).

**Action:** required: re-compile with `ng build`/`ng test` and fix any newly reported type errors on `@HostListener` handlers for targeted events — e.g. `@HostListener('document:click', ['$event'])` now infers `MouseEvent`, so a parameter typed `KeyboardEvent` fails with `Argument of type 'MouseEvent' is not assignable to parameter of type 'KeyboardEvent'.` Widen or correct the `$event` parameter type (e.g. `Event`/`MouseEvent`) in the handler signature. No change to the `@HostListener` string itself, dependencies, or config is required.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// new v20 test expectation in packages/compiler-cli/test/ngtsc/host_bindings_type_check_spec.ts
@Component({template: '', selector: 'button[foo]'})
export class Comp {
  @HostListener('document:click', ['$event']) handleEvent(event: KeyboardEvent) {}
}
// diagnostic: Argument of type 'MouseEvent' is not assignable to parameter of type 'KeyboardEvent'.

// TCB generation for <button (window:scroll)="handle()"></button>
// new: window.addEventListener("scroll", ($event): any => { (this).handle(); });
// new: document.addEventListener("click", ($event): any => { (this).handle(); });
```

**What changed:** The template compiler no longer drops parentheses around nullish-coalescing (`??`) expressions when emitting code. A new `parenthesizeNullishCoalescing` step in the `requiredParentheses` phase of `packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/phases/required_parentheses.ts` wraps `&&`/`||` sub-expressions and `o.ConditionalExpr` operands of an `o.BinaryOperator.NullishCoalesce` in `o.ParenthesizedExpr`, and `requiredParentheses` was moved *after* `expandSafeReads` in the `phases` list of `packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/emit.ts` so safe-property reads down-leveled into ternaries (`ctx.x == null ? null : ctx.x.y`) also get parenthesized. A corresponding special case was removed from `ExpressionTranslatorVisitor.visitBinaryOperator` in `ngtsc/translator/src/translator.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ffb19e64f1ced7b5eb55e1c1b96b6f7c54835a1d.patch). Related in the same PR: Angular stopped down-leveling `??` in templates itself; depending on the TypeScript `target`, TypeScript may still down-level it. Template semantics of `??` are unchanged — notably `a && b ?? c` is still treated as `a && (b ?? c)` in templates, unlike TS/JS where it is invalid (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60060).

**Affects:** Applications using `??` in templates together with `&&`, `||`, or safe navigation (`?.`), where emitted JIT/AOT output or `ɵɵtextInterpolate`-style generated code is inspected/asserted (e.g. snapshot or golden-file tests of compiled output).

**Action:** none — no application code, config or dependency change required; re-verify that rendered values of templates mixing `??` with `&&`/`||`/`?.` are unchanged and update any golden/snapshot expectations of compiler output.

**Before/after:**
```js
// templates
<div>{{ (x && y) ?? z }}</div>
<div>{{ x && (y ?? z) }}</div>
<div>{{ x?.y ?? y?.z }}</div>

// emitted (after fix)
$i0$.ɵɵtextInterpolate((ctx.x && ctx.y) ?? ctx.z);
$i0$.ɵɵtextInterpolate(ctx.x && (ctx.y ?? ctx.z));
$i0$.ɵɵtextInterpolate((ctx.x == null ? null : ctx.x.y) ?? (ctx.y == null ? null : ctx.y.z));
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, the template type-checker (TCB) now accounts for the `target` of targeted event listeners such as `(document:click)` and `(window:scroll)`. Previously the TCB generation code "doesn't handle targeted events (e.g. `(document:click)`) which ends up binding to the current element and can have type inference implications" — the generated type-check code called `addEventListener` on the element instead of the global target, so `$event` got the wrong event type (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7e03af898e5144eb3a64b17dd6470874467d9133, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60561). Now, for `output.target === 'window'` or `'document'`, the TCB emits `window.addEventListener(...)` / `document.addEventListener(...)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7e03af898e5144eb3a64b17dd6470874467d9133.patch). A companion commit fixes that "we weren't accounting for targeted events when creating the AST for `@HostListener`-decorated members": `host_bindings.ts` now uses `parser.parseEventListenerName` to populate `TmplAstBoundEvent`'s `target`, so e.g. `@HostListener('document:click', ['$event'])` typed as `KeyboardEvent` now reports `Argument of type 'MouseEvent' is not assignable to parameter of type 'KeyboardEvent'.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1e6faad479e879083550424f92b6501fe09d48ba.patch). PR #60561 also refactored name parsing into reusable `BindingParser` methods (no app-facing change) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60561).

**Affects:** Templates using targeted events with global targets (`(window:...)`, `(document:...)`, e.g. `(document:click)`) whose handlers or `$event` usage rely on the previous (incorrect) event-type inference, and `@HostListener('window:...')` / `@HostListener('document:...')` members with loosely or incorrectly typed `$event` parameters. Only surfaces when host binding type checking is active (`typeCheckHostBindings`).

**Action:** required: re-verify that AOT builds still type-check after upgrading; if new diagnostics appear on targeted event handlers, correct the handler's `$event` parameter type to the DOM event actually produced by the global target (e.g. `MouseEvent` for `document:click`, `Event` for `window:scroll`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// TCB code generated for `<button (window:scroll)="handle()"></button>`
// Before (v19): bound to the current element (wrong target, wrong $event type)
// After (v20), per the added tests:
window.addEventListener("scroll", ($event): any => { (this).handle(); });
document.addEventListener("click", ($event): any => { (this).handle(); });
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 added a new hook in `@angular/core/primitives/signals` that fires when a producer `ReactiveNode` (e.g. signal, computed, linkedSignal) is created, mirroring the existing `postSignalSetFn` pattern. New exports include `ReactiveHookFn` type and `setPostProducerCreatedFn` / `runPostProducerCreatedFn` functions. Additionally, `postSignalSetFn` was updated to accept the `ReactiveNode` being updated. The `setPostSignalSetFn` signature changed from accepting `(() => void) | null` to `ReactiveHookFn | null` where `ReactiveHookFn = (node: ReactiveNode) => void`. These are public APIs exported from `@angular/core/primitives/signals` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60333), https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/60333.diff).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries that directly import and use `setPostSignalSetFn` from `@angular/core/primitives/signals`, as the callback signature changes to accept a `ReactiveNode` argument.

**Action:** required: Update usages of `setPostSignalSetFn` to handle the new callback signature `(node: ReactiveNode) => void`. Verify if new `setPostProducerCreatedFn` should be used.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// From https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/60333.diff
// Before
export function setPostSignalSetFn(fn: (() => void) | null): (() => void) | null;

// After
export type ReactiveHookFn = (node: ReactiveNode) => void;
export function setPostSignalSetFn(fn: ReactiveHookFn | null): ReactiveHookFn | null;
export function setPostProducerCreatedFn(fn: ReactiveHookFn | null): ReactiveHookFn | null;
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds template-like bindings and host-directive application to dynamically-created components. The `bindings` and `directives` options are supported by the standalone `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` and `ComponentFactory.create`, using new exported helpers `inputBinding()`, `outputBinding()` and `twoWayBinding()` from `@angular/core` (#60137, commit fe57332). Per the PR, the new APIs "go through the same mechanisms as the template", `outputBinding` cleans up automatically when the component is destroyed, they can target specific directives (unlike `ComponentRef.setInput`, which "apply[s] to all directives at the same time"), and they are tree-shakeable, whereas `setInput` is a non-deletable method. The commit notes `inputBinding` "resolves a long-standing issue with `setInput` where it wasn't possible to set the initial value of an input before the first change detection run." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60137, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fe57332fc5c4e6b44f01b9b4343385e90b3edf77)

**Affects:** Applications that create components dynamically (`createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, `ComponentFactory.create`) and set inputs via `ComponentRef.setInput` or subscribe to outputs manually. Purely additive for other applications.

**Action:** none — existing `ComponentRef.setInput` behaviour is unchanged; optionally migrate manual input/output wiring to the new `bindings` array (with `directives` for host directives) as described in the "Binding inputs, outputs and setting host directives at creation" section of the programmatic rendering guide (https://angular.dev/guide/components/programmatic-rendering).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// From angular.love/angular-20-whats-new (Angular 20 form)
this.vcr().createComponent(AppWarningComponent, {
  bindings: [
    inputBinding('canClose', this.canClose),
    twoWayBinding('isExpanded', this.isExpanded),
    outputBinding<boolean>('close', (isConfirmed) => console.log(isConfirmed))
  ],
  directives: [
    FocusTrap,
    {
      type: ThemeDirective,
      bindings: [/* ... */]
    }
  ]
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 added a new public API in `@angular/core`: `provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners(): EnvironmentProviders`, exported from `error_handler.ts` alongside `ErrorHandler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/65adb3024d6ae2af14952f4afdb2b92b600da074, https://angular.dev/api/core/provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners). It "provides an environment initializer which forwards unhandled errors to the `ErrorHandler`"; the listeners added are for the window's `unhandledrejection` and `error` events, and the listeners call `preventDefault()` and are removed via `DestroyRef.onDestroy`. It is useful for both ZoneJS apps (errors thrown outside the Angular Zone reach the window) and Zoneless apps (errors not caught by the framework reach the window); without it, these errors would not be reported to `ErrorHandler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60704). Note the internal token reports through `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER`, and adding the provider more than once installs the listeners only once.

**Affects:** Applications that rely on `ErrorHandler` receiving global uncaught errors/unhandled promise rejections, particularly apps moving to zoneless (`provideZonelessChangeDetection()`). Opt-in only — upgrading alone changes nothing.

**Action:** required: add `provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners()` (imported from `@angular/core`) to the `providers` of `bootstrapApplication` / `AppConfig`, and re-verify that uncaught errors and unhandled rejections still reach the app's `ErrorHandler`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// After (v20) — from
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideZonelessChangeDetection(),
    provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners()
  ]
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds the `twoWayBinding()` function exported from `@angular/core`. It accepts the public name of a two-way compatible input and a `WritableSignal`, and returns a `Binding` instance that, when passed in the `bindings` array of `createComponent` or `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, keeps the component’s model input and the signal in sync (input updates the signal; the component’s output writes back to the signal) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b154fb391142db6139cddad820a9a72838f0f16c, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60342, https://angular.dev/api/core/twoWayBinding). Before this change, dynamically created components had to set inputs via `ComponentRef.setInput` and subscribe to outputs manually after creation.

**Affects:** Applications that dynamically create components with `createComponent` (standalone) or `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` and need a two-way (`model()`) binding to a signal.

**Action:** none (this is an additive feature; existing code continues to work). To adopt, import `twoWayBinding` from `@angular/core` and add it to the `bindings` option of `createComponent` / `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {createComponent, signal, twoWayBinding} from '@angular/core';
const value = signal('');
createComponent(MyCheckbox, {
  bindings: [
    twoWayBinding('value', value),
  ],
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 added the ability to apply directives and declarative bindings when creating components dynamically. `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` and `ComponentFactory.create` now accept a `directives` option (`(Type<unknown> | DirectiveWithBindings<unknown>)[]`) and a `bindings` option (`Binding[]`) in their options object (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/82aa2c1a527be85e09f0f660ece56b594bff5a76, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/src/linker/view_container_ref.ts). Bindings are built with the new public helpers `inputBinding(publicName, value)`, `outputBinding<T>(eventName, listener)` and `twoWayBinding(publicName, writableSignal)` exported from `@angular/core` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/src/render3/dynamic_bindings.ts, https://angular.dev/api/core/createComponent). Per the PR, these go through the same mechanisms as the template (so `outputBinding` auto-cleans up on destroy), can target specific directives via `DirectiveWithBindings` (`{ type: Type<T>, bindings: Binding[] }`), and are tree-shakeable unlike `setInput` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60137). These are additive optional parameters; previous calls without them behave the same.

**Affects:** Applications that dynamically create components via `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, or `ComponentFactory.create`; no change required for existing calls since `directives` and `bindings` are optional.

**Action:** none (optional new API). To use it, add `directives` and/or `bindings` (using `inputBinding`, `outputBinding`, `twoWayBinding`) to the `createComponent` options object.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New (Angular 20) — from https://angular.dev/guide/components/programmatic-rendering
this.vcr().createComponent(AppWarning, {
  bindings: [
    inputBinding('canClose', this.canClose),
    twoWayBinding('isExpanded', this.isExpanded),
    outputBinding<boolean>('close', (isConfirmed) => console.log(isConfirmed)),
  ],
  directives: [FocusTrap, { type: ThemeDirective, bindings: [...] }],
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 dropped support for TypeScript versions less than 5.8; the `@angular/compiler-cli` package now declares a strict peer dependency `typescript: >=5.8 <5.9` (previously lower bounds like 5.4–5.7 were supported). This is a breaking change that narrows the supported TypeScript range to exactly the 5.8.x line. The Angular 20 source repo also pins its own dev dependency to `typescript: "5.8.3"`.

**Affects:** all Angular applications and libraries built with `@angular/compiler-cli` (the `ng` CLI / Angular Compiler) that currently depend on TypeScript versions older than 5.8 (e.g. `~5.4`, `~5.5`, `~5.6`, `~5.7`) or that pin a 5.9/6.x pre-release.

**Action:** required: update the project's `typescript` dependency to a version satisfying `>=5.8 <5.9` (e.g. `5.8.3`) so that installing/upgrading `@angular/compiler-cli` v20 does not fail the peer-dependency resolution, then re-verify that the project compiles cleanly with TypeScript 5.8.

**Before/after:**
```json
// package.json (peerDependencies of @angular/compiler-cli)
{
  "peerDependencies": {
    "typescript": ">=5.8 <5.9"
  }
}
```
Sources: https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60197 , https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/326d48afb4266ef9b028860e2f845de005653d75 , https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/compiler-cli/package.json , https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/package.json

**What changed:** Angular's internal profiler callback (registered via `setProfiler`) now receives the component's template function as the third argument (`eventFn`) for template-related events (template create/update), whereas previously only lifecycle hooks and output listeners were passed there — template events sent nothing. The third parameter of the `profiler` function was renamed from `hookOrListener` to `eventFn`, documented as "function associated with event. For example a template function, lifecycle hook, or output listener" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/d260ca3091a6de215ba31f2516134d1aa11fe04c/packages/core/src/render3/profiler.ts; v19 param was `hookOrListener` per https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.0/packages/core/src/render3/profiler.ts). The commit states this is "fully backwards compatible, since previously these template events did not use the third arg of the profiler function" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60174).

**Affects:** Only applications that register a profiler via the internal `setProfiler` API (marked "*INTERNAL* and should not be relied upon in application's code") and inspect the third callback argument during template create/update events. Regular applications are unaffected.

**Action:** none — the change is additive and backwards compatible; profilers that ignore the third argument behave identically. Profiler tooling can now optionally read `eventFn` for template events.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19 (profiler.ts)
export const profiler: Profiler = function (event, instance, hookOrListener) { ... }

// v20 (profiler.ts)
export const profiler: Profiler = function (event, instance = null, eventFn) { ... }
```

**What changed:** Prior to Angular 20.0.0, `Injector.create()` returned an `Injector` instance that did not expose the `destroy()` method, even though the returned instance was internally destroyable. In Angular 20.0.0, the `destroy()` method is explicitly exposed on the `Injector` interface/type for instances created with `Injector.create()`, allowing explicit lifecycle management for user-owned injectors (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60054, https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new). The internal implementation of the injector remained unchanged; this change is purely about the public API surface (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4812215a7b3bcb54bce3f017d89246aa39af2cc5).
**Affects:** Applications that call `Injector.create()` to manually instantiate dependency injection containers, which previously had no way to manually destroy those instances or call `destroy()` on them.
**Action:** required: Manually calling `.destroy()` on an `Injector` created with `Injector.create()` is now supported and recommended if these injector instances are created dynamically and no longer needed.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular v19 - destroy() was not exposed in the API
const injector = Injector.create({ providers: [...] });
// destroy() call would fail with a type error if injector was typed as Injector
// injector.destroy(); // ❌

// Angular v20 - destroy() is exposed
const injector = Injector.create({ providers: [...] });
// destroy() can be called
injector.destroy(); // ✅
```

**What changed:** Angular v20 adds a new public `enableProfiling()` function to `@angular/core` (PR #60789, commit c1bcae91d) that integrates Angular runtime data with the Chrome DevTools Performance panel extensibility API, using `console.timeStamp` for low overhead (https://blog.angular.dev/announcing-angular-v20-b5c9c06cf301, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c1bcae91ddb03efb04451799fbf92e9fd1e82026). When enabled, a custom "Angular" track appears in the Chrome performance timeline showing component/provider instantiation, template execution, change detection cycles, lifecycle hooks and event listener execution, with color-coded entries (blue = developer TypeScript code, purple = compiler-transformed templates, green = entry points) (https://angular.dev/best-practices/profiling-with-chrome-devtools). It can also be enabled ad hoc at runtime via the global `ng.enableProfiling()` in the DevTools console. Profiling works exclusively in development mode (https://angular.dev/best-practices/profiling-with-chrome-devtools).

**Affects:** All applications upgrading to v20; nothing is enabled or changed automatically — this is purely an opt-in new capability.

**Action:** none. Optionally, developers wanting the Chrome DevTools Angular track can import and call `enableProfiling()` from `@angular/core` before bootstrapping, or call `ng.enableProfiling()` in the DevTools console; this is a debugging convenience, not a migration requirement.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// New in v20 (optional) — enable before bootstrap to capture start-up events
import {enableProfiling} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {MyApp} from './my-app';

// Turn on profiling before bootstrapping your application
// in order to capture all of the code run on start-up.
enableProfiling();
bootstrapApplication(MyApp);
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 added a new dev-mode-only DI profiler event `InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent` (commit message: "This new event allows us to measure DI tokens instantiation time") (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60158, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/809b5b4596cafcdabdb1c5fa92fcab539c6f637f). A new enum member `InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent` was added to the const enum `InjectorProfilerEventType`, and a new interface `InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent` (fields `type`, `context: InjectorProfilerContext`, `token: ProviderToken`) was added to the union type `InjectorProfilerEvent`. A new helper `emitInjectorToCreateInstanceEvent(token)` emits the event right before an injector/factory creates an instance, so it pairs with the existing `InstanceCreatedByInjector` event (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/809b5b4596cafcdabdb1c5fa92fcab539c6f637f.patch). These symbols live in internal paths (`packages/core/src/render3/debug/injector_profiler.ts`) and are not part of the public `@angular/core` API surface.

**Affects:** Only applications that register a DI profiler via `setProfiler` (or `setupFrameworkInjectorProfiler`) and exhaustively switch/handle every `InjectorProfilerEventType` member. Normal applications are unaffected.

**Action:** none for normal applications. Applications with a custom `setProfiler` callback that branches on `InjectorProfilerEventType` must re-verify or add a case for the new `InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent` so new events are not dropped; otherwise `none`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// After: new event interface and enum member added to the profiler union
export interface InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent {
  type: InjectorProfilerEventType.InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent;
  context: InjectorProfilerContext;
  token: ProviderToken<unknown>;
}

export type InjectorProfilerEvent =
  | InjectedServiceEvent
  | InjectorToCreateInstanceEvent
  | InjectorCreatedInstanceEvent
  | ProviderConfiguredEvent
  | EffectCreatedEvent;
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0, `TestBed.flushEffects()` was initially removed in commit d5fd7349fb (PR #60993), but was subsequently re-introduced as deprecated in PR #61462 due to documentation issues regarding its "Developer Preview" status. Currently, `TestBed.flushEffects()` delegates directly to `TestBed.tick()`. The new `TestBed.tick()` method performs full synchronization (change detection, root effects, component effects, etc.) to mirror `ApplicationRef.tick()` behavior in production, whereas `flushEffects` previously only flushed pending effects. This change makes unit tests more symmetric to production but can break tests that relied on the narrower scope of `flushEffects` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60993, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61462, https://cookbook.marmicode.io/angular/testing/flushing-flusheffects).

**Affects:** Applications with unit tests that call `TestBed.flushEffects()`.

**Action:** required: replace calls to `TestBed.flushEffects()` with `TestBed.tick()` and re-verify test results, as `tick()` triggers a more complete synchronization cycle that may cause previously passing tests to fail if they relied on the specific behavior of only flushing effects.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19)
TestBed.flushEffects();

// After (Angular 20)
TestBed.tick();
```

**What changed:** In Angular 19, `toObservable` in `@angular/core/rxjs-interop` was annotated with `@developerPreview`; in Angular 20 it is annotated with `@publicApi 20.0`, marking the function and the `ToObservableOptions` interface as stable API (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4e88e18a8ef0f19aed85316e80627ad6d2ec80a7, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_observable.ts, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_observable.ts). Alongside this, the internal helper `toObservableMicrotask` (which used the private `ɵmicrotaskEffect` symbol from `@angular/core`) was removed in the 20.0 source, while `toObservable` now guards the injection-context assertion with `ngDevMode` and uses the public `effect` import from `../../src/core` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_observable.ts, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_observable.ts). The public signature `toObservable<T>(source: Signal<T>, options?: ToObservableOptions): Observable<T>` is unchanged (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/to_observable.ts).

**Affects:** Applications that call `toObservable(...)` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop` (the developer-preview warning is gone); additionally, applications that directly imported the non-public `toObservableMicrotask` export from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`.

**Action:** none for ordinary `toObservable` callers; required: remove any direct imports of `toObservableMicrotask` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop` and re-verify that calls to `toObservable` outside an injection context pass `options.injector`, since the `assertInInjectionContext(toObservable)` check now runs only under `ngDevMode`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// 19.0 (developer preview, plus internal microtask variant)
import {ɵmicrotaskEffect as microtaskEffect} from '@angular/core';
/** @developerPreview */
export function toObservable<T>(source: Signal<T>, options?: ToObservableOptions): Observable<T>;
export function toObservableMicrotask<T>(source: Signal<T>, options?: ToObservableOptions): Observable<T>;

// 20.0 (stable)
/** @publicApi 20.0 */
export function toObservable<T>(source: Signal<T>, options?: ToObservableOptions): Observable<T>;
```

**What changed:** The `linkedSignal` API, introduced as experimental in prior versions, has been stabilized and marked as public API starting with Angular 20.0.0. This was achieved via commit 727cda385690066c0bbc94734e344cf5ad741e9a which followed up on PR 60741 that originally stabilized the API. The API remains in `@angular/core` and now carries the "stable since v20.0" designation, allowing production use without experimental warnings or breaking-change guarantees for minor/patch updates.
**Affects:** Applications that import or use `linkedSignal` from `@angular/core`, or that previously required experimental feature flags or suppression warnings for signal-dependent writable state.
**Action:** required: Remove any experimental feature flags, compiler options, or TypeScript suppression comments (e.g., `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-expect-error`) that were used to silence experimental API warnings for `linkedSignal`. Verify that signal-based writable state using `linkedSignal` behaves correctly with the new stable semantics.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19 - Experimental)
// Might require @ts-expect-error or experimental flag
// @ts-expect-error: linkedSignal is not yet stable
selectedItem = linkedSignal(() => this.items());

// After (Angular 20 - Public/Stable)
import { linkedSignal, input } from '@angular/core';

selectedItem = linkedSignal(() => this.items());
```

**What changed:** `provideExperimentalCheckNoChangesForDebug` was renamed to `provideCheckNoChangesConfig` and promoted from experimental to developer preview (since v20.0) in `@angular/core` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60906, https://angular.dev/api/core/provideCheckNoChangesConfig). The `exhaustive` behavior is now applied to *all* `checkNoChanges` runs — all views attached to `ApplicationRef` and their descendants are treated as `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager`/`Default` when verifying no expressions changed, so `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` is now also surfaced for `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` components that were not marked for check (this fixes ). Exhaustive checking can be turned off via `provideCheckNoChangesConfig({ exhaustive: false })`. The `useNgZoneOnStable` option was removed; only `interval?: number` (periodic `checkNoChanges` runs, useful in zoneless apps) and the required `exhaustive` flag remain in the options, and the function returns `EnvironmentProviders` (https://angular.dev/api/core/provideCheckNoChangesConfig).

**Affects:** applications that call `provideExperimentalCheckNoChangesForDebug` in providers (e.g. `app.config.ts`), especially apps using `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`, which may now see new `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` failures in dev mode.

**Action:** required: rename the imported provider to `provideCheckNoChangesConfig` (from `@angular/core`); remove any `useNgZoneOnStable` option and use `interval` instead; add `exhaustive: true` (or `exhaustive: false` to opt out of the new always-exhaustive checking) since the option is required; re-verify the app in dev mode for newly thrown `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` from OnPush components.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19)
provideExperimentalCheckNoChangesForDebug({ useNgZoneOnStable: true })

// After (Angular 20, developer preview)
// exhaustive: true — treat all views as Eager/Default during checkNoChanges
// interval — periodically run checkNoChanges on application views
provideCheckNoChangesConfig({ exhaustive: true, interval: 1000 })

// Opt out of exhaustive checks (previous default behavior for OnPush)
provideCheckNoChangesConfig({ exhaustive: false })
```
(Signature `options: { interval?: number; exhaustive: true }` / `{ exhaustive: false }` returning `EnvironmentProviders` copied from https://angular.dev/api/core/provideCheckNoChangesConfig; renaming and option removal from https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60906.)

**What changed:** The `DOCUMENT` injection token was moved from `@angular/common` into `@angular/core`, because it is relevant for many SSR use cases and "users shouldn't have to install `common` for it" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7ccec1494f864a485d2188da62d2006c31849f3f). To preserve backwards compatibility, `DOCUMENT` is still re-exported through `@angular/common`, so existing `import { DOCUMENT } from '@angular/common'` statements keep compiling (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60663). The PR was labeled `area: migrations`, and third-party write-ups of the v20 release note that a migration rewrites the imports automatically if needed ().

**Affects:** Applications that inject `DOCUMENT` (e.g. `constructor(@Inject(DOCUMENT) private doc: Document)`) imported from `@angular/common`; also SSR code that previously needed `@angular/common` solely for this token.

**Action:** none — the change is backwards compatible (re-export kept) and the `ng update @angular/core@20 @angular/cli@20` migrations rewrite the imports; optionally switch imports to `@angular/core` manually.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (v19)
import { DOCUMENT } from '@angular/common';

// after (v20) — both still work, core is the new canonical location
import { DOCUMENT } from '@angular/core';
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20, zoneless change detection moved from "Experimental" to "Developer Preview", and the provider API was renamed: `provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection` → `provideZonelessChangeDetection`, exported from `@angular/core` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/953c4b25808b357e78bf1cf6b2ef8b4a84ffaf49, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60748). The commit notes a temporary re-export of the provider under the old name pending internal cleanup ("Remove re-export of the provider once g3 cleanup to the new name is finished"), so the old name must not be relied on (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60748). Docs moved from "experimental features" to "Best practices -> Performance" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60748). Related v20 rename in the same area: `provideExperimentalCheckNoChangesForDebug` → `provideCheckNoChangesConfig` (). The Angular CLI flag `ng new --experimental-zoneless` was renamed to `--zoneless` (). In v20 zoneless is still opt-in via `provideZonelessChangeDetection()` at bootstrap (standalone or NgModule providers) (https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless).

**Affects:** Applications that enabled zoneless change detection in Angular 19 via `provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection()`.

**Action:** required: rename every import and call of `provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection` from `@angular/core` to `provideZonelessChangeDetection`; if the app relies on the no-changes debug check, also switch `provideExperimentalCheckNoChangesForDebug` to `provideCheckNoChangesConfig`; re-verify the zoneless docs location (`guide/zoneless`, now under Performance best practices).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19 (experimental) — old name, removed as a supported API in v20
import { provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';

// v20 (developer preview)
import { provideZonelessChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
```

**What changed:** The deprecated `InjectFlags` enum was removed from the `@angular/core` public API. As a result, `inject`, `Injector.get`, `EnvironmentInjector.get`, `TestBed.get`, and `TestBed.inject` no longer accept `InjectFlags` as an argument. Instead of bitwise flags, these APIs now require the object literal options syntax. The internal implementation replaces public `InjectFlags` with `InternalInjectFlags` which is a `const enum` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60318).

**Affects:** Applications passing `InjectFlags` to DI functions and methods like `inject`, `Injector.get`, `EnvironmentInjector.get`, `TestBed.get`, or `TestBed.inject`.

**Action:** required: Run the automated migration. The migration schematic replaces usages of `InjectFlags` with the equivalent options object (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e170d242402f5ee4671dd425c24e5f31fbc67b21).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import { inject, InjectFlags, Directive, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';

@Directive()
export class Dir {
  el = inject(ElementRef, InjectFlags.Optional | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.SkipSelf);
}
```

**What changed:** The deprecated `get(token: any, notFoundValue?: any): any` method was removed from the `TestBed` interface and `TestBedImpl` (both the static and instance overloads, which had been marked `@deprecated from v9.0.0 use TestBed.inject`) in `packages/core/testing/src/test_bed.ts`, and the entry was deleted from the public API golden `goldens/public-api/core/testing/index.api.md` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5e209cb560fdfb943169a09d35389c703fa71418). It applied to `TestBed.get(...)` and `getTestBed().get(...)` calls. Rationale: "`TestBed.get` isn't type safe and has been deprecated for several years now" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60414). The old signature defaulted `notFoundValue` to `Injector.THROW_IF_NOT_FOUND` and internally delegated to `this.inject(token, notFoundValue, options)`. The v20 release notes state: "`TestBed.get` has been removed. Use `TestBed.inject` instead" (), and an automated migration runs on `ng update` to rewrite calls to `TestBed.inject()` ().

**Affects:** Applications with spec/test code calling `TestBed.get(...)` or `getTestBed().get(...)`, or code passing the `TestBed` object itself where an `Injector` was expected (e.g. `modFactory.create(TestBed)`, `new HammerGesturesPlugin(document, config, TestBed)`).

**Action:** required: Replace `TestBed.get(token)` / `getTestBed().get(token)` with `TestBed.inject(token)` / `getTestBed().inject(token)` in test files — this is done automatically by the `ng update @angular/core` v20 migration, so re-verify the result compiles (results previously typed via an explicit variable annotation now get inferred types). Where `TestBed` was used as an `Injector`, pass `TestBed.inject(Injector)` instead (import `Injector` from `@angular/core`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (v19)
const router: Router = TestBed.get(Router);
const testBedInjector: Injector = TestBed.get(Injector);
const module = modFactory.create(TestBed);

// After (v20)
const router = TestBed.inject(Router);
const testBedInjector = TestBed.inject(Injector);
const module = modFactory.create(TestBed.inject(Injector));
```

**What changed:** The `afterRender` function from `@angular/core` has been renamed to `afterEveryRender`. Additionally, the renamed `afterEveryRender` (and its companion `afterNextRender`) API is now stable. The old `afterRender` name was removed without backward compatibility or an automatic migration schematic (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d8fbb909ce4380c0ea48512cf5d364a2785fd428, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications using the `afterRender` function or importing it from `@angular/core`.

**Action:** required: Replace all usages of `afterRender` with `afterEveryRender`. Update imports to ensure `afterEveryRender` is imported from `@angular/core`. Re-verify any logic that depended on the timing or behavior of `afterRender` to ensure it functions correctly with the renamed API.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import { Component, afterRender } from '@angular/core';

@Component({...})
export class MyComponent {
  constructor() {
    afterRender(() => window.scrollTo(0, 0));
  }
}
```
```typescript
import { Component, afterEveryRender } from '@angular/core';

@Component({...})
export class MyComponent {
  constructor() {
    afterEveryRender(() => window.scrollTo(0, 0));
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The "developer preview" label has been removed from incremental hydration APIs in Angular v20.0.0, marking them as stable and production-ready (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/567522398ffc4149e726ef30d87b0169ae6f3e21). The official release notes confirm no breaking changes were introduced in the API itself during this stabilization (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60888).
**Affects:** Applications that use the `provideClientHydration` function with incremental hydration features, such as `withIncrementalHydration`, `withEventReplay`, or route-level rendering modes.
**Action:** required: re-verify incremental hydration and event replay behavior to ensure it functions correctly in production environments, as it is no longer subject to developer preview warnings or experimental limitations.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular 20+ (Stable)
import { bootstrapApplication, provideClientHydration, withIncrementalHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    // No longer "experimental" or "developer preview"
    provideClientHydration(withIncrementalHydration()),
  ],
});
```

**What changed:** The `linkedSignal` API was promoted from developer preview to stable in Angular 20.0.0 (#60741) and explicitly marked as public API (#60865) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60741, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60865). `linkedSignal` creates a writable signal whose value is automatically recomputed from a `source` signal using a `compute` function (https://angular.dev/guide/signals/linked-signal). This stabilization means Angular no longer reserves the right to make breaking changes to its signature or behavior in patch/minor releases. Note that the `linkedSignal` function itself (signature and options like `source`, `computation`, `previous`) remained unchanged; only its stability tier changed (https://angular.dev/guide/signals/linked-signal).

**Affects:** Applications currently using `linkedSignal()` from `@angular/core` (imported via the developer-preview path in v19).

**Action:** none (no code changes required, as the API is stable; re-verify runtime behavior after updating to v20)

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular 19 (Developer Preview)
import { signal, linkedSignal } from '@angular/core';
// Code remains the same, but the API was considered 'preview'.

// Angular 20 (Stable API)
import { signal, linkedSignal } from '@angular/core';
const source = signal(10);
const derived = linkedSignal({
  source,
  computation: (newSource) => newSource * 2
});
```

**What changed:** The `PendingTasks` injectable class in `@angular/core` is promoted to stable, while the `PendingTasks.run` method and the `@angular/core/rxjs-interop` operator `pendingUntilEvent` (which relies on `PendingTasks` internally) remain in developer preview (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/866cea9a057ac67fa3f679f1f3da18700926c15a). No public API surface, class name, or method signature changes occurred; only the support status changed (https://v20.angular.dev/api/core/PendingTasks).

**Affects:** Applications that inject `PendingTasks` to control SSR stabilization, or that use `PendingTasks.add`, `PendingTasks.run`, or `pendingUntilEvent`.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import { PendingTasks } from '@angular/core';
import { pendingUntilEvent } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

const tasks = inject(PendingTasks);

const cleanup = tasks.add();
tasks.run(async () => { /* ... */ });
source$.pipe(pendingUntilEvent());
```

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 introduced the `outputBinding` function in `@angular/core` to support listening to outputs on dynamically-created components, mirroring template binding behavior. Previously, users had to manually retrieve the component instance and subscribe to its `EventEmitter`. `outputBinding` ensures errors are forwarded to `ErrorHandler`, the view is marked dirty, and listeners are automatically cleaned up when the component is destroyed. It also correctly handles host directive outputs. This function is currently available for use in the `bindings` option of `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, and `ComponentFactory.create` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/be44cc8f40fb2364dbaf20ba24496e4355f84e78, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60137, https://angular.dev/api/core/outputBinding).

**Affects:** Applications that use `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, or `ComponentFactory.create` to dynamically create components and bind to their outputs.

**Action:** none: Applications can continue to manually subscribe to outputs via the component instance. However, to adopt the new behavior (automatic cleanup, error handling, host directive support), update the component creation call to use `outputBinding` in the `bindings` array.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Manual subscription)
const ref = createComponent(MyCheckbox);
ref.instance.onChange.subscribe(event => console.log(event.value));

// After (Using outputBinding in v20.0.0)
import { createComponent, outputBinding } from '@angular/core';

interface CheckboxChange { value: string; }

createComponent(MyCheckbox, {
  bindings: [
    outputBinding<CheckboxChange>('onChange', event => console.log(event.value))
  ]
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 dropped Node.js v18 (End-of-Life April 30, 2025) and Node.js 22.0–22.10; the supported runtime range became `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0` per the repo `engines.node` field on the 20.0.x branch, while 19.0.x supported `^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1 || ^22.0.0` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60545, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fe9b79b615bb72989498c2dc9e84a89e618ebeb4, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/package.json).
**Affects:** All applications and libraries built or tested with Angular v20; developers running Angular CLI, builds (`ng build`, `ng test`, `ng serve`) or SSR on Node.js v18 or Node.js 22.0–22.10.
**Action:** required: switch the development, CI and runtime Node.js to at least 20.19.0 (per the 20.0.x `engines.node` field) or the minimum stated in the changelog, 20.11.1; re-verify that local tooling, CI runners and deployment environments no longer pin Node.js 18.x or 22.0–22.10. Note: the PR/commit text states "at least 20.11.1" while the 20.0.x package `engines` field lists `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0` — use the `engines` range as authoritative.

**Before/after:**
```json
// Angular 19.x engines (per compatibility table)
"engines": {
  "node": "^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1 || ^22.0.0"
}

// Angular 20.0.x engines (repo package.json)
"engines": {
  "node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0"
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds an automated `ng update` migration away from the deprecated `InjectFlags` API (commit e170d24240, PR #60318) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/e170d242402f5ee4671dd425c24e5f31fbc67b21), and a companion commit (611baaf069) removes `InjectFlags` from the `@angular/core` public API entirely. Breaking changes per the PR: `InjectFlags` has been removed; `inject`, `Injector.get`, `EnvironmentInjector.get`, `TestBed.get`, and `TestBed.inject` no longer accept `InjectFlags` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60318). Internally, remaining usages were replaced with an `InternalInjectFlags` `const enum` (not public) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60318). The migration converts bitwise flags to the options-object syntax, e.g. `inject(ElementRef, InjectFlags.Optional | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.SkipSelf)` becomes `inject(ElementRef, { optional: true, host: true, skipSelf: true })` (https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new).

**Affects:** Applications that reference `InjectFlags` or pass bitwise flags as the second argument to `inject`, `Injector.get`, `EnvironmentInjector.get`, `TestBed.get`, or `TestBed.inject`.

**Action:** required: run `ng update` so the automatic migration rewrites flag usage to the options object; then re-verify any DI call sites the schematic could not convert (e.g. dynamically computed flag values) and remove remaining `InjectFlags` imports — these APIs no longer accept the enum at compile time.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19)
import { inject, InjectFlags, Directive, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
@Directive()
export class Dir {
  el = inject(ElementRef, InjectFlags.Optional | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.SkipSelf);
}

// After (Angular 20, options-object syntax)
el = inject(ElementRef, { optional: true, host: true, skipSelf: true });
```
(before-snippet and after-snippet from https://angular.love/angular-20-whats-new)

**What changed:** In Angular 20.0.0, the `@angular/core` package metadata adds `"@angular/compiler": "20.0.0"` to `peerDependencies` and marks it optional via `"peerDependenciesMeta": { "@angular/compiler": { "optional": true } }` (https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@angular/core@20.0.0/package.json). In 19.0.0, `peerDependencies` contained only `rxjs` and `zone.js`, and `peerDependenciesMeta` was absent (https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@angular/core@19.0.0/package.json). Reason given in the PR: `@angular/core/testing` imports `@angular/compiler` without listing it as a dependency, so strict/hoisting-free package managers such as pnpm and Yarn 2 PnP did not link `@angular/compiler` for `@angular/core` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/55610, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7eb59d38872667c73e09a42e4260e8a58f102448, issue #38096). The PR states it is not a breaking change; no source code change is required — the fix is in published package metadata only (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/55610).

**Affects:** Applications/workspaces resolved by strict package managers (pnpm, Yarn PnP) that import `@angular/core/testing` or otherwise rely on the JIT compiler being reachable from `@angular/core`; npm/yarn-classic users are unaffected behaviourally. Libraries listing `@angular/core` as a peer dependency may now see an additional optional peer.

**Action:** none — re-verify that `@angular/compiler` still resolves for tests after updating (previously added `.yarnrc.yml` package extensions / pnpm workarounds for issue #38096 can be removed, and `@angular/compiler` must be at exactly the matching `20.0.0`-line version to satisfy the new optional peer).

**Before/after:**
```json
// @angular/core@19.0.0
"dependencies": { "tslib": "^2.3.0" },
"peerDependencies": {
  "rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0",
  "zone.js": "~0.15.0"
}

// @angular/core@20.0.0
"dependencies": { "tslib": "^2.3.0" },
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/compiler": "20.0.0",
  "rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0",
  "zone.js": "~0.15.0"
},
"peerDependenciesMeta": {
  "@angular/compiler": { "optional": true },
  "zone.js": { "optional": true }
}
```

**What changed:** In v19 `PendingTasks.run` (from `@angular/core`, developer preview) was declared as `async run<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T>` — it awaited the function and returned its result, with `removeTask()` in a `finally` block (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/packages/core/src/pending_tasks.ts). In v20 it became `run(fn: () => Promise<unknown>): void`, calling `fn().catch(this.errorHandler).finally(removeTask)` where `errorHandler` is injected via `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER`, so rejections are reported to the Angular error handler instead of becoming dangling (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/src/pending_tasks.ts). The returned `Promise` is gone; the 19.x docs even showed awaiting it (`const userData = await pendingTasks.run(() => fetch('/api/user'));`). The commit/PR state: "PendingTasks.run no longer returns the result of the async function. If this behavior is desired, it can be re-implemented manually with the `PendingTasks.add`. Be aware, however, that promise rejections will need to be handled or they can cause the node process to shut down when using SSR." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60044, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7232ce5b17c9cce87bebe41c81f55043f21e639b). In v20 `PendingTasks` also became `@publicApi 20.0` (stable) (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/src/pending_tasks.ts).

**Affects:** Code that calls `PendingTasks.run(...)` and uses/awaits its return value (typed as `Promise<T>` in v19), or relied on rejection propagation from the call site. Not a TypeScript error for callers who ignored the return value.

**Action:** required: remove `await`/`.then`/`async` usage of the value returned by `PendingTasks.run` (it now returns `void`); where the resolved result is needed, re-implement with `PendingTasks.add()` — call the cleanup function when work finishes — and attach your own `.catch`/`try-catch` to the promise, since unhandled rejections from manually-added tasks can shut down the node process under SSR. Re-verify that failures in these async tasks now surface through the application error handler (`ErrorHandler`) rather than at the call site.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// v19 (packages/core/src/pending_tasks.ts)
async run<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const removeTask = this.add();
  try {
    return await fn();
  } finally {
    removeTask();
  }
}

// v20
run(fn: () => Promise<unknown>): void {
  const removeTask = this.add();
  fn().catch(this.errorHandler).finally(removeTask);
}
```

**What changed:** In `@defer` blocks, two error paths previously routed through the injectable `ErrorHandler` token (`injector.get(ErrorHandler).handleError(error)`) now route to Angular's internal application error handler (`INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER` in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/shared.ts`, via a new `handleUncaughtError` that replaced `handleError` calls in `packages/core/src/defer/rendering.ts` `renderDeferBlockState` and `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts` `triggerResourceLoading`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fd12220a35665d2378b74905c998fcff6130eb91). This covers errors while rendering a defer state and dependency-loading failures when no `@error` block is configured ("...but no \`@error\` block was configured... Consider using the `@error` block to render an error state."). Because the internal handler also rethrows, tests using `TestBed` now rethrow these defer errors by default; the Angular docs show opting out with `TestBed.configureTestingModule({rethrowApplicationErrors: false})` (https://angular.dev/best-practices/error-handling).

**Affects:** Applications using `@defer` blocks that rely on a custom `ErrorHandler` provider to intercept defer dependency-load/render failures, and unit tests exercising defer error scenarios without an `@error` block.

**Action:** required: re-verify custom `ErrorHandler` logging still receives defer failures (it no longer intercepts them at the source — errors now flow through Angular's application error handler), and for tests that intentionally cover defer load failures, add `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` to `TestBed.configureTestingModule`, or add an `@error` block so errors are rendered instead of reported.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Angular internal deferral path (v19): reported to user-provided token
const errorHandler = injector ? injector.get(ErrorHandler, null) : null;
errorHandler && errorHandler.handleError(error);

// Angular v20: reported to the application error handler
const errorHandler = injector.get(INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER, null);
errorHandler?.(error);

// TestBed opt-out for tests covering defer error cases
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  rethrowApplicationErrors: false,
});
```

**What changed:** A new error `RuntimeErrorCode.INVALID_SET_INPUT_CALL` (code `317`) was added to Angular core. If a component instance was created via `createComponent`, `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, or `ComponentFactory.create` with an active `inputBinding`, calling `ComponentRef.setInput` now throws: "Cannot call `setInput` on a component that is using the `inputBinding` function." This is enforced in dev mode only (`ngDevMode`) in `ComponentRef.setInput`. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3459faadbfce9be7b1ca69f4d4db82a65b31de50.patch)
**Affects:** Applications that dynamically create components and pass an `inputBinding` (or a directive with an `inputBinding`) through the new `bindings` option of `createComponent`, and then also call `setInput` on the resulting `ComponentRef` in dev mode.
**Action:** required: re-verify any dynamically created components that mix `inputBinding` with `ComponentRef.setInput` and migrate the remaining `setInput` calls to `inputBinding` (or remove the `inputBinding`) so the same mechanism sets those inputs.
**Before/after:**
```ts
// Throws in v20 dev mode:
const ref = createComponent(RootComp, {
  hostElement, environmentInjector,
  bindings: [inputBinding('someInput', () => 'hello')],
});
ref.setInput('someInput', 'changed'); // throws INVALID_SET_INPUT_CALL (317)
```

**What changed:** Angular now installs global `error` and `unhandledrejection` event listeners outside the Angular zone via `Zone.root.run(setupEventListeners)` (or `setupEventListeners()` when `Zone` is undefined), instead of calling `window.addEventListener('error', errorListener)` and `window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', rejectionListener)` directly. Previously these listeners ran inside the zone, so their callbacks triggered change detection every time they fired; now they run without invoking change detection (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0ac949c266637ab723430ff17adb1af58b14fa0d). The change lives in `packages/core/src/error_handler.ts` behind the `globalErrorListeners` `InjectionToken`, so global error/unhandledrejection callbacks no longer cause a change-detection cycle, and code that expected CD to run as a side effect of these listeners will no longer see the view refresh.

**Affects:** Applications (using Zone.js) whose views or state updates relied on change detection being implicitly triggered by global `error` or `unhandledrejection` events; the listeners themselves are still registered and still report to `ErrorHandler`.

**Action:** required: re-verify that any state mutations performed inside global `error`/`unhandledrejection` handlers still refresh the UI; such updates must now be applied via signals or otherwise trigger change detection explicitly, since these callbacks no longer run change detection. No build, dependency, or configuration change is indicated by the fetched sources.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', rejectionListener);
window.addEventListener('error', errorListener);

// After
const setupEventListeners = () => {
  window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', rejectionListener);
  window.addEventListener('error', errorListener);
};
if (typeof Zone !== 'undefined') {
  Zone.root.run(setupEventListeners);
} else {
  setupEventListeners();
}
```

**What changed:** Previously, event-stashing code (the `stashEventListeners` Map and stashing path) was always bundled into `@angular/core`, so every application carried it. This commit reworks the feature so the stashing logic is registered only when the event replay feature is enabled: the no-op default `_stashEventListenerImpl` is swapped for the real implementation only after `enableStashEventListenerImpl()` is called, which `withEventReplay()` (in `@angular/platform-browser`) invokes at app-init. As a result the event-replay/stashing code is now tree-shakable and excluded from bundles that do not call `withEventReplay()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4fe34f4cfea2aa0e355afa04c7183545637283c3, https://angular.dev/api/platform-browser/withEventReplay).

**Affects:** Applications using client hydration. Event replay is still enabled by passing `withEventReplay()` to `provideClientHydration` (or the equivalent hydration feature), which activates the previously-unconditional stashing behavior (https://angular.dev/api/platform-browser/withEventReplay).

**Action:** none for applications that do not use `withEventReplay()`; stashing code is simply dropped from the bundle. Applications that call `withEventReplay()` should re-verify that user events fired before hydration complete are replayed after upgrade, since the enablement now flows through `enableStashEventListenerImpl()`/`setStashFn` rather than the old always-on `stashEventListeners` map (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4fe34f4cfea2aa0e355afa04c7183545637283c3).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Enable event replay via a hydration feature:
bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration(withEventReplay())],
});
```

**What changed:** `ComponentFixture` no longer rethrows errors received from the `NgZone.onError` subscription when the test runs inside a `fakeAsync` zone, because the `fakeAsync` machinery already captures and reports those errors, so rethrowing caused the same error to be thrown twice ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/962b59b14e9dad166973be72d74d1e04d25db5a4)). The subscription now returns early when `typeof Zone === 'undefined' || Zone.current.get('FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec')` is true, so the throw is suppressed inside `fakeAsync`; non-`fakeAsync` zones still rethrow as before. Correspondingly, `FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec.onHandleError` stores the error in `this._lastError` and returns `false` so it is not propagated to the parent zone ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/962b59b14e9dad166973be72d74d1e04d25db5a4)). The change lands in `@angular/core` and the bundled `zone.js` (`packages/zone.js/lib/zone-spec/fake-async-test.ts`), area `core`/`testing` ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60104)).
**Affects:** Unit tests that wrap a `ComponentFixture` (via `TestBed.createComponent` + `detectChanges`/`tick`) in `fakeAsync` and provoke an error inside the Angular zone (e.g. a template event handler or component method that throws, with an `ErrorHandler` that rethrows). Tests that previously expected the error to surface twice are affected; tests expecting it once are now correct.
**Action:** none — this is an internal behavior fix; no code, configuration, or dependency changes are required. Re-verify any `fakeAsync` tests that asserted duplicated error throwing (e.g. extra `toThrow` expectations) and adjust assertions to expect the error only once.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/core/testing/src/component_fixture.ts (next handler of _ngZone.onError)
// OLD
next: (error: any) => {
  throw error;
},

// NEW
next: (error: any) => {
  if (typeof Zone === 'undefined' || Zone.current.get('FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec')) {
    return;
  }
  throw error;
},
```
```typescript
// packages/zone.js/lib/zone-spec/fake-async-test.ts (FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec.onHandleError)
// NEW
this._lastError = error;
return false; // Don't propagate error to parent zone.
```

**What changed:** Uncaught errors in event listeners are now reported to Angular's internal error handling machinery via `handleUncaughtError` instead of being caught and routed directly to the `ErrorHandler` via `handleError` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7b819be83fc3c5ced7a7d14f777cad0691eef709). In tests, this means errors will now cause `fixture.whenStable` to reject or the error to be rethrown by default, rather than only being logged or sent to the `ErrorHandler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60251, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications with tests where event listeners throw uncaught errors, or tests that rely on `ErrorHandler` spies for listener errors without expecting the test framework to rethrow/reject.

**Action:** required: Fix uncaught errors in event listeners. If tests intentionally cover error cases and fail due to rethrown errors, add `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` to `TestBed.configureTestingModule` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7b819be83fc3c5ced7a7d14f777cad0691eef709, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// After (Angular 20): Opt-out if errors should not be rethrown in tests
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  rethrowApplicationErrors: false,
  providers: [{provide: ErrorHandler, useValue: handler}],
});
```

**What changed:** The `any` overload was removed from `Injector.get` in Angular core. Previously, `injector.get` accepted `any` types, allowing looser type checking. Now, it only supports `ProviderToken<T>` and `string` (the latter being deprecated since v4). This change tightens type safety by preventing the use of arbitrary tokens that previously fell under `any` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60202, https://www.gitclear.com/open_repos/angular/angular/release/20.0.0).
**Affects:** Applications calling `injector.get` (including `EnvironmentInjector.get` and `TestBed.inject` where applicable via shared typing mechanisms or direct Injector usage) with tokens that rely on the `any` type or are not strictly `ProviderToken<T>` or `string`.
**Action:** required: Update calls to `injector.get` to use a specific `ProviderToken<T>` instead of relying on `any`. If `string` tokens are used, migrate them to `ProviderToken<T>` as `string` support is deprecated. Re-verify type checking for all dependency injection calls.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: Could use any type (e.g., a loosely defined object or variable typed as any)
import { Injector } from '@angular/core';

class Service {
  constructor(private injector: Injector) {
    const value = this.injector.get(someTokenAny); // someTokenAny typed as any
  }
}

// After: Must use ProviderToken<T> or string
import { Injector, InjectionToken } from '@angular/core';

const MY_TOKEN = new InjectionToken<string>('MY_TOKEN');

class Service {
  constructor(private injector: Injector) {
    const value = this.injector.get(MY_TOKEN); // ProviderToken<string>
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular now guarantees that animations are flushed whenever it runs application synchronization — automatic change detection or manual calls to `ApplicationRef.tick` — "regardless of whether change detection was run on any components attached to `ApplicationRef`." Previously "animations would not be flushed in some situations if change detection did not run on any views attached to the application," and since "DOM removal is delayed until animations are flushed," elements of removed components could stay in the DOM. The listed case is zoneless change detection + `provideAnimations()` with a dynamically created CDK overlay: `overlayRef.detach()` left the overlay content in the DOM unless `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` was called manually; with `ZoneChangeDetection` (`provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true })`) it worked (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58075). The PR is labeled `area: core`, `action: merge`, `target: major`, "detected: breaking change", fixes #58075 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58089). It is listed under v20.0.0 **Breaking Changes → core** (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications using animations (`provideAnimations()`), especially combined with zoneless change detection and dynamically created/detached components (e.g. CDK overlays, `OverlayRef.detach()`); also existing tests that asserted on DOM elements that were not removed before animations flushed.

**Action:** required: re-verify animation-driven add/remove behavior at runtime (in particular detach of dynamically created components under `provideZonelessChangeDetection()`), and re-verify/update unit tests that assert DOM removal timing of animated elements, since those elements are now removed as soon as Angular syncs. No code, provider or build change is described by the release notes for this item.

**Before/after:**

```ts
// issue #58075 reproduction — before v20 the overlay content stayed in the DOM
providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true }), provideAnimations()] // work
// providers: [provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection(), provideAnimations()] // no work

export class ModalContainerComponent {
  private readonly overlayRef = inject(OverlayRef);
  private cdk = inject(ChangeDetectorRef);

  close() {
    this.overlayRef.detach();
    this.cdk.detectChanges(); // manual detectChanges will work
    // without detectChanges no work (pre-v20)
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Resource API's `value` signal now throws an error when the resource is in an error state, instead of returning `undefined` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3ba39bc28f93c208f7b50fcb878fe1aa1bc0413d). Previously, error states were silently swallowed by derived computations (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3ba39bc28f93c208f7b50fcb878fe1aa1bc0413d). The status table now explicitly states `'error'` causes `value()` to throw an error (https://angular.dev/guide/signals/resource). The commit also moves `reload` from `Resource` to `WritableResource` and narrows the resource error type (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60610).
**Affects:** Applications that read the resource's `value()` signal (e.g., `res.value()` or `resource.value()`) inside a `computed`, template binding, or direct function call, while the resource's `error` signal may be populated.
**Action:** required: Guard access to `res.value()` by checking for an error state first using the `error` signal. Re-verify all usages of `resource.value()` and `rxResource.value()` to ensure they explicitly handle the error state using `res.error()`, `res.hasValue()`, or by reading `res.status()`.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
const fooValue = computed(() => {
  return this.fooResource.value()?.items ?? [];
});

// After
const fooValue = computed(() => {
  if (this.fooResource.hasValue()) {
    return this.fooResource.value();
  } else if (this.fooResource.error()) {
    return this.fooResource.error();
  } else if (this.fooResource.isLoading()) {
    return "Loading...";
  }
});
```

**What changed:** The `inject` migration (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`) previously converted constructor parameters decorated with `@Attribute('foo')` into `inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'))` without options. This was behavior-incorrect: `@Attribute` injects `null` when the host attribute is missing, but `inject(new HostAttributeToken(...))` throws a no-provider error. The migration now emits `{ optional: true }` for `@Attribute`-based parameters, matching the decorator's implicit optionality (the migration internally treats `Attribute` like `Optional`, adding `optional` to the inject options object alongside any `skipSelf`/`self`/`host` flags) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60916, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0162ceb427243e065d2cd81042451d705838d090). Additionally, when the migration's `nonNullableOptional` option is enabled, a non-null assertion `!` is now appended to these `HostAttributeToken` declarations, e.g. `inject(new HostAttributeToken('tabindex'), { optional: true })!;` (commit diff test in `packages/core/schematics/test/inject_migration_spec.ts`).

**Affects:** Applications whose code uses constructor parameters decorated with `@Attribute(...)` and that run the `inject` migration; the fix is in the migration tooling itself, not in runtime DI behavior.

**Action:** required: re-run the `inject` migration so `@Attribute` parameters migrate to `inject(new HostAttributeToken('...'), { optional: true })`; re-verify migrated attributes still resolve (or return `null`) at runtime, and re-verify compilation when `nonNullableOptional: true` is used since fields keep a non-nullable type via `!`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (old migration output)
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'));

// After (fixed migration output, from migration spec)
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'), { optional: true });

// With nonNullableOptional: true, input:
//   constructor(@Attribute('tabindex') private foo: string) {}
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('tabindex'), { optional: true })!;
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0162ceb427243e065d2cd81042451d705838d090)
</summary>

**What changed:** The `inject` migration (`ng generate @angular/core:inject`) previously converted constructor parameters decorated with `@Attribute('foo')` into `inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'))` with no options. This was behavior-incorrect: `@Attribute` injects `null` when the host attribute is missing, but `inject(new HostAttributeToken(...))` throws a no-provider error. The migration now emits `{ optional: true }` for `@Attribute`-based parameters, matching the decorator's implicit optionality (internally it sets `hasOptionalDecorator` and adds `optional` to the inject options, alongside any `skipSelf`/`self`/`host` flags) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60916, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0162ceb427243e065d2cd81042451d705838d090). Also, when the migration's `nonNullableOptional` option is enabled, a non-null assertion `!` is now appended to these `HostAttributeToken` declarations (per the commit's spec changes in `packages/core/schematics/`).

**Affects:** Applications using constructor parameters decorated with `@Attribute(...)` that run the `inject` migration; the fix is in the migration tooling, not in runtime DI behavior.

**Action:** required: re-run the `inject` migration so `@Attribute` parameters migrate to `inject(new HostAttributeToken('...'), { optional: true })`; re-verify migrated attributes still resolve at runtime (or return `null` when absent), and re-verify compilation when `nonNullableOptional: true` is used, since fields keep a non-nullable type via the `!` assertion.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (old migration output)
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'));

// After (fixed migration output, from migration spec)
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('foo'), { optional: true });

// Input: constructor(@Attribute('tabindex') private foo: string) {}
// With nonNullableOptional: true:
private foo = inject(new HostAttributeToken('tabindex'), { optional: true })!;
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0162ceb427243e065d2cd81042451d705838d090)

**What changed:** This is an internal bug fix to the Angular core runtime function `setDirectiveInput` (in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/shared.ts`). Previously, the function did not check whether the specified input actually existed on the host directive before attempting to write to it. This caused runtime assertion errors when targeting an input that does not exist. The fix adds a check using `target.inputs.hasOwnProperty(publicName)` before proceeding with the write. This is relevant for applications that use the new v20 API for dynamic component creation (`ViewContainerRef.createComponent` or `createComponent`) with targeted bindings (`inputBinding`, etc.) and attached directives. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ea5eb288651a87923edd86b2445d6ed32e52ed85, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60137)

**Affects:** Applications that dynamically create components using `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` (or `createComponent`) with a `directives` array and targeted `bindings` (e.g., using `inputBinding`) where an input name might not match any existing input on the target directive.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Internal core change: packages/core/src/render3/instructions/shared.ts

// Before:
  if (hostIndex !== null) {
    ngDevMode && assertIndexInRange(lView, hostIndex);
    writeToDirectiveInput(target, lView[hostIndex], publicName, value);
    hasSet = true;
  }

// After:
  if (hostIndex !== null && target.inputs.hasOwnProperty(publicName)) {
    ngDevMode && assertIndexInRange(lView, hostIndex);
    writeToDirectiveInput(target, lView[hostIndex], publicName, value);
    hasSet = true;
  }
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core` `package.json`, `zone.js` moved from a required `peerDependencies` entry to being declared optional. Angular 19.2.x listed `"zone.js": "~0.15.0"` under `peerDependencies` with no `peerDependenciesMeta` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.2.x/packages/core/package.json). Angular 20.0.x keeps `"zone.js": "~0.15.0"` in `peerDependencies` but adds `peerDependenciesMeta: { "zone.js": { "optional": true } }` (also marking `@angular/compiler` optional) (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/packages/core/package.json). PR/commit state: "`zone.js` is no longer mandatory" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61616, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c8951159ac1994ecd98798627333af958aeb56cf).

**Affects:** Applications on Angular 20 that depend on `zone.js` being pulled in or enforced as a mandatory peer dependency of `@angular/core`, or that rely on the version range `~0.15.0` being a hard install-time requirement.

**Action:** none — this is a metadata-only loosening; `zone.js` remains supported for `zone.js`-based change detection. Re-verify that Zone-based change detection still initializes correctly when `zone.js` is present, and confirm the resolved `zone.js` version satisfies `~0.15.0`. No automatic `ng update` migration exists for this change (no entry in `packages/core/schematics/migrations.json` for 20.0.0 covers `zone.js`).

**Before/after:**
```json
// @angular/core package.json (19.2.x)
"peerDependencies": {
  "rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0",
  "zone.js": "~0.15.0"
}
```
```json
// @angular/core package.json (20.0.x)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/compiler": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "rxjs": "^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0",
  "zone.js": "~0.15.0"
},
"peerDependenciesMeta": {
  "@angular/compiler": { "optional": true },
  "zone.js": { "optional": true }
}
```

**What changed:** The `reload` method was removed from the base `Resource` class and moved to the `WritableResource` class, meaning only mutable resources can now be reloaded (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d62379bb13f08e9e0fe9c7b93fe7c6ef46f8f181). The pull request notes this is part of broader "Gated Resource API changes" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61441) and includes fixes to make reading resource values throw errors instead of returning undefined and to narrow error types for the resources API (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60610).

**Affects:** Applications that call `.reload()` on a resource object typed or returned as a non-mutable `Resource` (the base class).

**Action:** required: Update code to invoke `.reload()` only on mutable resource types, specifically `WritableResource`. If using `httpResource` or `rxResource` (which are writable), verify they still expose `.reload()` as expected. If a resource is read-only, you cannot call `.reload()` on it; you may need to change how the resource is created or managed.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19):
const myResource = resource({ ... });
myResource.reload(); // Works because reload was on the base class

// After (Angular 20):
// The return type is WritableResource, so it still works
const myWritableResource = resource({ ... });
myWritableResource.reload(); // Works because it is a WritableResource
```

**What changed:** The `Resource<T>` interface's `error` signal changed type from `Signal<unknown>` to `Signal<Error | undefined>`, and `ResourceStreamItem<T>` changed from `{value: T} | {error: unknown}` to `{value: T} | {error: Error}` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a89f1cff2465cca383765e9f9d7e719970a3a6e4.patch). Errors thrown by a `loader`/`stream`, by `rxResource` streams, or by `httpResource`'s `parse` function that are not `Error` instances are now wrapped by an internal `ResourceWrappedError extends Error` (message in dev mode: `Resource returned an error that's not an Error instance: <value>. Check this error's .cause for the actual error.`) with the original value in `error.cause`; `Error` instances pass through unchanged. PR #61441 was "Gated Resource API changes" — a copy of #60610 "with a flag to allow the behavior to be patched out" (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61441, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60610).

**Affects:** Applications using `resource()`, `rxResource()`, or `httpResource()` that either read `resource.error()` under `strict` typing (e.g. comparing it to a string, or accessing custom properties) or that deliberately surface non-`Error` throwables (strings, objects) from loaders/streams/parsers.

**Action:** required: update code that assumed `unknown` — `resource.error()` is now `Error | undefined`, so read the original payload via `error().cause` when loaders throw non-`Error` values, and stream loaders must emit `{error: new Error(...)}` rather than `{error: <non-Error>}`. Re-verify error-display logic, since the visible error is now the wrapper `Error` with the original in `.cause`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// ResourceStreamItem / Resource.error (goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md)
- readonly error: Signal<unknown>;
+ readonly error: Signal<Error | undefined>;

- export type ResourceStreamItem<T> = {value: T} | {error: unknown};
+ export type ResourceStreamItem<T> = {value: T} | {error: Error};

// test: non-Error thrown value is now wrapped
- expect(echoResource.error()).toBe('Something went wrong....');
+ expect(echoResource.error()).toEqual(
+   jasmine.objectContaining({cause: 'Something went wrong....'}));
+ expect(echoResource.error()!.message).toContain('Resource');
```

**What changed:** The internal hydration "stash" event listener used by `withEventReplay()` was a single module-level function in `@angular/core` (`packages/core/src/render3/view/listeners.ts`) that each bootstrapped application overwrote via `setStashFn(fn)`. When multiple microfrontend / simultaneously bootstrapped apps (e.g. one server-rendered, one client-only) used event replay, the second app's `setStashFn` call unsafely replaced the first app's listener, breaking event replay for the first app. Angular 20 replaced the single function with a `Map<string, StashEventListener>` keyed by `APP_ID`: `setStashFn(appId, fn)` and a new `clearStashFn(appId)` are now used in `withEventReplay()` (`packages/core/src/hydration/event_replay.ts`), and `listenToDomEvent` looks up the listener with `lView[INJECTOR].get(APP_ID)`. On app destroy, cleanup now calls `clearStashFn(appId)` instead of `setStashFn(() => {})` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/624be2ef0c7255e62082751cc339d2cd618bd633, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/59635).

**Affects:** Applications using `provideClientHydration(withEventReplay())` where more than one Angular application is bootstrapped on the same page (microfrontends, or mixed SSR-hydrated and client-only apps). All other applications are unaffected — these are private internal functions, not public API.

**Action:** none — no application code, configuration, or dependency changes are required; upgrading apps that bootstrap multiple apps with event replay automatically get correct per-`APP_ID` listener isolation. Distinct `APP_ID` values (e.g. `{provide: APP_ID, useValue: 'random_name'}`) still apply per app as before.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19) — single global stash fn, overridden per app
let stashEventListener = (el: RNode, eventName: string, listenerFn: EventCallback) => {};
export function setStashFn(fn: typeof stashEventListener) {
  stashEventListener = fn;
}

// After (Angular 20) — per-APP_ID map
const stashEventListeners = new Map<string, StashEventListener>();
export function setStashFn(appId: string, fn: StashEventListener) {
  stashEventListeners.set(appId, fn);
}
export function clearStashFn(appId: string) {
  stashEventListeners.delete(appId);
}
```

The sources are the commit/PR/issue and the PR diff, which I fetched. The fix: `triggerResourceLoading` in `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts` previously registered a pending task via `injector.get(PendingTasksInternal)` and removed it when loading completed; the bug (issue #61038) was the app was marked stable prematurely — SSR/prerendered HTML for components inside `@defer(hydrate never)` (and zoneless change detection) was incomplete because the defer loading task was removed before the loading promise fully settled, and the task wasn't tracked properly for stabilization. The fix switches to `injector.get(PendingTasks).add()` which returns a `removeTask` function, and removes the task inside `tDetails.loadingPromise.finally(...)`. This is a core framework internal fix; applications need no code change, but apps using SSR + zoneless + `@defer` should re-verify their prerendered/SSR output and that `ApplicationRef.isStable`/app-stable-based logic (prerendering, tests waiting for stability) still works. Note the test components in the integration suite added `markForCheck()` but that's test code, not a migration requirement.

**What changed:** In Angular core, `triggerResourceLoading` (in `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts`) now tracks defer-block resource loading as a pending task so the application is not marked stable prematurely. Previously it used `injector.get(PendingTasksInternal)` and called `pendingTasks.remove(taskId)` when loading finished, which caused the app to be considered stable too early; now it uses `injector.get(PendingTasks).add()` returning a `removeTask` function that is invoked in `tDetails.loadingPromise.finally(...)` (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61040.diff). The symptom (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/61038) was that with `zoneless` change detection and `@defer` (e.g. `@defer(hydrate never)`), SSR/prerendered HTML was incomplete and input data failed to resolve on first render; a regression introduced in 19.0.5 and fixed in 20.0.0-next.4.

**Affects:** Applications using SSR/incremental hydration with `@defer` blocks (especially with zoneless change detection), or that rely on application-stability signals (`ApplicationRef` stability) around defer-block loading.

**Action:** required: re-verify SSR/prerendered output for `@defer` blocks (including `@defer(hydrate never)`) and any code/tests that wait for app stability (e.g. `ApplicationRef.isStable`) now that the app stays unstable until defer-block resource loading completes. No dependency or configuration change is documented in the sources.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts)
const pendingTasks = injector.get(PendingTasksInternal);
const taskId = pendingTasks.add();
// ...on completion...
tDetails.loadingPromise = null;
pendingTasks.remove(taskId);

// after
const removeTask = injector.get(PendingTasks).add();
// ...
return tDetails.loadingPromise.finally(() => {
  tDetails.loadingPromise = null;
  removeTask();
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular 19, reading `Resource.value()` while the resource was in the `error` state returned the `defaultValue` (or `undefined`); in Angular 20 `value()` throws a `ResourceValueError` (message `Resource is currently in an error state (see Error.cause for details): <message>`, with `cause` set to the resource error) — this is gated by the internal flag `RESOURCE_VALUE_THROWS_ERRORS_DEFAULT = true` in `packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts` (PR #60610 / #61441). Commit `6e79eaf739` (#61441) then fixed a corner case: when a resource in the `error` state is reloaded via `WritableResource.reload()` and is in the `reloading`/`loading` internal status while `error()` is still set, `value()` now returns the `defaultValue` instead of throwing, which prevents `Resource.hasValue()` from throwing (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/6e79eaf7399170611bcbefda0082947b629f2693.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61441). New `reloading` behaviour: `status()` is `'reloading'`, `isLoading()` is true, `hasValue()` is true, `value()` equals the `defaultValue`, and `error()` still returns the previous error, until the reload resolves (per the added spec in the patch).

**Affects:** Applications using the `@experimental` `resource()` / `rxResource()` APIs that read `Resource.value()` (directly, or through `computed`s/`hasValue()`) while the resource may be in the `error` state, including after calling `reload()`.

**Action:** required: guard reads of `value()` with `hasValue()` (or check `error()` first) in components, templates and `computed`s, since reading `value()` in the error state now throws `ResourceValueError`; re-verify UI behaviour during `reload()` from an error state — `hasValue()` now returns `true` and `value()` yields `defaultValue` while `error()` remains set, so error banners driven only by `error()` may coexist with a default-valued render.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// after (Angular 20) — from the spec added in commit 6e79eaf739
echoResource.reload();
TestBed.tick();

expect(echoResource.status()).toBe('reloading');
expect(echoResource.isLoading()).toBeTrue();
expect(echoResource.hasValue()).toBeTrue();
expect(echoResource.value()).toEqual('my-default-value');
expect(echoResource.error()).toEqual(new Error('Something went wrong....'));

// in the error state itself, value() now throws:
// ResourceValueError: "Resource is currently in an error state (see Error.cause for details): ..."
```

**What changed:** Tsurge's execution host inside angular-devkit was reworked so TypeScript programs are no longer created up front for every tsconfig and retained for the whole run. In `runMigrationInDevkit` (packages/core/schematics/utils/tsurge/helpers/angular_devkit/run_in_devkit.ts) the analysis phase now creates, analyzes and releases one program per tsconfig, and for non-funnel (`TsurgeFunnelMigration`) "complex" migrations the program is re-created in a second loop before `migration.migrate(globalMeta, info)`; funnel migrations reuse their analysis results and are a pure performance win. A new exported `enum MigrationStage { Analysis, Migrate }` was added and the callback signatures became `beforeProgramCreation?: (tsconfigPath: string, stage: MigrationStage) => void` and `afterProgramCreation?: (info: ProgramInfo, fileSystem: FileSystem, stage: MigrationStage) => void` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3d85d9363c6dd02d2f14181bdad1dc0b05fedc31.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60774). This fixes "FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory" when running `ng generate @angular/core:cleanup-unused-imports` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59813). Affected schematics: `cleanup-unused-imports`, `output-migration`, `self-closing-tags-migration`, `signal-input-migration`, `signal-queries-migration`; log output now prints `Preparing analysis for: <tsconfig>...` versus `Running migration for: <tsconfig>...`.
**Affects:** applications that run the Tsurge-based `ng generate` migration schematics listed above (e.g. `ng generate @angular/core:cleanup-unused-imports`), especially large multi-tsconfig workspaces that previously hit Node heap limits; no application source code, build or runtime behaviour is affected.
**Action:** none — this is internal memory/performance behaviour of the migration tooling. Users who previously had to raise the heap (e.g. `NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size`) to run these schematics can re-verify that they now complete; "complex" (non-funnel) migrations may take somewhat longer to execute (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60774).
**Before/after:**
```diff
-  beforeProgramCreation?: (tsconfigPath: string) => void;
+  beforeProgramCreation?: (tsconfigPath: string, stage: MigrationStage) => void;

-  afterProgramCreation?: (info: ProgramInfo, fileSystem: FileSystem) => void;
+  afterProgramCreation?: (info: ProgramInfo, fileSystem: FileSystem, stage: MigrationStage) => void;

-  const programInfos = [...buildPaths, ...testPaths].map((tsconfigPath) => {
-    config.beforeProgramCreation?.(tsconfigPath);
+  const isFunnelMigration = migration instanceof TsurgeFunnelMigration;
+  for (const tsconfigPath of tsconfigPaths) {
+    config.beforeProgramCreation?.(tsconfigPath, MigrationStage.Analysis);
     const baseInfo = migration.createProgram(tsconfigPath, fs);
```

**What changed:** The `forceRoot?: true` property has been removed from the `CreateEffectOptions` interface passed to the `effect()` function (@angular/core). Previously, passing `{forceRoot: true}` forced an effect to be created as a root effect (scheduled as a microtask) even when created within a component. In Angular 20, all effects created within a component, directive, or service context are strictly component effects, and the `forceRoot` option no longer exists.

**Affects:** Applications or libraries that explicitly pass `forceRoot: true` in the options object when calling `effect()`.

**Action:** required: Remove `forceRoot` from the options object passed to `effect()` in code. If an application relies on `forceRoot: true` to force root-effect semantics (microtask scheduling) while creating the effect inside a component, it must move the `effect()` creation outside of the component's context to achieve root-effect behavior, or accept that it will now behave as a component effect tied to change detection.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19)
import { effect, inject } from '@angular/core';

class MyComponent {
  constructor() {
    const counter = inject(someSignal);
    effect(() => console.log(counter()), { forceRoot: true });
  }
}

// After (Angular 20)
import { effect, inject } from '@angular/core';

class MyComponent {
  constructor() {
    const counter = inject(someSignal);
    // forceRoot is removed; effect is a component effect
    effect(() => console.log(counter()));
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The `rejectErrors` boolean property was removed from the `ToSignalOptions<T>` interface used by `toSignal` in `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`. Previously, setting `rejectErrors: true` caused `toSignal` to throw errors back to RxJS, resulting in an uncaught exception while the signal retained its last good value. Now, errors from the observable always set the signal state to `StateKind.Error` internally, rather than throwing to RxJS (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/48974c3cf88ab1a70411bea4950823f975994087.patch).

**Affects:** Applications that passed `{rejectErrors: true}` in the options object to `toSignal`.

**Action:** required: Remove `rejectErrors: true` from `toSignal` options. If the previous uncaught exception behavior was required for error propagation, use RxJS operators on the source observable such as `catchError(() => EMPTY)` to ignore errors or handle them explicitly before calling `toSignal` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/48974c3cf88ab1a70411bea4950823f975994087.patch).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19)
const signal = toSignal(myObservable$, { rejectErrors: true });

// After (Angular 20)
// rejectErrors is no longer a valid option.
// Errors are captured in the signal state.
// To ignore errors (similar to async pipe swallowing them silently or handling them), use RxJS operators:
import { catchError, EMPTY } from 'rxjs';
const signal = toSignal(myObservable$.pipe(catchError(() => EMPTY)));
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 removes the direct `ErrorHandler.handleError` call from `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges`. Previously `detectChangesInternal` (in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/change_detection.ts`) wrapped the traversal in `try { detectChangesInViewWhileDirty(...) } catch (error) { if (notifyErrorHandler) { handleError(lView, error); } throw error; }`, so an error thrown during `CDR.detectChanges` was both reported to the `ErrorHandler` and rethrown — often reported a second time when caught further up the stack. Now the internal functions drop the `notifyErrorHandler` parameter entirely: `detectChangesInternal(lView, mode)`, `checkNoChangesInternal(lView, mode)`, `detectChangesInViewIfRequired(lView, isFirstPass, zonelessEnabled)`, and the `notifyErrorHandler` constructor argument/field is removed from `ViewRef` and from `ApplicationRef.allViews` entries (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60056.diff). Errors thrown by `detectChanges` are now only rethrown. Per the PR description: "for zone-based applications, this will likely have no effect other than removing duplicate reporting of the error. If the rethrown error is not already being caught, it will reach the NgZone's error trap and still be reported to the application `ErrorHandler`." (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60056, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/491b0a4ead98822c767543e1f1c8046ed9d1be20). Listed as a `fix` (behavioral change) in the 20.0.0 release notes ().

**Affects:** Applications that call `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` (directly or via `ComponentRef`/`ViewRef`) where an error can be thrown during change detection, and code that relied on `ErrorHandler.handleError` being invoked even when the rethrown error was subsequently swallowed by a surrounding `try`/`catch`. Notably impactful in zoneless applications (no NgZone error trap to catch the rethrown error) and in unit tests that call `detectChanges` and assert on `ErrorHandler` invocations. Zone-based apps that don't catch the error see only the duplicate report removed.

**Action:** required: re-verify error-handling behavior around manual `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()` calls. If an application wraps `detectChanges()` in a `try`/`catch` and previously relied on the automatic `ErrorHandler` report, it must now call the error handler itself in the `catch` block (or let the error propagate to `NgZone`'s error trap). Update tests that counted duplicate `ErrorHandler.handleError` calls from a single `detectChanges` failure.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19): detectChangesInternal reported AND rethrew
try {
  detectChangesInViewWhileDirty(lView, mode);
} catch (error) {
  if (notifyErrorHandler) {
    handleError(lView, error);
  }
  throw error;
} finally { ... }

// After (Angular 20): only rethrows
try {
  detectChangesInViewWhileDirty(lView, mode);
} finally { ... }
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60056.diff)

**What changed:** The internal `ɵɵlistener` instruction (exported from `@angular/core` as `ɵɵlistener`, defined in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/listener.ts`) lost its third parameter `useCapture?: boolean` in v20. The signature is now `ɵɵlistener(eventName: string, listenerFn: (e?: any) => any, eventTargetResolver?: GlobalTargetResolver)` — the event-target resolver (e.g. `i0.ɵɵresolveDocument`, `i0.ɵɵresolveWindow`, `i0.ɵɵresolveBody`) moved from the 4th to the 3rd argument position. The template pipeline in `packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/instruction.ts` no longer emits `false` for `useCapture` before the target resolver. Generated host-binding code changed accordingly, e.g. `i0.ɵɵlistener("dragover", function MyComponent_dragover_HostBindingHandler($event) { return ctx.foo($event); }, i0.ɵɵresolveDocument);` instead of `..., false, i0.ɵɵresolveDocument)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/04d963c0a5f68c93015c13973d29107fe35bb942.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60406). The change is marked `action: merge, area: core, target: major` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60406).

**Affects:** Only code that calls the private `ɵɵlistener` instruction directly with a `useCapture` argument (hand-written/patched `ɵɵ` output, e.g. `hostBindings` functions), or tooling that parses generated `ɵɵlistener` calls. Normal application templates and `@HostListener` decorators are unaffected — the compiler emits the new form automatically when recompiled.

**Action:** none — recompiled applications need no changes; only direct uses of `ɵɵlistener` passing a boolean third argument must drop that argument.

**Before/after:**
```js
// v19 generated host binding (before)
i0.ɵɵlistener("dragover", function MyComponent_dragover_HostBindingHandler($event) {
  return ctx.foo($event);
}, false, i0.ɵɵresolveDocument);

// v20 generated host binding (after)
i0.ɵɵlistener("dragover", function MyComponent_dragover_HostBindingHandler($event) {
  return ctx.foo($event);
}, i0.ɵɵresolveDocument);
```

**What changed:** `ApplicationRef.prototype.bootstrap` now executes its entire body inside `ngZone.run(...)`, whereas previously it ran outside `NgZone`. Before, callers had to wrap the call manually (`ngZone.run(() => appRef.bootstrap(SomeComp))`) or the bootstrapped component would not be tracked by the zone and would fail to run change detection correctly. `bootstrapApplication` already created the root component inside `NgZone`, so this change aligns `ApplicationRef.prototype.bootstrap` with that behavior, ensuring all bootstrapped components run CD as expected. The implementation now retrieves `NgZone` from `this._injector.get(NgZone)` and wraps the bootstrap logic in `ngZone.run(() => { ... return compRef; })` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60720, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0ae18895605eef6b4946898ff0752ae3917c0057, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0ae18895605eef6b4946898ff0752ae3917c0057.patch)
**Affects:** Applications that manually bootstrap components via `ApplicationRef.prototype.bootstrap` while wrapped in `ngZone.run(...)` to compensate for the old behavior, or applications using a custom `NgZone` (such as a noop zone for zoneless change detection) where they previously relied on bootstrap running outside the zone.
**Action:** required: Remove redundant `ngZone.run(...)` wrappers around calls to `ApplicationRef.prototype.bootstrap`, since the method now runs within `NgZone` internally. Applications using `provideZonelessChangeDetection()` must verify that `ApplicationRef.prototype.bootstrap` correctly uses the noop `NgZone` provided in the root `Injector` to avoid unexpected scheduling behavior.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (workaround required for CD):
ngZone.run(() => appRef.bootstrap(SomeComp));

// After (now handles NgZone internally):
appRef.bootstrap(SomeComp);
```

**What changed:** Previously, the order in which root effects were executed was non-deterministic and relied on the order in which signal graph dirty notifications were propagated. With this commit, root effects are always run in creation order (#60534, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a611b234d7405f3a06389d66860a139cd9202c60). Root effects (effect() created with an explicit injector, e.g. ApplicationRef.injector or EnvironmentInjector) are now flushed in the order they were created rather than in dirty-notification order. The new test asserts that if effect A is created before effect B but signal B's source is set before signal A's source, the log is still ['A: 2', 'B: 1'] (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a611b234d7405f3a06389d66860a139cd9202c60).

**Affects:** Applications with two or more root effects (effect() with {injector: ...}) whose callbacks have side effects that depend on relative execution order, and tests that assert effect execution order.

**Action:** required: re-verify the execution order of all root effects; either rely on the new creation-order guarantee or refactor to remove order dependencies between independent root effects.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Creation order: A, B
effect(() => log.push(`A: ${sourceA()}`), {injector: appRef.injector});
effect(() => log.push(`B: ${sourceB()}`), {injector: appRef.injector});

// Dirty order: B, A
sourceB.set(1);
sourceA.set(2);
// New behavior: effects run in creation order
expect(log).toEqual(['A: 2', 'B: 1']);
```

**What changed:** `TestBed.tick()` in `@angular/core/testing` was changed so that it synchronizes **all** fixtures created with `TestBed.createComponent`, not only those with auto-detect enabled. Previously `tick()` was just `this.inject(ApplicationRef).tick()` and only fixture host views registered in `ApplicationRef.externalTestViews` (i.e. `autoDetect: true` fixtures, plus zoneless special-casing inside `ComponentFixture.detectChanges`) participated in that run (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61382.diff). The fix replaces `ApplicationRef.externalTestViews` with internal `allTestViews` / `autoDetectTestViews` sets plus an `includeAllTestViews` flag; `TestBedImpl.tick()` sets `includeAllTestViews = true` around `appRef.tick()`, so fixtures with `autoDetect: false` are now checked too. Automatic (zone/zoneless) scheduling calls the internal `ApplicationRef._tick()`, which does *not* include `autoDetect: false` fixtures (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61382.diff). `TestBed.tick()` is newly promoted to `@publicApi 20.0` and the deprecated `TestBed.flushEffects()` now simply delegates to `tick()` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/testing/src/test_bed.ts). New tests show a fixture created without auto-detect previously had `nativeElement.textContent` `''` after `TestBed.tick()`, and now shows the updated signal value (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61382.diff).

**Affects:** Tests that call `TestBed.tick()` (or deprecated `TestBed.flushEffects()`) while a `TestBed.createComponent` fixture has auto-detect off (the default, or after `fixture.autoDetectChanges(false)`), and that assert on DOM state before/after the tick. Both zone and zoneless (`provideZonelessChangeDetection()`) tests.

**Action:** required: re-verify existing specs using `TestBed.tick()` / `TestBed.flushEffects()` — DOM from non-auto-detect fixtures is now updated by the tick, so assertions that expected stale (never-rendered) output, or that relied on a following `fixture.detectChanges()` to be the first render, may change outcome. No code, dependency or configuration change is mandated.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (v19): tick() did not update a fixture without autoDetect
const {nativeElement, componentInstance} = TestBed.createComponent(Thing1);
expect(nativeElement.textContent).toBe(''); // change detection didn't run yet
componentInstance.state.set(2);
TestBed.tick();
expect(nativeElement.textContent).toBe(''); // needed fixture.detectChanges()

// after (v20): TestBed.tick() synchronizes all test components
componentInstance.state.set(2);
TestBed.tick();
expect(nativeElement.textContent).toBe('2');
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core/testing`, the internal `resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists()` helper in `packages/core/testing/src/fake_async.ts` previously called `fakeAsyncTestModule.resetFakeAsyncZone()` whenever the zone.js `fakeAsyncTest` symbol was present, and threw / misbehaved when ZoneJS was loaded but had not patched the current test framework's APIs (there is no patching of Vitest APIs, so loading Zone on a Vitest page threw) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61628, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3d4ddd224766401982b6dd4b30d88eba3d99f029). The condition is now gated on `fakeAsyncTestModule && (Zone as any)['ProxyZoneSpec']?.isLoaded()` — if `ProxyZoneSpec` from `zone-testing` is not loaded, the reset is silently skipped instead of throwing (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.0/packages/core/testing/src/fake_async.ts). The public `fakeAsync()`, `tick()`, `flush()`, `flushMicrotasks()`, `discardPeriodicTasks()` still throw `zone-testing.js is needed for the fakeAsync() test helper but could not be found. Please make sure that your environment includes zone.js/testing` when the fakeAsync test module is missing.

**Affects:** Applications using ZoneJS (`zone.js`) together with a test framework whose APIs Zone does not patch (e.g. Vitest), where `TestBed` reset previously threw; also internal test-setup code calling `resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists()`.

**Action:** none — no code or configuration change is required; this only removes a spurious error. Applications that call `fakeAsync()`/`tick()` under such a framework still need a Zone-patched test environment (or should use zoneless testing), and that pre-existing failure mode is unchanged; re-verify that `fakeAsync`-based specs still work.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/testing/src/fake_async.ts — resetFakeAsyncZoneIfExists()
-  if (fakeAsyncTestModule) {
+  if (fakeAsyncTestModule && (Zone as any)['ProxyZoneSpec']?.isLoaded()) {
     fakeAsyncTestModule.resetFakeAsyncZone();
   }
```

**What changed:** The minimum supported Node.js version for Angular 20.0.0 was raised to `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0`, up from `^20.11.1 || ^22.11.0 || >=24.0.0` (which was the requirement for Angular 19.0.x, listed as `^18.19.1 || ^20.11.1 || ^22.0.0`). The `engines.node` field in every published `@angular/*` package (`@angular/core`, `@angular/compiler`, `@angular/compiler-cli`, `@angular/common`, `@angular/router`, `@angular/forms`, `@angular/animations`, `@angular/platform-browser`, `@angular/platform-server`, `@angular/upgrade`, etc.) was updated to the new range (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/30e081287da68d6801f8ebd5a00d495b51d4c68d). The corresponding `@angular/cli` engine range was changed in tandem so that Angular and the CLI accept the same Node versions (https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/pull/30349). The Angular CLI PR explains these floors were chosen because `require(esm)` is enabled by default at those versions and because upstream dependencies such as Undici and Pacote now require them (https://github.com/angular/angular-cli/pull/30349).
**Affects:** all applications upgrading to Angular 20.0.0 whose development or CI machine runs a Node.js version below the new floors — specifically Node.js 20.11.1–20.18.x (below 20.19.0), 22.11.x (below 22.12.0), or any Node.js below 20.19.0 (including Node.js 18.x, which is no longer a floor for Angular 20 at all).
**Action:** required: install a Node.js release satisfying `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0`, or pin it with an `engines.node` / `.nvmrc` constraint; re-verify the build, `ng` CLI, and `@angular/compiler-cli` (ngc) run correctly under the upgraded Node.js (no source-code changes are required by this change itself).
**Before/after:**
```json
// Angular 19.x @angular/core package.json  ->  Angular 20.0.0 @angular/core package.json
{ "engines": { "node": "^20.11.1 || ^22.11.0 || >=24.0.0" } }
{ "engines": { "node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0" } }
```

**What changed:** The structural directives `ngIf`, `ngFor`, and `ngSwitch` (and their helpers `ngSwitchCase`, `ngSwitchDefault`) are now deprecated. JSDoc `@deprecated` tags and `@public @deprecated` markers were added to `NgIf`, `NgForOf` (exported as `NgFor`), `NgForOfContext`, `NgSwitch`, `NgSwitchCase`, and `NgSwitchDefault`, with intent to remove them in v22 (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b407157ee848296cd264891b1d7cc9c57c719418.diff). The control-flow schematic was moved from a `ng generate` collection to the `ng update` migration set so it runs automatically during `ng update` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60492). This deprecates only these directives, not structural directives in general.

**Affects:** Applications that use `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, or `[ngSwitch]` / `[ngSwitchCase]` / `[ngSwitchDefault]` in templates, or that import `NgIf`, `NgFor`/`NgForOf`, `NgSwitch`, `NgSwitchCase`, `NgSwitchDefault` from `@angular/common`.

**Action:** required: Run the control-flow migration during `ng update` (or later via `ng g @angular/core:control-flow-migration`) to convert `*ngIf`/`*ngFor`/`[ngSwitch]` templates to `@if`/`@for`/`@switch` blocks; re-verify that rendering behavior matches after migration.

**Before/after:**
```html
<div *ngIf="condition">Content</div>

<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let item of items">{{ item }}</li>
</ul>

<div [ngSwitch]="expression">
  <p *ngSwitchCase="1">one</p>
  <p *ngSwitchDefault>other</p>
</div>
```
```html
@if (condition) { <div>Content</div> }

<ul>
  @for (item of items; track item) {
    <li>{{ item }}</li>
  }
</ul>

@switch (expression) {
  @case (1) { <p>one</p> }
  @default { <p>other</p> }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular v20 deprecates `ng-reflect-*` attributes and stops producing them by default in dev mode (they were never produced in production). To retain the old behavior, Angular adds a new `provideNgReflectAttributes()` function importable from `@angular/core`, which re-enables the logic that produces `ng-reflect-*` attributes on elements with bindings, in dev mode only (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c2987d8402b7b03333b0081a7f97750cdf612e99, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60973, https://angular.dev/api/core/provideNgReflectAttributes)

**Affects:** Applications or tests that read/select DOM elements by `ng-reflect-*` attributes (e.g., `ng-reflect-*` presence checks in specs or selectors).

**Action:** required: refactor application and test code to avoid relying on `ng-reflect-*` attributes, or temporarily restore the previous dev-mode behavior by adding the `provideNgReflectAttributes()` function to the list of providers within the bootstrap call.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// main.ts
import {provideNgReflectAttributes} from '@angular/core';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideNgReflectAttributes(),
  ],
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds a new `markAllAsDirty()` method to `AbstractControl` (the base class of `FormControl`, `FormGroup`, and `FormArray`) in `@angular/forms`. It "Marks the control and all its descendant controls as dirty", duplicating the behaviour and tests of the existing `markAllAsTouched()` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58663, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a07ee60989441c38e6539fd25cad5166622e9f9e, fixing issue #55990). Per the API docs, it accepts a configuration object with an `emitEvent` option: "When true or not supplied (the default), the events observable emits a `PristineChangeEvent` with the `pristine` property being `false`. When false, no events are emitted." (https://angular.dev/api/forms/AbstractControl). The Angular 20 reactive-forms guide documents it alongside `markAllAsTouched` (). The PR was marked `action: merge, area: forms, target: major` and is not a breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/58663).

**Affects:** All applications; purely additive — existing applications are not affected unless they previously defined their own helper named `markAllAsDirty` on a custom control subclass, in which case the inherited method could be shadowed/overridden.

**Action:** none (optional: replace hand-rolled "mark control and children dirty" helpers with the built-in `markAllAsDirty()`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular 20 reactive forms guide — new usage:
// Mark imported data as dirty
loadData(data: FormData) {
  this.form.patchValue(data);
  this.form.markAllAsDirty();
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds an optional second parameter to `FormGroupDirective.resetForm` in `@angular/forms`, so the directive's reset can be silenced like `FormGroup.reset` already could (fixes #60274, "Expose an API to reset the submitted state of a form"). New signature: `resetForm(value: any = undefined, options: {onlySelf?: boolean; emitEvent?: boolean} = {})`, which now calls `this.form.reset(value, options)` (previously `this.form.reset(value)`), sets `this._submittedReactive.set(false)`, and only emits `FormResetEvent` when `options?.emitEvent !== false` (https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/bdfbd5493240869e9a25fa10a0f6c21510e12492/packages/forms/src/directives/reactive_directives/form_group_directive.ts, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60354, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/bdfbd5493240869e9a25fa10a0f6c21510e12492). `NgForm.resetForm(value: any = undefined)` was not changed, so it still takes no options (same commit, `packages/forms/src/directives/ng_form.ts`). Per `AbstractControl`/`FormGroup` docs, `emitEvent: false` suppresses `statusChanges`/`valueChanges` emissions on reset ().

**Affects:** reactive-form code calling `formGroupDirective.resetForm(...)` (e.g. via `#f="ngForm"` / `@ViewChild(FormGroupDirective)`) that relied on `resetForm` always propagating reset events/values to the bound `FormGroup`; also code subclassing `FormGroupDirective` and overriding `resetForm`.

**Action:** none — purely additive optional parameter; existing one-argument calls behave as before. Optionally pass `{emitEvent: false}` to suppress `valueChanges`/`statusChanges` and `FormResetEvent` emissions during programmatic resets.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19)
resetForm(value: any = undefined): void {
  this.form.reset(value);
  this._submittedReactive.set(false);
  this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form));
}

// After (Angular 20)
resetForm(value: any = undefined, options: {onlySelf?: boolean; emitEvent?: boolean} = {}): void {
  this.form.reset(value, options);
  this._submittedReactive.set(false);
  if (options?.emitEvent !== false) {
    this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form));
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/forms`, `AbstractControl` tracked pending async validations in the internal `_hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator: null | {emitEvent: boolean}` field. When several updates requested with `emitEvent: false` raced with async validators, the internal `shouldHaveEmitted` flag was not propagated by `_runAsyncValidator(shouldHaveEmitted, emitEvent?)`, so the requested status events were dropped ("trashed") and neither `statusChanges` nor `events` (`StatusChangeEvent`) emitted, even though at least one update should have caused an emit (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/57098, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/81fe0536fdb86ba2428954ac5305c2424f369339). The fix stores `shouldHaveEmitted` alongside `emitEvent` in `_hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator` and, when cancelling an async validator subscription, keeps the emit if either flag was set, so `statusChanges` is emitted again (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/57098.diff). Reported scenario: an async validator with a delay plus `patchValue`/`setValue` called from an `@Input` setter with a `formControlName` directive, where Angular internally calls `updateValueAndValidity`/`_runAsyncValidator` multiple times (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/56999). Emissions stay suppressed when *none* of the updates requested an emit (per the added `form_group_spec.ts` test).
**Affects:** Reactive-form applications using async validators together with value updates (`setValue`, `patchValue`, `updateValueAndValidity`) passing `emitEvent: false`, especially repeated/overlapping updates, and code subscribing to `statusChanges` or the `events` observable.
**Action:** none — no code, config or dependency change is required; re-verify that status-change subscriptions still behave correctly, since previously dropped `statusChanges`/`StatusChangeEvent` emissions may now arrive (extra `PENDING`/`VALID` notifications in the racing scenarios above).
**Before/after:**
```diff
-  _hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator: null | {emitEvent: boolean} = null;
+  _hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator: null | {emitEvent: boolean; shouldHaveEmitted: boolean} = null;

-      this._hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator = {emitEvent: emitEvent !== false};
+      this._hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator = {
+        emitEvent: emitEvent !== false,
+        shouldHaveEmitted: shouldHaveEmitted !== false,
+      };

-      const shouldHaveEmitted = this._hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator?.emitEvent ?? false;
+      const shouldHaveEmitted =
+        (this._hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator?.emitEvent ||
+          this._hasOwnPendingAsyncValidator?.shouldHaveEmitted) ??
+        false;
```
(https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/57098.diff)

**What changed:** Angular 20.0.0 adds support for the Fetch API's `keepalive` option in `HttpClient` requests when using the fetch backend (`withFetch()`). The `keepalive` property was added to the `HttpRequestInit` interface, and `FetchBackend` was modified to pass this option to the native fetch API (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ccc5cc068f788013c19498c44d13530c1bc98912>). When set to `true`, the browser will not abort the associated request if the page that initiated it is unloaded before the request is complete, which is useful for analytics or logging during page unload (<https://angular.dev/guide/http/making-requests>).

**Affects:** Applications using `HttpClient` with the fetch backend (`withFetch()`) that require requests to complete even if the page is unloaded (e.g., analytics). Applications using the XHR backend or not requiring keepalive behavior are not affected in a way that requires action.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Angular 20+ usage (requires withFetch() in provideHttpClient)
// Simple GET with keepalive
http.get('/api/data', { keepalive: true }).subscribe();

// POST request with keepalive
http.post('/api/log', analyticsData, {
  keepalive: true,
  headers: new HttpHeaders({'Content-Type': 'application/json'})
}).subscribe();
```

**What changed:** `HttpClient` internals in `@angular/common/http` switched from the private `ɵPendingTasksInternal` token to the public `PendingTasks` API from `@angular/core` (this API was also stabilized in v20 via "Stabilize PendingTasks Injectable" #60716). In `packages/common/http/src/interceptor.ts`, both `legacyInterceptorFnFactory` and `HttpInterceptorHandler` changed from `const taskId = pendingTasks.add(); ... finalize(() => pendingTasks.remove(taskId))` to `const removeTask = pendingTasks.add(); ... finalize(removeTask)`. As a result, when an HTTP request completes, the app no longer becomes stable synchronously; stability is now delayed until the next `ApplicationRef.tick`, so follow-up work chained off the response (e.g. RxJS operators after the response value) runs before the app reports stable. This fixed zoneless SSR hangs where `forkJoin` of multiple `HttpClient` requests produced an empty SSR `app-root` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/59352). Updated tests show the old assertion `await Promise.resolve(); expect(stable).toBe(true);` replaced by `await expectAsync(appRef.whenStable()).toBeResolved();` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5795e03cdf25f4a96b73a49be915efbeeb398d83, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60656). A related test guards against a circular dependency: `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER -> ErrorHandler -> HttpClient -> PendingTasks -> ChangeDetectionScheduler` (same commit).

**Affects:** Applications using `HttpClient` (via `provideHttpClient()`) whose code depends on the precise timing of `ApplicationRef.isStable` / `isStable.subscribe` immediately after an HTTP response settles, such as SSR stability checks or tests asserting stability synchronously after a microtask; also apps injecting `HttpClient` into an `ErrorHandler`.

**Action:** none for application code — no API, property or dependency change is required; re-verify timing-sensitive logic around `ApplicationRef.isStable`/`whenStable()` and SSR stability after HTTP responses, and update any tests that asserted stability via `await Promise.resolve()` to await `appRef.whenStable()` instead.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// @angular/common/http internal interceptor.ts (old → new)
-      const taskId = pendingTasks.add();
-      return chain(req, handler).pipe(finalize(() => pendingTasks.remove(taskId)));
+      const removeTask = pendingTasks.add();
+      return chain(req, handler).pipe(finalize(removeTask));

// test: stability after an HttpClient request completes
-    await Promise.resolve();
-    expect(stable).toBe(true);
+    await expectAsync(appRef.whenStable()).toBeResolved();
```

**What changed:** All public entries of `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` — `platformBrowserDynamic`, `JitCompilerFactory`, `platformBrowserDynamicTesting`, `VERSION` and related types — are deprecated in Angular 20. The replacement is the `@angular/platform-browser` package (`platformBrowser`, `BrowserTestingModule`, `platformBrowserTesting`), and JIT apps/tests additionally require `import '@angular/compiler'`. JIT compilation itself remains supported; only the dedicated package is deprecated. There is no automatic schematic for this rename, so imports must be edited by hand (, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61043, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/bc2cab747f23e517128bdefedd7d5296a1c9ebbc).
**Affects:** Applications or tests that import from `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` (e.g. `platformBrowserDynamic`, `platformBrowserDynamicTesting`) or that list `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` as a dependency.
**Action:** required: replace the `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` import with `@angular/platform-browser` (`platformBrowserDynamic` → `platformBrowser`, `platformBrowserDynamicTesting` → `platformBrowserTesting` from `@angular/platform-browser/testing`), add `import '@angular/compiler'` to the entry point or `test.ts` when JIT is used, and remove the `@angular/platform-browser-dynamic` dependency once no imports remain; re-verify that JIT compilation still works as before.
**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (v19)
import { platformBrowserDynamic } from '@angular/platform-browser-dynamic';
platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule(AppModule);

// after (v20)
import '@angular/compiler';
import { platformBrowser } from '@angular/platform-browser';
platformBrowser().bootstrapApplication(AppComponent);
```

Based on the provided sources, the HammerJS integration in Angular 19/20 is deprecated but not yet removed (removal is planned for a future major version). The sources confirm the deprecation of `HammerModule`, `HammerGestureConfig`, and related APIs. No code changes are strictly required in version 20.0.0, but users should plan for removal.

**What changed:** The HammerJS integration provided by the framework is deprecated. Specifically, `HammerModule`, `HammerGestureConfig`, `HAMMER_GESTURE_CONFIG`, and `HAMMER_LOADER` from `@angular/platform-browser` are marked as deprecated and will be removed in a future major version. The Angular team recommends replacing them with a custom implementation or native browser Pointer Events API. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a980ac9a6a9b47246db8690e7779e563bf231a90)
**Affects:** Applications that import `HammerModule` from `@angular/platform-browser`, use `HammerGestureConfig`, provide `HAMMER_GESTURE_CONFIG`, or rely on Angular's built-in HammerJS gesture support (e.g., `(tap)`, `(pan)` directives).
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (and still valid in v20, but deprecated)
import { HammerModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';

@NgModule({
  imports: [HammerModule],
  // ...
})
export class AppModule {}
```

**What changed:** Angular 20 adds a public `abort()` method to the router's `Navigation` interface (`readonly abort: () => void`), so an in-flight navigation can be cancelled directly via `Router.getCurrentNavigation()?.abort()`. Calling it triggers a `NavigationCancel` with the new `NavigationCancellationCode.Aborted` enum member (value 4), resolves the navigation promise to `false`, and clears `getCurrentNavigation()`. It is a no-op once the navigation completes or reaches the activation stage (no errors, no extra events). Internally the router now tracks aborts with an `AbortController` per transition, `transitionAbortSubject` was renamed to `transitionAbortWithErrorSubject` (internal), and `HistoryStateManager` history restoration on `NavigationCancel` changed to restore unless the code is `SupersededByNewNavigation` or `Redirect`, so aborted navigations now restore browser history (previously only `GuardRejected`/`NoDataFromResolver` did) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0bb4bd661e8fafe3228692181397272898fb9e9a, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60380).

**Affects:** Applications that inspect `NavigationCancellationCode` values or handle `NavigationCancel` events (the new `Aborted` member is only produced by explicitly calling `abort()`); otherwise none — this is purely additive new API.

**Action:** none (new capability; adopt optionally to cancel in-flight navigations, e.g. to shim the browser navigation API).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// New in Angular 20 public API (goldens/public-api/router/index.api.md)
export interface Navigation {
    readonly abort: () => void;
    ...
}

export enum NavigationCancellationCode {
    Aborted = 4,
    GuardRejected = 3,
    ...
}

// Usage from the new tests:
const navigationPromise = router.navigateByUrl('a');
router.getCurrentNavigation()!.abort();
// -> getCurrentNavigation() is null, promise resolves to false, NavigationCancel emitted
```

**What changed:** The `redirectTo` property on router routes can now accept a `RedirectFunction` that returns an `Observable` or `Promise`, allowing redirects to be resolved dynamically (e.g., via API or async function). Previously, `redirectTo` could only be a string or a synchronous function returning `string | UrlTree`. The commit states: "The `RedirectFn` can now return `Observable` or `Promise`. Any code that directly calls functions returning this type may need to be adjusted to account for this" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/62de7d930a5d2f3cc39b6bf38dedbe3e9d938842). The new type alias `RedirectFunction` is documented in the `@angular/router` API reference for v20 (https://v20.angular.dev/api/router/RedirectFunction). The official guide now notes: "It typically returns a string or `URLTree`, but it can also return an observable or promise" (https://angular.dev/guide/routing/redirecting-routes).

**Affects:** Applications that use a function for the `redirectTo` property in route configurations, or that directly invoke functions typed as `RedirectFn`/`RedirectFunction`.

**Action:** required: re-verify any code that directly calls `redirectTo` function implementations to handle the possibility that they return `Observable` or `Promise` rather than `string | UrlTree` synchronously.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 19): synchronous redirect function
{ path: 'old', redirectTo: (route) => route.queryParams['id'] ? 'new' : 'home' }

// After (Angular 20): asynchronous redirect function
{ path: 'old', redirectTo: () => inject(AuthService).isAuthorized$.pipe(
    map(isAuth => isAuth ? 'home' : 'login')
  )
}
```

**What changed:** In `runResolve`, the router now sets `futureARS.data` (from `getInherited(...)resolve`) *before* executing the node's resolvers, then merges each resolver's result into `futureARS.data` (`{...futureARS.data, ...resolvedData}`). Previously, `futureARS.data` was set only *after* all resolvers completed, so a child `ResolveFn` could not see its parent's resolved data (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7c12cb1df980734c64a4d127c2b9a7094e0fe9fb.patch). Resolvers execute sequentially from parent to child, so this is a new capability for child resolvers (https://angular.dev/guide/routing/data-resolvers).

**Affects:** Applications with nested routes where a child route's `resolve` callback reads `ActivatedRouteSnapshot.data` of a parent's resolved key.

**Action:** none. This is additive and non-breaking; no code or config change is required. Child resolvers can now read parent data via `route.data['<key>']` instead of the `route.parent.data` workaround mentioned in the linked issue.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New capability: child resolver reads parent's resolved data
{
  path: 'a',
  resolve: { aResolve: () => 'a' },
  children: [{
    path: 'b',
    resolve: {
      bResolve: (route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot) => route.data['aResolve'] + 'b',
    },
    children: [{path: 'c', component: Empty}],
  }],
}
```

**What changed:** Angular now catches rejections from router navigation promises triggered *internally* by the framework and routes them to `ɵINTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER`, instead of leaving an unhandled ("dangling") promise rejection. The internal callers `RouterLink` (in `router_link.ts`, via `this.router.navigateByUrl(urlTree, extras)`) and `Router.attemptNavigation` (in `router.ts`, via `this.scheduleNavigation(urlTree, source, restoredState, extras)`) now attach a `.catch((e) => { this.applicationErrorHandler(e); })` / `this.injector.get(ɵINTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER)(e)` handler (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/219f41d049cf6798f81fbb393b4e23b2a030ff48.patch). This is a preventive fix: while the long‑term plan is to resolve failed navigations with `false`, rejections still happen under the current (opt‑in) behavior when a navigation error occurs.

**Affects:** Applications using the `Router` (`navigateByUrl` / `navigate`) and `RouterLink`/`RouterLinkWithHref` directives where internal (framework‑triggered) navigations reject the navigation promise — i.e. the prior behavior under which the promise was rejected on navigation errors rather than resolved with `false`.

**Action:** none — this is an internal framework fix that prevents unhandled promise rejections from router‑triggered navigations; application code needs no changes. Re‑verify that navigation‑error handling in apps relying on promise rejections still routes errors as expected.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (router.ts, attemptNavigation)
this.scheduleNavigation(urlTree, source, restoredState, extras);

// After (router.ts, attemptNavigation)
this.scheduleNavigation(urlTree, source, restoredState, extras).catch((e) => {
  this.injector.get(ɵINTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER)(e);
});
```

**What changed:** The router's public API no longer requires writable (`any[]`) arrays for command inputs; it now accepts `readonly` arrays because the router never mutates them. `Router.createUrlTree` now takes `commands: readonly any[]`, `Router.navigate` takes `commands: readonly any[]`, the standalone `createUrlTreeFromSnapshot` takes `commands: readonly any[]`, and the `RouterLink` directive input `routerLink` now accepts `readonly any[] | string | UrlTree | null | undefined` (previously `any[]`). (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2419060fef4e59a5633c29bfd5d55e2d5a17dd00) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2419060fef4e59a5633c29bfd5d55e2d5a17dd00.patch) The PR notes this "should not cause any breaking change" for code passing mutable arrays, since a mutable array is assignable to a readonly array. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60345)

**Affects:** Applications that call `Router.createUrlTree`, `Router.navigate`, or `createUrlTreeFromSnapshot`, or bind `routerLink`, passing an array-typed variable (especially `readonly`/frozen/`ReadonlyArray`/`as const` arrays) whose type they declared as a writable `any[]`, or that wrap these APIs with their own `commands: any[]`-typed parameters.

**Action:** required: re-verify code that implements wrappers around `Router.navigate`, `Router.createUrlTree` or the `routerLink` input. Since the parameters widened from `any[]` to `readonly any[]`, existing mutable-array callers remain compatible; only call sites that themselves require a writable array for the commands (e.g. mutating them after passing in) are unaffected and should be re-verified. No property keys or dependency coordinates change.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Router.createUrlTree / Router.navigate / createUrlTreeFromSnapshot
// before:
createUrlTree(commands: any[], navigationExtras?: UrlCreationOptions): UrlTree;
navigate(commands: any[], extras?: NavigationExtras): Promise<boolean>;
export function createUrlTreeFromSnapshot(relativeTo: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, commands: any[], ...): UrlTree;
// after:
createUrlTree(commands: readonly any[], navigationExtras?: UrlCreationOptions): UrlTree;
navigate(commands: readonly any[], extras?: NavigationExtras): Promise<boolean>;
export function createUrlTreeFromSnapshot(relativeTo: ActivatedRouteSnapshot, commands: readonly any[], ...): UrlTree;

// RouterLink input
// before:
set routerLink(commandsOrUrlTree: any[] | string | UrlTree | null | undefined)
// after:
set routerLink(commandsOrUrlTree: readonly any[] | string | UrlTree | null | undefined)
```

**What changed:** In v19 `@angular/router` declared `export type DeprecatedGuard = ProviderToken<any> | any;`, so the `any` member made the guard unions on `Route` (`canActivate`, `canActivateChild`, `canDeactivate`, `canLoad`, `canMatch` — all typed `Array<CanActivateFn | DeprecatedGuard>` etc.) accept anything, and prevented TypeScript from inferring parameter types of inline functional guards (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/19.0.x/goldens/public-api/router/index.api.md). Commit c57951d58f (PR #60378) narrowed it to `export type DeprecatedGuard = ProviderToken<any> | string;` — a union of functional-guard functions plus a type matching `Injector.get`: `ProviderToken<T>|string`; `string` remains deprecated on both route guards and `Injector.get` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60378). The same commit added a separate deprecated `export type DeprecatedResolve = DeprecatedGuard | any;` and changed `ResolveData` from `{[key: string | symbol]: ResolveFn<unknown> | DeprecatedGuard}` to `{[key: string | symbol]: ResolveFn<unknown> | DeprecatedResolve}`, so resolver values keep the old `any` escape hatch (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/20.0.x/goldens/public-api/router/index.api.md). Both `DeprecatedGuard` and `DeprecatedResolve` are marked `@public @deprecated`.

**Affects:** Applications that put non-function, non-`ProviderToken` values (anything previously allowed only via `any`, e.g. mis-typed or loosely typed variables) into `Route.canActivate`, `canActivateChild`, `canDeactivate`, `canLoad` or `canMatch`, and code that explicitly annotates those arrays or imports `DeprecatedGuard`. Guard arrays whose entries are functional guards (`CanActivateFn`, `CanMatchFn`, …) or `ProviderToken`s still type-check, and now infer their `ActivatedRouteSnapshot`/`RouterStateSnapshot` parameters.

**Action:** required: re-verify that route config compiles — every entry in `canActivate`/`canActivateChild`/`canDeactivate`/`canLoad`/`canMatch` must now be a matching functional guard or a `ProviderToken<any> | string`; replace values that only type-checked because of `any` with functional guards (per the deprecation docs, an injectable class can be used as a functional guard using `inject`: `canActivate: [() => inject(myGuard).canActivate()]`). Prefer migrating deprecated `string` guard entries to `ProviderToken`s or functions (strings are still accepted but deprecated). If an app intentionally relied on the old `any` behaviour for resolvers, note that `ResolveData` still permits it via `DeprecatedResolve`; `string` is scheduled for removal from `DeprecatedGuard`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Angular 19 (goldens/public-api/router/index.api.md)
// @public @deprecated
export type DeprecatedGuard = ProviderToken<any> | any;
export type ResolveData = {
    [key: string | symbol]: ResolveFn<unknown> | DeprecatedGuard;
};

// Angular 20
// @public @deprecated
export type DeprecatedGuard = ProviderToken<any> | string;
// @public @deprecated
export type DeprecatedResolve = DeprecatedGuard | any;
export type ResolveData = {
    [key: string | symbol]: ResolveFn<unknown> | DeprecatedResolve;
};
// Route guard arrays are unchanged in shape but the `any` member is gone:
canActivate?: Array<CanActivateFn | DeprecatedGuard>;
canMatch?: Array<CanMatchFn | DeprecatedGuard>;
```

**What changed:** In v20 the router's internal `RouterScroller` (packages/router/src/router_scroller.ts) no longer schedules the `Scroll` transition event with a plain `setTimeout(..., 0)` after `NavigationEnd`/`NavigationSkipped`. It now awaits a Promise that races `setTimeout(resolve)` against `requestAnimationFrame(resolve)` (when `requestAnimationFrame` is defined), still inside `this.zone.runOutsideAngular(...)`, before emitting `new Scroll(routerEvent, ..., anchor)` via `this.transitions.events.next(...)` inside `this.zone.run(...)`. Scrolling therefore happens as soon as change detection completes, before the browser paints, fixing visible flicker during scroll restoration (fixes #53985; PR #60086, commit db2f2d99c8) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/db2f2d99c82aae52d8a0ae46616c6411d070b35e, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/60086). Still at least one macrotask is awaited, per #57109 (patch in commit page). This is an internal implementation fix; no public API, class, or option changed.

**Affects:** Applications using `withInMemoryScroll({ scrollPositionRestoration: 'enabled' | 'top' })` (i.e., the router scroller), especially large/complex apps where the old `setTimeout`-delayed scroll painted in the next frame; also apps that subclass or observe the `Scroll` router event / custom `Scroller` strategies may see the event fire slightly earlier (after a rAF or a timeout, whichever comes first).

**Action:** none — no code, configuration, or dependency change is required; apps relying on scroll-restoration timing should simply re-verify their scroll behavior after upgrade.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 19) — packages/router/src/router_scroller.ts
this.zone.runOutsideAngular(() => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    this.zone.run(() => {
      this.transitions.events.next(new Scroll(routerEvent, /* position/anchor */));
    });
  }, 0);
});

// After (Angular 20)
this.zone.runOutsideAngular(async () => {
  await new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(resolve);
    if (typeof requestAnimationFrame !== 'undefined') {
      requestAnimationFrame(resolve);
    }
  });
  this.zone.run(() => {
    this.transitions.events.next(new Scroll(routerEvent, /* position/anchor */));
  });
});
```
